window.MS_SEARCH = [{"t":"Morningstar documentation","h":"","u":"index.html","s":"Guide","x":"Morningstar documentationMorningstar is a Rust framework for building Android and iOS apps from one codebase. You describe each screen as a function of your app's state; Morningstar lays it out and draws it itself on the GPU. There is no web view, and none of the platform's own widgets.src/lib.rs That is a whole application. view says what the screen looks like for the current state, a tap delivers a Message, and upd"},{"t":"Morningstar documentation","h":"Start here","u":"index.html#start-here","s":"Guide","x":"New to the framework? These four pages take you from an empty folder to an app running on your phone.InstallationWhat a machine needs to build an app, test it and put it on an Android phone.Your first appOne command writes an app that builds and passes its tests. Then change it.The application modelState, messages, view, update, and how work that takes time comes back as a message.Widget galleryEvery widget with its "},{"t":"Morningstar documentation","h":"What apps built with it look like","u":"index.html#what-apps-built-with-it-look-like","s":"Guide","x":"Every picture in these pages is one frame through the real renderer, produced with no phone by the app's own screenshot program. Four of the example apps follow a design, and each is held to its design's pictures by tests.FundiDesk: the dashboardCallReady: the home screenAtlas: the chatBarua Talk: a video callThe examples say what each app shows and how to run it.Where the project stands. Android works: apps have bee"},{"t":"Morningstar documentation","h":"Supported platforms","u":"index.html#supported-platforms","s":"Guide","x":"PlatformStateAndroidBuilds for API 24 (Android 7.0) and later. Run on one phone so far: a Galaxy A27 on Android 16iOSShared code type-checks for iOS; the Swift host has never been built or runDevelopment machineA Mac: the tests draw on its GPU, and the live preview opens a window on itRustStable, 1.87 or newer"},{"t":"Morningstar documentation","h":"Getting help","u":"index.html#getting-help","s":"Guide","x":"The repository is the reference. AGENTS.md at its root holds the rules every change follows, each subsystem's decision is recorded under decision records, and troubleshooting collects the problems that have actually come up."},{"t":"What Morningstar is, and is not","h":"","u":"guide/what-it-is.html","s":"Guide","x":"What Morningstar is, and is notMorningstar is a mobile application framework written in Rust. An app is one Rust crate, shared by Android and iOS. The framework owns everything between your state and the pixels: the widgets, the layout engine, text shaping, and a renderer that draws through the GPU."},{"t":"What Morningstar is, and is not","h":"What it is","u":"guide/what-it-is.html#what-it-is","s":"Guide","x":"One codebase in Rust. State, logic and screens are written once. The Android and iOS projects are thin hosts that an app developer never edits.Its own UI engine. A screen is a tree of Morningstar's widgets, laid out by Morningstar's layout engine. Nothing is translated into the platform's views, so a screen looks the same on both platforms and in a test.Drawn on the GPU. Rectangles, borders, gradients, shadows, text,"},{"t":"What Morningstar is, and is not","h":"What it is not","u":"guide/what-it-is.html#what-it-is-not","s":"Guide","x":"Not a wrapper around native widgets. There is no UIButton or Android Button underneath. Platform look-and-feel is whatever your screens draw.Not a web view, and there is no JavaScript anywhere in an app.Not on iOS yet. The shared code type-checks for iOS and the Swift host is written, but it has never been compiled. See iOS.Not a desktop or web framework. The preview runs an app in a window on a Mac for development o"},{"t":"What Morningstar is, and is not","h":"Who decides what","u":"guide/what-it-is.html#who-decides-what","s":"Guide","x":"The rules of the project are in AGENTS.md at the root of the repository: Rust first, an independently owned UI engine, GPU rendering as the foundation, one shared app codebase, platform code behind stable interfaces, performance that is measured before it is claimed. Each decision that followed from them has a record under decision records."},{"t":"How rendering works","h":"","u":"guide/rendering.html","s":"Guide","x":"How rendering worksA frame starts as your state and ends as pixels. This page follows it through the crates in between. You do not need any of it to write an app; it explains why the rules elsewhere in these pages are what they are."},{"t":"How rendering works","h":"From state to pixels","u":"guide/rendering.html#from-state-to-pixels","s":"Guide","x":"view builds a tree. The framework calls your view and gets an Element tree: boxes, texts, pictures. The tree is thrown away after the frame. The few pieces of widget state that outlive it (a scroll position, the focused field, a press in progress, an animation on its way) are kept by identity; see the application model.Layout turns the tree into rectangles. The layout engine (morningstar-layout) knows three kinds of "},{"t":"How rendering works","h":"When a frame is drawn","u":"guide/rendering.html#when-a-frame-is-drawn","s":"Guide","x":"Rendering is demand-driven. A frame is produced after input, when an answer to an effect arrives, while something animates, and when a timer the app asked for fires. Otherwise nothing is drawn: on the phone the render thread is blocked and the app uses no CPU or GPU. The first device session recorded exactly one frame in six idle seconds.On Android a dedicated render thread paces frames to the display's vsync and sto"},{"t":"How rendering works","h":"Colour","u":"guide/rendering.html#colour","s":"Guide","x":"Colours are composited as sRGB-encoded values, the way the web platform does it, so a translucent layer over a background gives the colour a browser or a design tool shows. Give colours as the design's hex values through hex(0xRRGGBB). ADR 0023."},{"t":"How rendering works","h":"The hosts","u":"guide/rendering.html#the-hosts","s":"Guide","x":"The Android project (Java, with Kotlin for live rooms) and the iOS project (Swift) contain no application logic. They create the surface, forward touches and the keyboard, and carry out platform services such as HTTP requests and recording. The app crate is the native library the host loads; morningstar::app! registers it. ADR 0003, ADR 0005."},{"t":"How rendering works","h":"What a frame costs","u":"guide/rendering.html#what-a-frame-costs","s":"Guide","x":"Only what has been measured is stated in Performance: a baseline on the development machine, and one early session on a phone."},{"t":"Installation","h":"","u":"guide/installation.html","s":"Guide","x":"InstallationMorningstar is not installed as a package. You work in a clone of its repository, and an app is a crate in that workspace. This page lists what the machine needs."},{"t":"Installation","h":"Before you begin","u":"guide/installation.html#before-you-begin","s":"Guide","x":"You only need the tools for what you plan to do.ToolVersionNeeded forRustStable, 1.87 or newer. CI uses 1.92.0Every appA MacAny with a GPU that Metal runs onRunning the tests that draw, screenshots and the live previewAndroid SDK and NDKNDK 27.1 is the one in useAndroid buildscargo-ndkCI uses 4.1.2Android buildsJDK17 or newerAndroid buildsNode20 or newer for a generated server; 22 or newer for the example apps' serve"},{"t":"Installation","h":"Set up","u":"guide/installation.html#set-up","s":"Guide","x":"Get the repository and check Rust.Clone the repository, then confirm the toolchain from its root: Add the Android tools.Install the Android SDK and an NDK (for example with Android Studio's SDK manager), then: tools/build-android.sh finds the newest complete NDK under $ANDROID_HOME/ndk by itself. Set ANDROID_NDK_HOME to choose one.Create and run an app. Your first app continues from here, up to installing it on a pho"},{"t":"Installation","h":"Disk space","u":"guide/installation.html#disk-space","s":"Guide","x":"A full debug build of the workspace with every example takes several gigabytes under target/. Build one package at a time (-p my-app) and build Android for one architecture (MORNINGSTAR_ABIS=arm64-v8a) to keep it small. Troubleshooting says what is safe to delete."},{"t":"Getting started: from nothing to an app on a phone","h":"","u":"getting-started.html","s":"Guide","x":"Getting started: from nothing to an app on a phoneFor a developer or an AI agent starting a new Morningstar app. It takes one command to have an app that builds, passes its tests and can be installed; the rest of this page is what to do with it. The decision behind the command is ADR 0041.This page works inside the Morningstar repository, and everything is run from the repository root. For an app anywhere else, see O"},{"t":"Getting started: from nothing to an app on a phone","h":"What you need","u":"getting-started.html#what-you-need","s":"Guide","x":"ForYou needWriting and testing the appRust (stable; the version is in the root Cargo.toml) and a GPU the tests can draw on. A Mac has oneThe live previewA MacAn Android phoneThe Android SDK and NDK (ANDROID_HOME), cargo-ndk, a JDK 17 or newer, and for --install a phone with USB debugging onThe optional serverNode 20 or neweriOS is not ready: the code is shared, but the iOS host has never been built."},{"t":"Getting started: from nothing to an app on a phone","h":"1. Create the app","u":"getting-started.html#1-create-the-app","s":"Guide","x":"ArgumentMeansIf left outmy-appThe Cargo package and the app's directory: lower-case letters, digits, hyphensRequired--nameThe name under the launcher iconThe package in words: \"My App\"--idThe application id. It cannot be changed once people have the appdev.morningstar.my_app--services live,live-video,live-screen,live-backgroundPlatform services the app's host is built with (voice rooms, the camera in them, sharing th"},{"t":"Getting started: from nothing to an app on a phone","h":"2. Run its tests","u":"getting-started.html#2-run-its-tests","s":"Guide","x":"They pass. The first run also writes apps/my-app/tests/references/home.png and settings.png: pictures of the two screens, which later runs compare with. Look at them and commit them.What the tests show is how every later test is written. tests/e2e.rs taps, types, and reads the app's state; nothing in it needs a phone, because the app asks for work as data and the test answers (Ui::testing)."},{"t":"Getting started: from nothing to an app on a phone","h":"3. Look at it","u":"getting-started.html#3-look-at-it","s":"Guide","x":"The screenshot is one frame through the real renderer. The preview is the app running, with the mouse as a finger (ADR 0040). Use both: after changing anything visual, look at it. Passing tests do not show that a screen looks right."},{"t":"Getting started: from nothing to an app on a phone","h":"4. Change it","u":"getting-started.html#4-change-it","s":"Guide","x":"Open apps/my-app/src/lib.rs. The loop is always the same:State: add a field to Model.Message: add what can happen to Message.view: draw the state. It changes nothing.update: change the state for the message. It draws nothing and does no I/O.effects: for work that takes time (a request, a stored value, sound, a live room), say what to start; the answer arrives as a message.Test: add the journey to tests/e2e.rs, then l"},{"t":"Getting started: from nothing to an app on a phone","h":"5. Follow a design","u":"getting-started.html#5-follow-a-design","s":"Guide","x":"Put the design's pictures in apps/my-app/design/reference/ and hold the screens to them; apps/my-app/design/README.md has the commands, docs/design-to-code.md the method, and examples/fundidesk (twelve screens) and examples/barua-talk (five) show it carried through with a measured difference per screen."},{"t":"Getting started: from nothing to an app on a phone","h":"6. Put it on a phone","u":"getting-started.html#6-put-it-on-a-phone","s":"Guide","x":"Connect the phone, then: That builds the app's library for the phone, generates the launcher icons from assets/icon.png, packages the APK and installs and starts it. Without --install it only builds; the APK's path is printed. MORNINGSTAR_ABIS=arm64-v8a builds for phones only, which halves the time and the disk space.The icon is a placeholder in the app's colour. Replace assets/icon.png with a square PNG of 1024 x 10"},{"t":"Getting started: from nothing to an app on a phone","h":"7. If the app has a server","u":"getting-started.html#7-if-the-app-has-a-server","s":"Guide","x":"In another terminal, let the phone reach it over USB (this is undone when the cable is unplugged): server/API.md is the contract between the app and the server: change it, server/src/app.js and src/lib.rs together. Secrets go in server/.env only, which git ignores; server/.env.example names the settings and holds no value. For a deployed server, give its address when building, so that it is never in the repository:"},{"t":"Getting started: from nothing to an app on a phone","h":"8. Before calling it done","u":"getting-started.html#8-before-calling-it-done","s":"Guide","x":"AGENTS.md defines done. For an app that means: the tests pass, the screens have been looked at, SCREENS.md says what every control does and what is not done, and what has and has not been seen on a real phone is written down."},{"t":"Getting started: from nothing to an app on a phone","h":"Removing an app","u":"getting-started.html#removing-an-app","s":"Guide","x":"Delete its directory and its line under members in the root Cargo.toml."},{"t":"Getting started: from nothing to an app on a phone","h":"Outside this repository","u":"getting-started.html#outside-this-repository","s":"Guide","x":"Morningstar is released on crates.io (ADR 0045). The morningstar command does there what this repository's scripts do here: morningstar new takes the same options as morningstar-new, except --dir, which here names the parent directory (default: the current one). The app depends on one release, morningstar = { package = \"morningstar-framework\", version = \"=0.1.1\" }. The crate is morningstar-framework on crates.io beca"},{"t":"Project layout","h":"","u":"guide/project-layout.html","s":"Guide","x":"Project layoutTwo layouts matter: an app's own directory, and the repository around it."},{"t":"Project layout","h":"An app","u":"guide/project-layout.html#an-app","s":"Guide","x":"This is what morningstar-new writes. The example apps have the same shape.PathWhat it isCargo.tomlThe crate, and under [package.metadata.morningstar] the app's name, id, icon and opt-in services. They are declared here and nowhere elsesrc/lib.rsThe application: state, messages, view, update, effects, and the morningstar::app! linetests/e2e.rsJourneys through the app: taps, typing and answers, with no devicetests/acce"},{"t":"Project layout","h":"The repository","u":"guide/project-layout.html#the-repository","s":"Guide","x":"PathOwnscrates/morningstarThe public API. The only crate an app depends oncrates/morningstar-uiWidgets, state and messages, themes, effects. Apps use it as morningstar::uicrates/morningstar-layoutThe layout enginecrates/morningstar-textFonts, shaping, the glyph atlas, vector icons, SVGcrates/morningstar-gpuThe renderer; the only crate that uses wgpucrates/morningstar-runtimeThe engine a host drives: lifecycle in, fra"},{"t":"The application model","h":"","u":"guide/application-model.html","s":"Guide","x":"The application modelA Morningstar app is state, messages, and three functions. view draws the state, update changes it, and effects asks the phone for work that takes time. Nothing else touches the state, and nothing in the app does I/O."},{"t":"The application model","h":"State, messages, view, update","u":"guide/application-model.html#state-messages-view-update","s":"Guide","x":"State is a struct that implements Application.view builds a tree of widgets from &self. It changes nothing. It is called again whenever a frame is needed, so it must be cheap and must not remember anything between calls.Messages are values of your own type. A tappable widget carries one; a text field makes one from each new value.update receives a message and changes the state. It returns at once: no sockets, files, "},{"t":"The application model","h":"Registering the app","u":"guide/application-model.html#registering-the-app","s":"Guide","x":"Ui runs an Application on the runtime. morningstar::app! makes it the app that the Android or iOS host starts. The crate is built as a native library; there is no main."},{"t":"The application model","h":"Everything an application can implement","u":"guide/application-model.html#everything-an-application-can-implement","s":"Guide","x":"Only update is required. Each other method has a default.MethodCalledDefaultsetup(&mut self, setup)Once, before the first frame. Add fonts and images, read the install id and stored valuesNothingtheme(&self) -> ThemeEvery frameTheme::LIGHTview(&self) -> Element<Message>Every frame that is drawnAn empty boxview_in(&self, viewport) -> Element<Message>Instead of view, for a layout that depends on the screenCalls viewupd"},{"t":"The application model","h":"Work that takes time","u":"guide/application-model.html#work-that-takes-time","s":"Guide","x":"update cannot wait for a server. It records what should start, effects starts it, and the answer arrives later as a message. The host does the work off the render thread. Because a request is data and its answer is a message, a test can play the phone and the server. Effects and services covers every service; Testing shows the other side."},{"t":"The application model","h":"Identity","u":"guide/application-model.html#identity","s":"Guide","x":"The tree returned by view is thrown away after each frame. Four pieces of widget state outlive it, kept by the framework:which pressable a finger is holding,each scroll view's position and motion,which text input has focus, with its caret,what an animated widget is on its way from.Each belongs to a widget's identity. By default that is its position in the tree: the chain of child indices from the root. .id(value) rep"},{"t":"The application model","h":"Units","u":"guide/application-model.html#units","s":"Guide","x":"Every length is in dp: density-independent units, which are dp on Android and points on iOS. Font sizes too. The framework converts to physical pixels with the display's scale and snaps edges to whole pixels."},{"t":"The application model","h":"Touch","u":"guide/application-model.html#touch","s":"Guide","x":"A tap is a finger going down and coming up on the same pressable. Sliding off before lifting cancels it. Inside a scroll view, moving more than 8 dp turns the gesture into a scroll. One finger is followed at a time. After a message has been delivered, touches are ignored until the next frame is laid out, so a stale screen cannot be tapped."},{"t":"The application model","h":"Next","u":"guide/application-model.html#next","s":"Guide","x":"Layout: how sizes and positions are decided.Navigation and time: several screens, Back, and timers.Building screens: the long worked guide, from a picture to code."},{"t":"Layout","h":"","u":"guide/layout.html","s":"Guide","x":"LayoutLayout uses the words a design tool uses. A box is a column, a row or a stack. Its size on each axis is fixed, hugs its contents, or fills its parent. There is padding inside a box and a gap between its children."},{"t":"Layout","h":"Fixed, hug and fill","u":"guide/layout.html#fixed-hug-and-fill","s":"Guide","x":"SizeMethodMeaningFixed.width(dp), .height(dp), .size(w, h)Exactly this many dpHug.hug_width(), .hug_height()As big as the contents. The defaultFill.fill_width(), .fill_height(), .fill()The space the parent has leftFill by weight.fill_width_weight(n), .fill_height_weight(n)A share of that space, against the other fills.min_width, .max_width, .min_height and .max_height bound any of them, and .aspect_ratio(w / h) gives"},{"t":"Layout","h":"Rows and columns","u":"guide/layout.html#rows-and-columns","s":"Guide","x":"col![..] and row![..] are short for Column::new().push(..) and Row::new().push(..). Both are a Container, so both take padding, a gap, alignment and paint..gap(dp) is the space between neighbours..main_align(..) distributes children along the box's own axis; .space_between() is the common case..cross_align(..) places each child across the axis; a child overrides it with .align_self(..)..center() centres on both axes."},{"t":"Layout","h":"Stacks","u":"guide/layout.html#stacks","s":"Guide","x":"A stack lays its children on top of each other, the later over the earlier. A child is pinned with .place(..) to one of nine anchors and nudged with .offset(x, y)."},{"t":"Layout","h":"Rows that wrap","u":"guide/layout.html#rows-that-wrap","s":"Guide","x":"A row wraps only if told to. Then a child that does not fit starts the next line, and the row is as tall as its lines."},{"t":"Layout","h":"Scrolling","u":"guide/layout.html#scrolling","s":"Guide","x":"A ScrollView fills its parent, so it needs a height from above. Its position belongs to the widget's identity: while a scroll view with the same id is in consecutive frames, its position is kept. ScrollView::horizontal(..) scrolls sideways. To move one from code, see ScrollTo."},{"t":"Layout","h":"The safe area and the keyboard","u":"guide/layout.html#the-safe-area-and-the-keyboard","s":"Guide","x":"SafeArea::new(child) insets its child by the status bar, the gesture bar and any display cutout. Container::safe_area(edges) does the same for chosen edges, for a bar that should reach the screen's edge with its contents kept clear.While the keyboard is up, boxes that pad for the bottom safe area pad for the keyboard instead, and the focused field is scrolled into view."},{"t":"Layout","h":"A layout that depends on the screen","u":"guide/layout.html#a-layout-that-depends-on-the-screen","s":"Guide","x":"Implement view_in instead of view to be told what you are drawn on. The Viewport gives the size, the insets and the keyboard's height in dp, and measures text exactly as layout will."},{"t":"Layout","h":"Rules worth knowing","u":"guide/layout.html#rules-worth-knowing","s":"Guide","x":"Sizes hug by default. A box is as big as its children; a text as big as its words.Fill inside hug collapses. A fill_width() child of a box that hugs its width gets no width. Give the parent a width.In a row, the text that should wrap must fill_width(). Two long hugging texts in one row overflow.Children start at the top-left unless the parent says otherwise.There is no right-to-left mode. Start is left.Ui::overflows("},{"t":"Text, fonts and pictures","h":"","u":"guide/text-and-pictures.html","s":"Guide","x":"Text, fonts and pictures"},{"t":"Text, fonts and pictures","h":"Text","u":"guide/text-and-pictures.html#text","s":"Guide","x":"A text takes a step of the theme's type scale, and any property can be overridden. RoleDefault sizeWeightUsed fordisplay34 dpBoldThe one big number or word on a screentitle24 dpBoldA screen titleheadline17 dpSemiboldA section or card headingbody15 dpRegularRunning text. The defaultlabel14 dpSemiboldButtons, tabs, field labelscaption12 dpRegularMetadata, timestamps, hints Text wraps at the width of its box, so a text "},{"t":"Text, fonts and pictures","h":"Fonts","u":"guide/text-and-pictures.html#fonts","s":"Guide","x":"An app bundles its fonts and adds them at setup. Nothing is drawn from a system font unless the app asks for the system's fonts. A family is chosen per text with .family(\"Space Grotesk\"), or for the whole app in the theme: Fonts::new(body, heading) sets the family for body roles and for heading roles. If the family has no italic file, italic is synthesised by slanting."},{"t":"Text, fonts and pictures","h":"Icons","u":"guide/text-and-pictures.html#icons","s":"Guide","x":"An icon is one-colour vector artwork that takes its colour where it is drawn. For icons kept as files, load them as SVG (below) and draw them with Icon::svg(asset.get()). A file that uses a single ink is tinted like any other icon."},{"t":"Text, fonts and pictures","h":"SVG files","u":"guide/text-and-pictures.html#svg-files","s":"Guide","x":"SVG is loaded at run time by a strict reader written for the purpose. It supports a static subset: shapes, groups, transforms, clips, gradients, dashes and currentColor. Anything else is an error that names the line, so a file either draws as designed or is refused. Check a file before using it: morningstar-svg art.svg out.png --scale 3 renders it exactly as an app would. See Tools and ADR 0027."},{"t":"Text, fonts and pictures","h":"Images","u":"guide/text-and-pictures.html#images","s":"Guide","x":"Images come from PNG data or raw pixels, added once at setup. A Picture draws one with a fit, a corner radius and an optional tint; an Avatar crops one to a circle and falls back to initials. Only PNG is decoded. The Barua Talk example keeps its photographs as JPEG for the design and converts them to PNG with a small script, examples/barua-talk/design/prepare-images.py.Each image is its own texture and splits the fra"},{"t":"Text, fonts and pictures","h":"Turned text, outlines and shadows","u":"guide/text-and-pictures.html#turned-text-outlines-and-shadows","s":"Guide","x":"A widget can be drawn through a transform (.rotate(degrees), .transform(..)), and text under a transform is rasterised for it, so turned text stays sharp. Text::shadow draws a hard shadow and Text::outline a stroke around the letters. Building screens has worked examples of each."},{"t":"Themes and motion","h":"","u":"guide/theming-and-motion.html","s":"Guide","x":"Themes and motion"},{"t":"Themes and motion","h":"The theme","u":"guide/theming-and-motion.html#the-theme","s":"Guide","x":"A theme is plain data: colour roles, a type scale, spacing, radii and font families. Start from Theme::LIGHT or Theme::DARK and change what the design changes. Widgets take a colour either as a value (hex(0x2F6BFF)) or as a role (ThemeColor::Primary). Roles follow whatever theme theme() returns, so one view can serve a light and a dark theme.VariantMeaningBackgroundThe screen background.SurfaceCards, sheets, bars: su"},{"t":"Themes and motion","h":"The system's bars","u":"guide/theming-and-motion.html#the-systems-bars","s":"Guide","x":"The screen is drawn edge to edge, under the status bar and the navigation bar. Their icons are dark over a light theme and light over a dark one without the app saying anything. system_bars() overrides that for a screen whose edges are not the theme's background, as in the sample above. ADR 0026.This has been tested on the development machine only; the bars' colours have not yet been checked on a phone."},{"t":"Themes and motion","h":"Motion","u":"guide/theming-and-motion.html#motion","s":"Guide","x":"view always describes the screen as it should be. A widget shows a change gradually only if it asks to. You wantUseA change of opacity, transform, offset, solid background or fixed size to be seen happening.animate(duration, easing) on the widgetA widget to fade, slide or scale in when it appears and out when it goes.transition(Transition::fade()), ::slide(x, y), ::scale(from)Screens to slide in the direction of the "},{"t":"Themes and motion","h":"Reduce motion","u":"guide/theming-and-motion.html#reduce-motion","s":"Guide","x":"When the user has asked the system for less motion, every animation, transition and gliding scroll takes no time. The app does nothing for this. In a test, Ui::set_reduce_motion(true) does the same."},{"t":"Text input","h":"","u":"guide/text-input.html","s":"Guide","x":"Text inputTextInput is an editable text. The application owns the string: the widget shows the value it is given and delivers each new value as a message, which update stores."},{"t":"Text input","h":"A field","u":"guide/text-input.html#a-field","s":"Guide","x":"Like Text, an input has no frame of its own. The box around it is the field. .input_frame() makes a tap anywhere in the box focus the input..focused_border(..) and .focused_background(..) are the box's look while the input has focus..id(..) keeps focus with the field when the screen changes around it. An input that leaves the tree loses focus, and the keyboard goes away."},{"t":"Text input","h":"The keyboard","u":"guide/text-input.html#the-keyboard","s":"Guide","x":"MethodEffect.keyboard(KeyboardType::..)Which keyboard is shown: Text, Number, Decimal, Phone or Email.action(ImeAction::..)The action key: Done, Next, Go, Search or Send.on_submit(message)What the action key sends.capitalization(..)What the keyboard capitalises by itself: nothing, sentences, words or every character.no_autocorrect()Turns correction and suggestions off.secure(true)A password: characters are shown as b"},{"t":"Text input","h":"Selection and the clipboard","u":"guide/text-input.html#selection-and-the-clipboard","s":"Guide","x":"A field behaves as a native one does. A double tap or a long press selects a word and brings up two handles and a small menu (Cut, Copy, Paste, Select all), drawn by the framework. A hardware keyboard's arrows and shortcuts work, and the clipboard is the platform's. None of this reaches the application, which still sees only the new string. ADR 0025.The app can also use the clipboard itself, as an effect: out.set_cli"},{"t":"Text input","h":"What has been seen on a phone","u":"guide/text-input.html#what-has-been-seen-on-a-phone","s":"Guide","x":"Typing on Android has been verified on one phone, with the system keyboard: focus, the keyboard's type, Next and Done, and keyboard avoidance. Selection, the menu and the clipboard pass their tests on the development machine and have not yet been tried on a device."},{"t":"Text input","h":"More","u":"guide/text-input.html#more","s":"Guide","x":"Every method is under TextInput. Building screens has a complete form with validation."},{"t":"Navigation and time","h":"","u":"guide/navigation.html","s":"Guide","x":"Navigation and time"},{"t":"Navigation and time","h":"Screens","u":"guide/navigation.html#screens","s":"Guide","x":"A Navigator is the stack of screens as plain application state. Nothing about it is special to the framework: a screen is a value of your own type, and view draws whichever is on top. Three things in that sample are worth copying:.id(screen) on each screen's root gives every screen its own scroll positions and focus..transition(self.screens.transition()) slides screens in and out in the direction of the last change: "},{"t":"Navigation and time","h":"The system's Back","u":"guide/navigation.html#the-systems-back","s":"Guide","x":"back() is called for the Back button and the back gesture. Return true when the app dealt with it, and false to leave it to the system, which then leaves the app. Navigator::back() returns exactly that: it pops if there is something to pop.A screen with something of its own to close first (a sheet, a search field, a selection) checks that before the navigator. ADR 0018."},{"t":"Navigation and time","h":"Time","u":"guide/navigation.html#time","s":"Guide","x":"tick() asks for a message after a delay. It is asked again after each one, so it repeats while it returns Some and stops when it returns None. With no timer, an idle app draws nothing and wakes for nothing.Use it for a clock that is on screen, for polling a server while a screen is open, and for timeouts. Do not use it to animate: .animate and .transition do that without messages."},{"t":"Navigation and time","h":"Reference","u":"guide/navigation.html#reference","s":"Guide","x":"Every method of Navigator is in the reference."},{"t":"Effects and services","h":"","u":"guide/effects.html","s":"Guide","x":"Effects and servicesAn application never does I/O itself. Work that takes time is asked for from effects as data, the host carries it out with the platform's own stack, and the result comes back as a message. This is what lets a test drive a whole app with no device: the test answers in the host's place."},{"t":"Effects and services","h":"The pattern","u":"guide/effects.html#the-pattern","s":"Guide","x":"update decides what should start and records it. effects, which is called after every update, queues the requests. Each request names the message its answer becomes."},{"t":"Effects and services","h":"What there is","u":"guide/effects.html#what-there-is","s":"Guide","x":"ServiceAsk withAnswerHTTPout.http(request, on_result)Result<HttpResponse, HttpError>A value kept on the phoneout.store(key, value), out.remove(key)None. Read back at the next setup, or with out.load(key, on_loaded)The clipboardout.set_clipboard(text), out.clipboard(on_text)Option<String>Recordingout.start_recording(on_result), out.stop_recording(on_result)Result<(), AudioError>, then Result<Recording, AudioError>Play"},{"t":"Effects and services","h":"HTTP","u":"guide/effects.html#http","s":"Guide","x":"A request is built from a method and a URL, with headers, a body and a timeout. The response has a status, headers and a body, read as text with response.text().Requests run on the platform's own HTTP stack with the platform's certificates.Plain http:// is refused by the hosts except to the development machine: localhost, 127.0.0.1 and 10.0.2.2.A response larger than 10 MB is an error.VariantMeaningOfflineThe server "},{"t":"Effects and services","h":"Values kept on the phone","u":"guide/effects.html#values-kept-on-the-phone","s":"Guide","x":"out.store(key, value) keeps a string under a key in the app's private storage. Stored values are handed to setup before the first frame, with no request and no waiting, which is the usual way to read them: setup.install_id() is an id made once per installation. The examples send it to their servers as X-Device-Id to tell one phone from another without an account."},{"t":"Effects and services","h":"Recording and playback","u":"guide/effects.html#recording-and-playback","s":"Guide","x":"start_recording asks for the microphone the first time and answers when it is running, or with AudioError::PermissionDenied. A recording stops by itself after 60 seconds and when the app leaves the screen. stop_recording hands over what was recorded as a Recording, which becomes an AudioClip to send to a server or play back.VariantMeaningPermissionDeniedThe user has not allowed the microphone.BusyA recording is alrea"},{"t":"Effects and services","h":"Where services work","u":"guide/effects.html#where-services-work","s":"Guide","x":"ServiceAndroidiOSPreview on a MacA testHTTP, storage, clipboardYesWritten, never compiledYesAnswered by the testRecording and playbackYesWritten, never compiledAnswers UnsupportedAnswered by the testLive roomsYes, for apps that opt inAnswers UnsupportedAnswers UnsupportedEvents sent by the test\"Yes\" for Android means the code is there and the app builds with it. What has actually been observed on a phone is in Platfo"},{"t":"Live rooms","h":"","u":"guide/live-rooms.html","s":"Guide","x":"Live roomsA live room carries sound, pictures and small data messages between the people in it. On Android it is LiveKit's client, behind the same effects interface as every other service. The app's server hands out a ticket (an address and a token); the app joins with it.Live rooms are opt-in per app, because LiveKit and WebRTC add about 20 MB to an APK. An app that does not ask for them carries none of the code. Se"},{"t":"Live rooms","h":"Joining, and following the room","u":"guide/live-rooms.html#joining-and-following-the-room","s":"Guide","x":"A join is answered more than once: one message per event until a final one. Disconnected and Failed are final. VariantMeaningConnectedThe room is joined: sound from the others plays, and the microphone is heard by them unless the room was joined muted.ParticipantJoinedSomeone is in the room: they were there already, or have come in.ParticipantLeftSomeone left. Their identity.SpeakingWho is speaking now, loudest first"},{"t":"Live rooms","h":"Pictures","u":"guide/live-rooms.html#pictures","s":"Guide","x":"A camera or a shared screen arrives as a VideoStarted event with a VideoTrack. The app draws the track with LiveVideo; the frames themselves never pass through the application. LiveVideo draws nothing until a frame exists, so whatever is stacked underneath is the placeholder. Camera pictures need the live-video service; sharing this phone's screen needs live-screen."},{"t":"Live rooms","h":"Controls","u":"guide/live-rooms.html#controls","s":"Guide","x":"MethodEffectout.set_live_microphone(bool)Mute and unmuteout.set_live_speaker(bool)Loudspeaker on or offout.set_live_audio_route(route)Choose the earpiece, the loudspeaker, a wired headset or a Bluetooth one. Routes are reported by LiveEvent::AudioRoutesout.set_live_camera(bool), out.switch_live_camera()The camera, and front or backout.set_live_screen_share(bool)Share this phone's screen. Android asks the user firstou"},{"t":"Live rooms","h":"When a room ends","u":"guide/live-rooms.html#when-a-room-ends","s":"Guide","x":"A live room holds the microphone open, so nothing may leave one connected by accident.VariantMeaningLeftThe application left it.ReplacedThe application joined another room: there is one at a time.HiddenThe app left the screen.HungUpThe user ended it from the notification of a room that was going on in the background (ADR 0042).EndedThe other side ended it: the room was closed, this person was removed, or the same tic"},{"t":"Live rooms","h":"What has been verified","u":"guide/live-rooms.html#what-has-been-verified","s":"Guide","x":"Everything on this page passes its tests on the development machine, and the Android side compiles and packages. None of it has been confirmed on a phone yet: no sound has been heard and no picture seen through a real room. The iOS host answers every join with Unsupported.The decisions, with every case a room ends in, are ADR 0038, ADR 0039 and ADR 0042. The Barua Talk example is the app that uses all of it."},{"t":"Accessibility","h":"","u":"guide/accessibility.html","s":"Guide","x":"AccessibilityWhen TalkBack or VoiceOver is running, each frame is also described as a tree of semantic nodes derived from its widgets, and the host mirrors that tree to the platform. While no assistive technology asks for it, none of this is built."},{"t":"Accessibility","h":"What you get without doing anything","u":"guide/accessibility.html#what-you-get-without-doing-anything","s":"Guide","x":"A Text is text.A Button is a button with its label.A TextInput is an edit box with its placeholder as the hint. A secure field's value is bullets, never its characters.A ScrollView is a list.Any box with on_press is one stop that reads the texts inside it in order.Icons, pictures and shapes say nothing."},{"t":"Accessibility","h":"What only you know","u":"guide/accessibility.html#what-only-you-know","s":"Guide","x":"The defaults cannot know what an icon means. Every widget has modifiers to say it.ModifierUse it for.label(text)What a control is called. Required for a control with no text inside it, such as an icon button.hint(text)What the control is for, spoken after its label.value(text)What it shows now: a progress, a count.role(Role::..)What it is, when the default is wrong.heading()A heading, which a screen reader's user can"},{"t":"Accessibility","h":"Checking a screen","u":"guide/accessibility.html#checking-a-screen","s":"Guide","x":"A test turns the description on and asks what is wrong with it. VariantMeaningMissingLabelA control with nothing to call it: an icon-only button without a .label(..), a text field with neither label nor placeholder.SmallTargetA control smaller than 48 x 48 dp: hard to hit, and hard to find by touch.DuplicateLabelTwo controls beside each other with the same label: they cannot be told apart by ear.LowContrastText whose"},{"t":"Accessibility","h":"What has been verified","u":"guide/accessibility.html#what-has-been-verified","s":"Guide","x":"The tree, its defaults and the checks pass their tests on the development machine. The Android host mirrors the tree to TalkBack as a virtual view hierarchy; it has not yet been tried with TalkBack on a phone. The iOS side is written and has never been compiled.The design is ADR 0032; Building screens has examples of each modifier."},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"","u":"building-screens.html","s":"Guide","x":"Building screens: from a picture to codeFor a developer or an AI agent handed a picture of a mobile screen. The API is morningstar::ui; the reasoning behind it is in ADR 0013; the compare-and-fix loop is in design-to-code.md. examples/showcase/src/lib.rs is three complete screens to imitate."},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"The shape of every app","u":"building-screens.html#the-shape-of-every-app","s":"Guide","x":"Cargo.toml depends on morningstar only (morningstar-devtools as a dev-dependency for screenshots), with crate-type = [\"cdylib\", \"lib\"].All numbers are dp (the units of a 1x design: a 360 or 390 wide artboard), font sizes included. Without fonts registered in setup, text is invisible."},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"Reading a design","u":"building-screens.html#reading-a-design","s":"Guide","x":"Work top-down. Every region of the picture is one of these:In the designWriteThings stacked verticallycol![a, b, c]Things side by siderow![a, b, c]Things on top of each other (badge on avatar, text on art)stack![below, above]; pin a child with .place(Alignment::TOP_RIGHT), nudge with .offset(x, y)Space between children.gap(12.0) on the row or columnSpace inside a frame.padding(16.0), .padding_xy(h, v), .padding_ltrb("},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"A worked example","u":"building-screens.html#a-worked-example","s":"Guide","x":"The design: a white card with 20 dp corners. Inside, rows of a 40 dp round avatar, a name in semibold with a grey caption under it, and an amount on the right in green or black. Hairlines between rows start under the text.Read it as structure first: Then write exactly that: Small functions that each return one piece (Container<Message> or Element<Message>) keep view reading like the design's layer list. Use Column::n"},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"A form","u":"building-screens.html#a-form","s":"Guide","x":"A text field is two things: a TextInput, which is only the text (like Text), and the Container around it, which is the field's frame. The application owns the string. What to know:on_input gets the whole new string, after every change. Store it and pass it back as the value. To restrict or reformat what is typed, change it in update (keep only digits, upper-case it): the field then shows what you stored. For the comm"},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"Screens, Back, and time","u":"building-screens.html#screens-back-and-time","s":"Guide","x":"Two optional methods of Application (ADR 0018). Navigation is state: keep a stack of screens, push in update, and pop in back. Navigator<S> is that stack (ADR 0021): a Vec that is never empty with push, replace, pop, pop_to_root, reset and back() -> bool. back is also what an on-screen back arrow should do: give the arrow a message whose update arm calls the same self.screens.back(). A flow that should not be returne"},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"Fitting the screen: the viewport, and rows that wrap","u":"building-screens.html#fitting-the-screen-the-viewport-and-rows-that-wrap","s":"Guide","x":"All of this section and the next two is ADR 0021.A row of chips or tags wraps if you say so. CSS display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px is: A chip goes to the next line when it does not fit after the ones before it; a line is as tall as its tallest chip; the row is as tall as its lines, so what follows moves down and a ScrollView around it scrolls the right amount. A hugging chip is offered the whole line, so a ver"},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"Scrolling: positions, and asking for a move","u":"building-screens.html#scrolling-positions-and-asking-for-a-move","s":"Guide","x":"A ScrollView's position belongs to the widget and is kept for as long as a scroll view with the same identity is in consecutive frames. Give a scroll view whose content changes an .id(..) and keep it: content that grows leaves the reader where they are; content that shrinks under them brings them back to its end. A different id is a different scroll view, at its top.The application does not set a position; it asks fo"},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"Motion","u":"building-screens.html#motion","s":"Guide","x":"view always describes the screen as it should be. Motion is asked for per widget and happens by itself; frames are produced only while something is on its way, and the app is idle again afterwards.A change that should be seen happening: .animate(duration, easing) on the widget that changes, which needs an .id(..). What is animated is the widget's own .opacity(..), .transform(..) / .rotate(..), .offset(..), solid .bac"},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"Talking to a server, sound, and remembering things","u":"building-screens.html#talking-to-a-server-sound-and-remembering-things","s":"Guide","x":"update must return at once, and it cannot touch the device. Work that takes time is asked of the platform and comes back as a message (ADR 0019). The pattern has three steps: update notes in the state that the work is wanted; effects, which the runtime calls after setup and after every update, hands it over; the result arrives in update like a tap would.A complete voice note: hold nothing, tap to record, tap to send,"},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"A live room","u":"building-screens.html#a-live-room","s":"Guide","x":"A live room is a conversation on a media server (LiveKit): the people in it hear each other. It is for calls (ADR 0038). Your own server hands the app a ticket for one person and one room, an address (wss://...) and a token; the app joins with it: It is the one request that is answered more than once. Every event of the room is a message, in order. The last is always exactly one Disconnected or Failed, and nothing co"},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"Name and icon","u":"building-screens.html#name-and-icon","s":"Guide","x":"What the app is called on the home screen and what its icon is go in the app's Cargo.toml; tools/build-android.sh and tools/build-ios.sh generate the platform's icon files from it at every build (ADR 0020): With no icon, the app gets the built-in star. A misspelt key is an error, not a default.With only icon, Android's adaptive icon shows it as drawn, cut by the launcher's mask: a circle removes the corners. Keep wha"},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"The loop","u":"building-screens.html#the-loop","s":"Guide","x":"Add an example next to the app (copy examples/showcase/examples/screenshot.rs): Render: cargo run -p my-app --example screenshot out/ (seconds).Look at the PNG. Always. Passing tests do not show that a screen looks right.Compare with the design when there is one, rendered at the design's size times a whole scale: cargo run -q -p morningstar-devtools --bin morningstar-diff -- design.png out/settings.png --out out/diff"},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"Using the app while you write it: the preview","u":"building-screens.html#using-the-app-while-you-write-it-the-preview","s":"Guide","x":"The loop above shows one frame. To tap through the app, type into it, scroll it and watch it move, run it in a window on the Mac (ADR 0040): It reloads. Save a source file and the app is built again and started again in the same place on the screen, without taking the keyboard from your editor. If the build fails, the old window stays up, its title says so, and the compiler's errors are in the terminal.It starts over"},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"Accessibility","u":"building-screens.html#accessibility","s":"Guide","x":"A Morningstar screen is described to TalkBack and VoiceOver as a tree of nodes derived from its widgets (ADR 0032). Most of it needs nothing from you:a Text is read as text; a Button as a button with its label, \"disabled\" without on_press;anything with on_press (a row, a card) is one stop, read as the texts inside it in order: a list row is one swipe, not five;a TextInput is an edit box: its placeholder is its hint, "},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"Pitfalls","u":"building-screens.html#pitfalls","s":"Guide","x":"Each of these produces a wrong picture, not an error.Fill inside hug collapses. fill_width() on a child of a box that hugs gives the child zero width (text vanishes). Give the parent a width: usually .fill_width() up the chain to something sized by the screen.In a row, exactly the element that should give way gets .fill_width(). Two long hugging texts in one row overflow instead of wrapping.A ScrollView needs a heigh"},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"From HTML/CSS or a design tool's inspector","u":"building-screens.html#from-htmlcss-or-a-design-tools-inspector","s":"Guide","x":"Learned reproducing a real twelve-screen design (examples/fundidesk, ADR 0015). The left column is what the design says; all numbers go across unchanged as dp.Design propertyMorningstardisplay: flex (row) / flex-direction: columnrow![..] / col![..]gap: 12px.gap(12.0)padding: 16px 20px 24px (top, sides, bottom).padding_ltrb(20.0, 16.0, 20.0, 24.0) (note the order: left, top, right, bottom)margin-top: 28px on a child o"},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"Pitfalls of translating CSS","u":"building-screens.html#pitfalls-of-translating-css","s":"Guide","x":"Each of these cost a pixel or more per element until it was understood.Padding starts inside the border in CSS; here the border is drawn over the box's edge and takes no room. border: 1px; padding: 16px is .border(1.0, c).padding(17.0). Centred content is unaffected.Check box-sizing. With box-sizing: border-box, height: 52px is the outer height. Without it (the default for div and a; button is border-box), the border"},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"The order that worked","u":"building-screens.html#the-order-that-worked","s":"Guide","x":"Survey every screen before building one: collect the colours, type styles, radii and icons into a theme, an icon file and shared components. Count how often each value occurs; the frequent ones are tokens.Build a screen top-down from the markup, one function per block, quoting the CSS in a comment.Render at the reference's exact size and compare. Before reading regions, compare the extent of ink row by row between th"},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"Turned things, illustrations and text effects","u":"building-screens.html#turned-things-illustrations-and-text-effects","s":"Guide","x":"Added for a design full of tilted tiles and inline illustrations (examples/callready, ADR 0017). Everything here is in morningstar::ui::prelude."},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"A turned tile, with its text","u":"building-screens.html#a-turned-tile-with-its-text","s":"Guide","x":".rotate(degrees) turns a widget and everything inside it about the widget's own centre, clockwise for positive degrees, exactly as CSS transform: rotate(..) does. Layout does not see it: the widget keeps the room it was laid out in, and its turned picture overlaps whatever is beside it. So build the tile as if it were upright, check it against the design upright if you can, and add the turn last. Background, border ("},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"A turned text, and transforms with more than one function","u":"building-screens.html#a-turned-text-and-transforms-with-more-than-one-function","s":"Guide","x":"Text takes the same modifier: text(\"3\").size(82.0).rotate(-5.0). It turns about the centre of its box, which is the line box (font size times line height), as in CSS, so give it the design's line-height.For a CSS list of functions, write them in the same order with the and_* methods. The order matters and this is the order CSS means: each function acts in the axes the ones before it left behind. Lengths are dp. Trans"},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"Italic","u":"building-screens.html#italic","s":"Guide","x":".italic() on a Text, or on one span(..) of a rich text. If the family has an italic font and it was added in setup, that font is used. If not, the upright letters are slanted by 14 degrees, which is what a browser does for font-style: italic on such a family; the text takes exactly the room the upright text would. There is nothing to do with Transform here."},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"An illustration or an icon from an SVG file","u":"building-screens.html#an-illustration-or-an-icon-from-an-svg-file","s":"Guide","x":"Load the SVG. Save the designer's file (or the inline <svg> of the design's HTML, with xmlns added) under the app's assets/svg, and name it once in a static (ADR 0027): The file is parsed the first time get() is called and kept; never call SvgPicture::parse from a view. A file that arrives at run time (from a server) is parsed once, where it arrives, and the SvgPicture kept in the app's state; it is a cheap handle to"},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"Glows, glass and soft light","u":"building-screens.html#glows-glass-and-soft-light","s":"Guide","x":"Added for a design made of light (examples/atlas, ADR 0033 to 0036). Everything here is drawn by the GPU as part of the box, in the same draw call as every other box: it costs no image and can change size every frame.A radial gradient is written as CSS writes it: a shape, where its centre is, how far it reaches, and its stops. Radial::circle() or Radial::ellipse() (CSS's default when the word is missing). .at(x, y) i"},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"A row that scrolls sideways","u":"building-screens.html#a-row-that-scrolls-sideways","s":"Guide","x":"It fills the width it is given and is as tall as its content, which is laid out as wide as it likes: chips keep their natural width instead of wrapping (ADR 0037). Put the side padding on the content, as above, so that the first chip starts in line with the page and still slides out under the screen's edge. Inside a page that scrolls, a sideways drag moves the row and an up-and-down drag the page. ScrollTo::offset(\"c"},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"A hard text shadow","u":"building-screens.html#a-hard-text-shadow","s":"Guide","x":"text-shadow with a zero blur is the text drawn twice. .shadow(x, y, colour) does that: the copy goes behind, moved by (x, y) dp, and takes no room. A blurred text shadow is not available."},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"An outlined digit","u":"building-screens.html#an-outlined-digit","s":"Guide","x":"The stroke is centred on the letters' edges, half inside and half outside, over the fill, as in a browser, and the text takes the room it would without it. Outlined text is drawn as an image (like a picture, with the same cost), so use it for a digit or a short word, not for paragraphs. A rich text is outlined in its one base colour."},{"t":"Building screens: from a picture to code","h":"Numbers that line up","u":"building-screens.html#numbers-that-line-up","s":"Guide","x":".tabular_nums() is font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums. It matters when the font's digits are proportional by default (Outfit's and Inter's are): a right-aligned figure or a running timer is laid out differently without it. On a font without the feature it does nothing."},{"t":"Design to code: the loop","h":"","u":"design-to-code.html","s":"Guide","x":"Design to code: the loopHow to turn a picture of a screen into Morningstar Rust code, whether you are a developer or an AI agent. The method is a loop that takes seconds per turn and needs no phone:Render the code to a PNG on this machine.Compare the PNG with the design.Read the report: does it match, by how much not, and where.Fix the worst region and go to 1.The background is in ADR 0008. The tools live in tools/mo"},{"t":"Design to code: the loop","h":"0. Prepare the design image","u":"design-to-code.html#0-prepare-the-design-image","s":"Guide","x":"PNG. Any bit depth, palette, greyscale or interlacing is read. Colour profiles are ignored and pixels are taken as sRGB, so export in sRGB (not Display P3).Export at the size you will render, or larger. A 2x or 3x export compared with a 1x-sized render is averaged down cleanly. The other direction (a small design against a large render) enlarges the design, which blurs its text and edges and produces differences that"},{"t":"Design to code: the loop","h":"1. Render","u":"design-to-code.html#1-render","s":"Guide","x":"morningstar_devtools::screenshot(app, size, elapsed) runs one frame of an App through the real renderer and returns PNG bytes. Put it in an example next to the app, as examples/hello-gpu/examples/screenshot.rs does: The frame is a function of the app's state, the clock you pass and the size, so the same call gives the same picture every time. Choose elapsed deliberately if the screen animates."},{"t":"Design to code: the loop","h":"2. Compare","u":"design-to-code.html#2-compare","s":"Guide","x":"The first image is the reference (the design), the second the candidate (your render). The report goes to standard output. The exit status is 0 when the images match within the tolerance, 1 when they differ, 2 when the comparison could not be made (bad arguments, unreadable file).OptionDefaultMeaning--out <file>Heat map PNG.--side-by-side <file>Reference, candidate and heat map in one PNG.--threshold <error>2Smallest"},{"t":"Design to code: the loop","h":"3. Read the report","u":"design-to-code.html#3-read-the-report","s":"Guide","x":"This is a real run: the example app idle (reference) against the same app with its button held (candidate). Read it in this order.result and reason. PASS means stop.aspect. If it says MISMATCH, fix the render size before anything else; every other number is about a partial comparison.region lines. They are ordered by severity (the sum of the errors of the differing pixels, so large areas and strong differences come f"},{"t":"Design to code: the loop","h":"The error scale","u":"design-to-code.html#the-error-scale","s":"Guide","x":"Colour error is the distance in Oklab (a perceptually uniform colour space) times 100.ErrorMeaning0identical0.3one step of one 8-bit channel near mid grey2about the smallest difference visible side by side; the default threshold10clearly a different colour100black against whitemean_error is over all compared pixels, including those under the threshold. diff_percent counts only pixels over it."},{"t":"Design to code: the loop","h":"What \"differs\" means","u":"design-to-code.html#what-differs-means","s":"Guide","x":"A pixel differs when its colour error is over the threshold and the difference is not explained by anti-aliasing. With --aa-radius 1, a pixel is accepted if each image's colour at that position either appears in the other image within one pixel (the edge moved by a pixel) or is a mix of the two most different colours there (the edge is covered by a different fraction). The check runs in both directions, so a missing "},{"t":"Design to code: the loop","h":"Aspect ratio","u":"design-to-code.html#aspect-ratio","s":"Guide","x":"If the shapes differ by more than a pixel of rounding, the report says aspect: MISMATCH with both ratios and the height the candidate should have, and the result is FAIL.--fit width (default) scales the design uniformly to the candidate's width and aligns the tops. Nothing is distorted. Rows only one image has are not compared (uncovered_rows, cropped_rows).--fit stretch distorts the design to cover the candidate. Ra"},{"t":"Design to code: the loop","h":"JSON","u":"design-to-code.html#json","s":"Guide","x":"--json prints the same content as one object: passed, failure, candidate, reference, aspect_mismatch, the totals, regions (each with rank, x, y, width, height, differing_pixels, mean_error, max_error, reference_color, candidate_color) and map. Both formats begin with the version string morningstar-diff report v1; key order and number formats are fixed."},{"t":"Design to code: the loop","h":"4. Iterate","u":"design-to-code.html#4-iterate","s":"Guide","x":"Fix one thing per turn, the top region first, and re-run. Large errors hide small ones.Get structure right before detail: size and aspect, background, the big blocks, then spacing, then text, then edges.Read coordinates from the report, not from the picture.Loosen the gate rather than fight noise: once the remaining regions are text rendering or edge anti-aliasing, the screen is as close as this loop can prove. Say w"},{"t":"Design to code: the loop","h":"Vector artwork: load the SVG, and look at it first","u":"design-to-code.html#vector-artwork-load-the-svg-and-look-at-it-first","s":"Guide","x":"An illustration or icon in the design is an SVG file, and an app loads it as it is (SvgAsset, Picture::svg, Icon::svg; docs/building-screens.md, \"An illustration or an icon from an SVG file\"). Before wiring one in, render it alone with the same loader: --scale is pixels per unit of the file's size (2 for a design reference at 2x); --size 96 or --size 200x120 fits it into that many pixels instead. --background checker"},{"t":"Design to code: the loop","h":"From Rust","u":"design-to-code.html#from-rust","s":"Guide","x":"The CLI is a thin wrapper over the library:"},{"t":"Design to code: the loop","h":"Keeping it right: visual regression tests","u":"design-to-code.html#keeping-it-right-visual-regression-tests","s":"Guide","x":"Once a screen matches, store the render and have a test hold it there: Create or update references by running the tests with MORNINGSTAR_UPDATE_REFERENCES=1, then review the changed PNGs like any other diff.On failure the test prints the report and writes home.actual.png and home.diff.png (side by side) next to the reference. The next passing run removes them. Add *.actual.png and *.diff.png to .gitignore.A missing r"},{"t":"Design to code: the loop","h":"Limits","u":"design-to-code.html#limits","s":"Guide","x":"The desktop render is not the phone. Screenshots come from this machine's GPU (Metal on a Mac). A phone uses Vulkan or GLES with different rasterisation of edges and glyphs. The loop proves layout and colour, not device-exact pixels. Check on a device before calling a screen done.Fonts must be the design's fonts. The app draws with the font files it registers in App::setup. If the design uses a font the app does not "},{"t":"Testing","h":"","u":"guide/testing.html","s":"Guide","x":"TestingAn app is tested on the development machine, with no phone and no server. That is possible because the app never does I/O: it asks for work as data, and a test can look at the request and answer it."},{"t":"Testing","h":"The kinds of test","u":"guide/testing.html#the-kinds-of-test","s":"Guide","x":"TestShowsWhereA journeyThat tapping, typing and answers lead to the right state and screentests/e2e.rsAn accessibility checkThat no screen has a control without a label, a small target or low contrasttests/accessibility.rsA picture of each screenThat a screen still looks as it did when you last accepted ittests/screens.rsA fidelity testThat a screen matches the design's picture to within a measured limittests/fidelit"},{"t":"Testing","h":"Answering in the phone's place","u":"guide/testing.html#answering-in-the-phones-place","s":"Guide","x":"Ui::testing(app) holds every request the app makes instead of sending it. The test reads it and answers. Stored values and the install id are given as the phone would have them: To playUseThe serverui.pending_effects() to read the request, ui.answer_http(..) to replyThe microphone and the speakerui.answer_recording_started(..), ui.answer_recording(..), ui.answer_playback(..)A live roomui.live_room() to see what was j"},{"t":"Testing","h":"A finger and a keyboard","u":"guide/testing.html#a-finger-and-a-keyboard","s":"Guide","x":"A journey taps where a widget was laid out and types through the same interface the keyboard uses. A generated app has a small Device in tests/support/mod.rs that does both: Tapping by id sends real pointer events to where the widget was laid out, so layout and hit testing are part of every journey. ui.rect_of(id) is where a widget was laid out, ui.visible_rect_of(id) the part that shows, and ui.overflows() lists box"},{"t":"Testing","h":"Pictures","u":"guide/testing.html#pictures","s":"Guide","x":"tests/screens.rs renders each screen and compares it with a reference picture in tests/references/. The first run writes the references; look at them and commit them. When a visual change is one you meant, accept it: A passing test does not show that a screen looks right, only that it has not changed. After changing anything visual, render it and look:"},{"t":"Testing","h":"Holding a screen to its design","u":"guide/testing.html#holding-a-screen-to-its-design","s":"Guide","x":"The designed examples compare every screen with the design's own picture and fail if more than a measured share of pixels differs. Barua Talk's limits, with what was measured:ScreenMeasured differenceLimitHome0.0847%0.10%Room0.6900%0.70%Incoming call0.0154%0.05%Video call0.2860%0.30%Phone call0.0499%0.05%Design to code is the method, and morningstar-diff the tool."},{"t":"Testing","h":"In CI","u":"guide/testing.html#in-ci","s":"Guide","x":"The workflow in .github/workflows/ci.yml runs formatting, lints, the tests that need no GPU, the Android lints and the servers' tests on every push. The job that runs every test on a GPU is started by hand. ADR 0043.The API a test uses is in the Ui reference."},{"t":"Build for Android","h":"","u":"guide/android.html","s":"Guide","x":"Build for AndroidOne script builds the app's library, generates its launcher icons, packages an APK and, if asked, installs and starts it. SettingMeaningDefaultMORNINGSTAR_APPThe package to buildhello-gpuMORNINGSTAR_ABISThe architectures. arm64-v8a alone is right for a phone and halves the time and diskarm64-v8a and x86_64ANDROID_HOMEThe Android SDKRequiredANDROID_NDK_HOMEThe NDK to useThe newest complete one under $"},{"t":"Build for Android","h":"What the build needs","u":"guide/android.html#what-the-build-needs","s":"Guide","x":"The Android SDK and NDK, cargo-ndk and a JDK 17 or newer; see Installation. The host targets API 36 and runs on API 24 (Android 7.0) and later."},{"t":"Build for Android","h":"What is in the APK","u":"guide/android.html#what-is-in-the-apk","s":"Guide","x":"The base host carries the app's library and asks for the INTERNET and RECORD_AUDIO permissions. Everything else is opt-in through services in the app's Cargo.toml, and an app that does not name a service carries none of it. Opt-in services lists what each one adds."},{"t":"Build for Android","h":"A server on your machine","u":"guide/android.html#a-server-on-your-machine","s":"Guide","x":"A phone connected by USB reaches a server on the development machine through adb reverse: The link is dropped when the cable is unplugged or the phone reconnects; run it again. For a deployed server, give its address when building, so that it is never in the repository:"},{"t":"Build for Android","h":"Reading what the app does","u":"guide/android.html#reading-what-the-app-does","s":"Guide","x":"The host logs under the tag Morningstar: Prefer proving behaviour from the log. A phone connected over adb belongs to a person who may be using it: the repository's rule is never to send it input or capture its screen directly. The only permitted route is tools/adb-guarded.sh, which checks that the expected app is in front immediately before each action, and even that only when the owner has agreed."},{"t":"Build for Android","h":"What has been verified on a phone","u":"guide/android.html#what-has-been-verified-on-a-phone","s":"Guide","x":"One phone so far, a Galaxy A27 on Android 16 with Vulkan: a frame is presented, text and the full shader match the desktop render by eye, touch reaches the app, the surface survives going to the background and back, an idle screen draws nothing, and typing works with the system keyboard. Platform status has the full list, and what has not been seen."},{"t":"Build for Android","h":"Type-checking the Android code without building an APK","u":"guide/android.html#type-checking-the-android-code-without-building-an-apk","s":"Guide","x":""},{"t":"iOS","h":"","u":"guide/ios.html","s":"Guide","x":"iOSiOS does not work yet. This page says exactly what exists, so that nobody is surprised."},{"t":"iOS","h":"What exists","u":"guide/ios.html#what-exists","s":"Guide","x":"The shared code builds for iOS. cargo check -p morningstar-ios --target aarch64-apple-ios passes, so the application crate, the UI layer and the renderer type-check for the platform.The host is written. templates/ios holds a UIKit host in Swift, the C header it shares with crates/morningstar-ios, and tools/build-ios.sh, which builds an app as an XCFramework and generates its icon and names.Both halves are kept in ste"},{"t":"iOS","h":"What does not","u":"guide/ios.html#what-does-not","s":"Guide","x":"Nothing has been compiled with Xcode. The Swift host has never been built, so nothing has ever run on an iPhone or a simulator: no frame, no touch, no keyboard.Live rooms answer Unsupported. The iOS host refuses every join honestly. Recording, playback, HTTP and storage are written in HostServices.swift and are equally unverified.The generated icon and build settings have been tested as files; that Xcode accepts them"},{"t":"iOS","h":"What it would take","u":"guide/ios.html#what-it-would-take","s":"Guide","x":"A machine with full Xcode and XcodeGen: Expect the first build to need fixes. Every row of the verification table that says \"never compiled\" is a thing to check; see Platform status."},{"t":"Widget gallery","h":"","u":"widgets/index.html","s":"Widgets","x":"Widget galleryEvery screen is a tree of these widgets. Each page lists the widget's methods, read from the source's own doc comments when the site is built, and shows a sample that is compiled with the workspace."},{"t":"Widget gallery","h":"Layout","u":"widgets/index.html#layout","s":"Widgets","x":"ContainerThe box. Padding, a background, a radius, a border, a shadow, and children.Column, Row and WrapChildren one under the other, side by side, or wrapping onto new lines.StackChildren on top of each other, pinned to an anchor.ScrollViewContent taller or wider than its box.SafeArea and SpacerClear of the system's bars, and flexible gaps."},{"t":"Widget gallery","h":"Content","u":"widgets/index.html#content-1","s":"Widgets","x":"TextText in the theme's type scale, with spans for mixed styles.TextInputAn editable text and its keyboard.Button and PressableFive kinds of button, and anything else that can be tapped.DividerA line between rows.Picture and AvatarImages, SVG files, and people.IconOne-colour vector artwork.LiveVideoA camera or a shared screen from a live room."},{"t":"Widget gallery","h":"Shared by all of them","u":"widgets/index.html#shared-by-all-of-them","s":"Widgets","x":"Common modifiersSize, position, identity, motion and accessibility, on every widget.Colours, gradients and shadowsWhat a box can be painted with."},{"t":"Widget gallery","h":"From a design's words to a widget","u":"widgets/index.html#from-a-designs-words-to-a-widget","s":"Widgets","x":"The design hasUseVertical or horizontal auto-layoutcol![..], row![..]Layers, a badge, an overlaystack![..], children pinned with .place(..)A frame with a fill, radius, stroke, shadowContainerTexttext(\"..\")A bold or coloured phrase inside a sentenceText::rich([..]) with span(..)A buttonButton; anything else tappable: .on_press(..) on a boxA line between rowsDividerA flexible or fixed gapSpacerScrolling contentScrollVi"},{"t":"Common modifiers","h":"","u":"widgets/modifiers.html","s":"Widgets","x":"Common modifiersEvery widget has these. They are chained after the widget's own methods, in any order."},{"t":"Common modifiers","h":"Size and position","u":"widgets/modifiers.html#size-and-position","s":"Widgets","x":"MethodWhat it doeswidth(dp: f32)Fixed width in dp.height(dp: f32)Fixed height in dp.fill_width()Take the width the parent has left (\"fill container\").fill_height()Take the height the parent has left.fill()Fill both axes.fill_width_weight(weight: f32)Fill the width with a weight: siblings that fill share the space left in proportion to their weights (flex in Flutter).fill_height_weight(weight: f32)Fill the height with"},{"t":"Common modifiers","h":"Identity","u":"widgets/modifiers.html#identity","s":"Widgets","x":".id(value) gives a widget an explicit identity, which is what scroll positions, focus, presses and animations belong to. The application model says when one is needed."},{"t":"Common modifiers","h":"Motion","u":"widgets/modifiers.html#motion","s":"Widgets","x":"MethodWhat it doesopacity(opacity: f32)Draws the widget and everything inside it at this opacityanimate(duration: Duration, easing: Easing)Shows changes of this widget's own properties over duration instead of at once: when a later view gives the same widget (the same identity; give it an .id(..)) a different opacity, transform or offset, solid background colour, or fixed width or height, it is drawn on the way from "},{"t":"Common modifiers","h":"Accessibility","u":"widgets/modifiers.html#accessibility","s":"Widgets","x":"MethodWhat it doessemantic_label(label: impl Into<Cow<str>>)What a screen reader calls this widget, instead of the text inside it.semantic_role(role: Role)What the widget is to a screen reader, when the default (a tappable box is a button, a text is text) is not rightheading()Marks the widget as a heading, which a screen reader's user can jump between.hint(hint: impl Into<Cow<str>>)What the widget is for, spoken afte"},{"t":"Common modifiers","h":"Converting messages","u":"widgets/modifiers.html#converting-messages","s":"Widgets","x":"Element::map converts the messages a subtree produces, so a view written for one message type can be used inside an app with another.MethodWhat it doesmap<N>(convert: impl Fn(M) -> N + Copy + Send + Sync + 'static) -> Element<N>Converts the messages this subtree produces, so a view written for one message type can be embedded in a parent with another"},{"t":"Container","h":"","u":"widgets/container.html","s":"Widgets","x":"ContainerA box: the one widget that has children, padding and decoration. Column, Row, Wrap, Stack, ScrollView, SafeArea, Pressable and Spacer, and the col!, row! and stack! macros, all produce a Container with a different preset, so every method here works on all of them. It corresponds to a frame with auto-layout in a design tool.Paint order: shadow, background, border, then the children in order. Later children co"},{"t":"Container","h":"Creating and filling","u":"widgets/container.html#creating-and-filling","s":"Widgets","x":"MethodWhat it doesnew(child: impl Into<Element<M>>)A box around one child.empty()A box with no children: a coloured shape.push(child: impl Into<Element<M>>)Adds a child after the existing ones.push_maybe(child: Option<impl Into<Element<M>>>)Adds a child if there is one"},{"t":"Container","h":"Padding and spacing","u":"widgets/container.html#padding-and-spacing","s":"Widgets","x":"MethodWhat it doespadding(all: f32)The same padding on all four sides, in dp.padding_xy(horizontal: f32, vertical: f32)horizontal padding on the left and right, vertical on the top and bottom.padding_ltrb(left: f32, top: f32, right: f32, bottom: f32)Padding per edge in the order left, top, right, bottom.gap(dp: f32)Space between adjacent children of a row or column, in dp.wrap()Makes a row wrap: children that do not "},{"t":"Container","h":"Alignment","u":"widgets/container.html#alignment","s":"Widgets","x":"MethodWhat it doesmain_align(align: MainAlign)How a row or column distributes its children along its own axis when there is space left over.cross_align(align: CrossAlign)Where a row or column puts each child across its axis: vertically in a row, horizontally in a column.content_align(anchor: Alignment)Where a stack (or a Container::new) puts its children: one of nine anchors.center()Centres the children on both axes,"},{"t":"Container","h":"Paint","u":"widgets/container.html#paint","s":"Widgets","x":"MethodWhat it doesbackground(color: impl Into<ColorRef>)Fills the box with a colour.gradient(gradient: Gradient)Fills the box with a gradient.radial(gradient: Radial)Paints a radial gradient over the background, and over the radial gradients added before it.radials(top_first: impl IntoIterator<Item = Radial>)Several radial gradients in CSS order, the first on top, over the background colourblur(sigma: f32)Blurs the b"},{"t":"Container","h":"Touch","u":"widgets/container.html#touch","s":"Widgets","x":"MethodWhat it doeson_increment(message: M)What the accessibility action \"increment\" sends: for a value that is raised and lowered (a stepper, a slider).on_decrement(message: M)What the accessibility action \"decrement\" sends.on_press(message: M)Makes the box tappable: a tap on it delivers message to Application::update.on_press_maybe(message: Option<M>)Like on_press, tappable only with Some.pressed_background(color: i"},{"t":"Container","h":"Around a text input","u":"widgets/container.html#around-a-text-input","s":"Widgets","x":"MethodWhat it doesinput_frame()Makes this box the frame of the TextInput inside it (the first one, if there are several): a tap anywhere on the box, its padding and icons included, focuses the input.focused_border(width: f32, color: impl Into<ColorRef>)The border while the TextInput inside has focus, replacing border.focused_background(color: impl Into<ColorRef>)The background while the TextInput inside has focus, re"},{"t":"Column, Row and Wrap","h":"","u":"widgets/column-row.html","s":"Widgets","x":"Column, Row and WrapA column arranges its children top to bottom, a row side by side. Most screens are columns and rows nested inside each other."},{"t":"Column, Row and Wrap","h":"Creating one","u":"widgets/column-row.html#creating-one","s":"Widgets","x":"WriteForcol![a, b, c]A column with these childrenrow![a, b, c]A row with these childrenColumn::new(), Row::new()An empty one, filled with .push(..) in a loopWrap::new()A row whose children continue on the next lineEach returns a Container, so padding, gap, alignment, paint and touch are the container's methods."},{"t":"Column, Row and Wrap","h":"Spacing and alignment","u":"widgets/column-row.html#spacing-and-alignment","s":"Widgets","x":"MethodWhat it doesgap(dp: f32)Space between adjacent children of a row or column, in dp.wrap()Makes a row wrap: children that do not fit on a line continue on the next, as CSS flex-wrap: wrap does.line_gap(dp: f32)Space between the lines of a wrapping row, in dp (CSS row-gap).main_align(align: MainAlign)How a row or column distributes its children along its own axis when there is space left over.cross_align(align: Cr"},{"t":"Column, Row and Wrap","h":"Sharing the space","u":"widgets/column-row.html#sharing-the-space","s":"Widgets","x":"Children that fill share what is left along the axis, by weight:"},{"t":"Column, Row and Wrap","h":"Wrapping onto new lines","u":"widgets/column-row.html#wrapping-onto-new-lines","s":"Widgets","x":".gap(..) is the space between neighbours on a line and .line_gap(..) the space between lines.Long lists. A column builds every child each frame. That is what the example apps do for lists of tens of rows. There is no widget that builds rows only as they scroll into view.Related: Stack, SafeArea and Spacer, and the layout rules."},{"t":"Stack","h":"","u":"widgets/stack.html","s":"Widgets","x":"StackA stack lays its children on top of each other. The first child is at the bottom. It hugs its largest child unless given a size."},{"t":"Stack","h":"Placing children","u":"widgets/stack.html#placing-children","s":"Widgets","x":"A child is pinned to one of nine anchors with .place(..) and moved from there with .offset(x, y), which does not affect layout.AnchorAlignment::TOP_LEFTAlignment::TOP_CENTERAlignment::TOP_RIGHTAlignment::CENTER_LEFTAlignment::CENTERAlignment::CENTER_RIGHTAlignment::BOTTOM_LEFTAlignment::BOTTOM_CENTERAlignment::BOTTOM_RIGHT.content_align(anchor) on the stack sets the anchor for every child that does not choose its own"},{"t":"Stack","h":"Creating one","u":"widgets/stack.html#creating-one","s":"Widgets","x":"WriteForstack![below, above]A stack with these childrenStack::new()An empty one, filled with .push(..)It is a Container: it takes a background, a radius, .clip() to cut its children to its shape, and touch."},{"t":"Stack","h":"Uses","u":"widgets/stack.html#uses","s":"Widgets","x":"A badge on an avatar or a tile.Text or controls over a picture or a video, as on Barua Talk's call screen.A sheet or a dialog over a screen: a dimmed box with .block_touches() under the sheet, so that nothing behind it reacts."},{"t":"ScrollView","h":"","u":"widgets/scroll-view.html","s":"Widgets","x":"ScrollViewA viewport around content that is taller, or wider, than the space it has. MethodWhat it doesnew<M>(content: impl Into<Element<M>>) -> Container<M>A viewport around content, filling its parent.horizontal<M>(content: impl Into<Element<M>>) -> Container<M>A viewport that scrolls sideways: a row of chips or cards wider than the screen."},{"t":"ScrollView","h":"Rules","u":"widgets/scroll-view.html#rules","s":"Widgets","x":"It fills its parent, so it needs a height from above: put it in a column that fills, or directly in a SafeArea.Its position belongs to its identity. Give a scroll view whose content changes an .id(..) and keep it; while a scroll view with the same id is in consecutive frames, its position is kept. One that is absent from a frame starts again at the top.Content that grows leaves the position where it is. Content that "},{"t":"ScrollView","h":"Moving it from code","u":"widgets/scroll-view.html#moving-it-from-code","s":"Widgets","x":"Ask from effects, with a ScrollTo:"},{"t":"ScrollView","h":"Sideways","u":"widgets/scroll-view.html#sideways","s":"Widgets","x":"ScrollView::horizontal(row) scrolls a row of chips or cards that is wider than the screen. A drag is given to whichever scroll view it moves along, so a sideways row works inside a vertical screen. ADR 0037."},{"t":"SafeArea and Spacer","h":"","u":"widgets/safe-area-spacer.html","s":"Widgets","x":"SafeArea and Spacer"},{"t":"SafeArea and Spacer","h":"SafeArea","u":"widgets/safe-area-spacer.html#safearea","s":"Widgets","x":"The screen is drawn edge to edge, under the status bar, the gesture bar and any display cutout. SafeArea keeps its child clear of them.MethodWhat it doesnew<M>(child: impl Into<Element<M>>) -> Container<M>A full-size box that insets child on all four edges.For a bar that should reach the screen's edge with only its contents kept clear, use .safe_area(edges) on the bar's own box instead:MethodWhat it doesnew(left: boo"},{"t":"SafeArea and Spacer","h":"Spacer","u":"widgets/safe-area-spacer.html#spacer","s":"Widgets","x":"A spacer is an empty box along the parent's axis.MethodWhat it doesnew<M>() -> Container<M>Takes all the space left along the parent's axis, pushing what follows to the far end.weighted<M>(weight: f32) -> Container<M>Like new, sharing the space left with other fills in proportion to weight.fixed<M>(dp: f32) -> Container<M>Exactly dp along the parent's axis and nothing across it.Spacer::new() between two children push"},{"t":"SafeArea and Spacer","h":"Pressable","u":"widgets/safe-area-spacer.html#pressable","s":"Widgets","x":"Pressable::new(child) is a box around a child, ready for .on_press(..). It is described with Button."},{"t":"Text","h":"","u":"widgets/text.html","s":"Widgets","x":"TextText in a step of the theme's type scale. text(\"..\") is short for Text::new(\"..\")."},{"t":"Text","h":"Creating","u":"widgets/text.html#creating","s":"Widgets","x":"MethodWhat it doesnew(content: impl Into<Cow<str>>)Text from a literal or a String.rich<S: Into<Span>>(spans: impl IntoIterator<Item = S>)Text made of several Spans, each of which may have its own weight, colour or font family: a sentence with a bold or coloured phrase in it.A &'static str or a String also becomes a text wherever a widget is expected."},{"t":"Text","h":"The type scale","u":"widgets/text.html#the-type-scale","s":"Widgets","x":"MethodWhat it doesrole(role: TextRole)Uses a step of the theme's type scale (size, weight and line height).display()The one big number or word on a screen.title()A screen title.headline()A section or card heading.body()Running text. The default.label()Button, tab and field labels.caption()Metadata, timestamps and hints."},{"t":"Text","h":"Size, weight, colour and family","u":"widgets/text.html#size-weight-colour-and-family","s":"Widgets","x":"MethodWhat it doessize(dp: f32)Font size in dp, overriding the role's.weight(weight: TextWeight)Font weight, overriding the role's.regular()Regular weight (400).medium()Medium weight (500).semibold()Semibold weight (600).bold()Bold weight (700).color(color: impl Into<ColorRef>)Text colour: a Color or a ThemeColor.muted()The theme's secondary text colour.family(family: impl Into<FontFamily>)The font family, overriding"},{"t":"Text","h":"Lines","u":"widgets/text.html#lines","s":"Widgets","x":"MethodWhat it doesalign(align: TextAlign)Alignment of the lines within the text's own box.centered()Centres the lines within the text's box.line_height(multiple: f32)Line height as a multiple of the font size, overriding the role's.line_height_dp(dp: f32)Line height in dp, as CSS and design tools usually give it (line-height: 22px), overriding the role's.letter_spacing(dp: f32)Extra space after every character, in dp"},{"t":"Text","h":"Effects","u":"widgets/text.html#effects","s":"Widgets","x":"MethodWhat it doesshadow(x: f32, y: f32, color: impl Into<ColorRef>)A hard shadow: the same text drawn once more behind it, moved by (x, y) dp, in color (CSS text-shadow: 3px 3px 0 #fff).outline(width: f32, color: impl Into<ColorRef>)Outlines the letters with a stroke width dp wide, centred on their edges, over the fill (CSS -webkit-text-stroke: 1.5px #fff)."},{"t":"Text","h":"Several styles in one text","u":"widgets/text.html#several-styles-in-one-text","s":"Widgets","x":"MethodWhat it doesnew(content: impl Into<Cow<str>>)A span in the style of the text it is part of.italic()Sets this span in italics (CSS font-style: italic, an <em>).weight(weight: TextWeight)Font weight of this span.regular()Regular weight (400).medium()Medium weight (500).semibold()Semibold weight (600).bold()Bold weight (700).color(color: impl Into<ColorRef>)Colour of this span: a Color or a ThemeColor.family(famil"},{"t":"TextInput","h":"","u":"widgets/text-input.html","s":"Widgets","x":"TextInputAn editable text. It shows the value it is given and delivers each new value as a message; it has no frame of its own."},{"t":"TextInput","h":"Value and messages","u":"widgets/text-input.html#value-and-messages","s":"Widgets","x":"MethodWhat it doesnew(value: impl Into<String>)A single-line field showing value, in the theme's body text.placeholder(text: impl Into<Cow<str>>)Shown, in the placeholder_color, while the value is empty.placeholder_color(color: impl Into<ColorRef>)Colour of the placeholder (the theme's muted text by default).on_input(message: impl Fn(String) -> M + Send + Sync + 'static)The message for a new value.on_submit(message: "},{"t":"TextInput","h":"The keyboard","u":"widgets/text-input.html#the-keyboard","s":"Widgets","x":"MethodWhat it doessecure(secure: bool)A password: each character is shown as a bullet, and the keyboard neither learns nor suggests.keyboard(keyboard: KeyboardType)Which keyboard to show.capitalization(capitalization: Capitalization)What the keyboard capitalises by itself (nothing by default).action(action: ImeAction)The keyboard's action keyno_autocorrect()Turns the keyboard's correction and suggestions off (they ar"},{"t":"TextInput","h":"What the value may hold","u":"widgets/text-input.html#what-the-value-may-hold","s":"Widgets","x":"MethodWhat it doesmultiline()Several lines: the text wraps at the field's width, the field grows downwards with it, and the Return key inserts a line break.max_lines(lines: u32)For a multiline field: it grows to at most this many lines and then scrolls inside itself.max_length(characters: usize)Most characters the value may hold.digits_only()Keeps only the digits 0 to 9 of whatever is typed or pasted by the keyboard."},{"t":"TextInput","h":"Style","u":"widgets/text-input.html#style","s":"Widgets","x":"MethodWhat it doesstyle(text: Text)Takes size, weight, line height, colour, alignment, family and letter spacing from a Text (its content is ignored), so a field can share a design's text style helperrole(role: TextRole)Uses a step of the theme's type scale; see Text::role.size(dp: f32)Font size in dp.weight(weight: TextWeight)Font weight.color(color: impl Into<ColorRef>)Colour of the value (the theme's text colour b"},{"t":"TextInput","h":"The box around it","u":"widgets/text-input.html#the-box-around-it","s":"Widgets","x":"These are methods of the Container that holds the input:MethodWhat it doesinput_frame()Makes this box the frame of the TextInput inside it (the first one, if there are several): a tap anywhere on the box, its padding and icons included, focuses the input.focused_border(width: f32, color: impl Into<ColorRef>)The border while the TextInput inside has focus, replacing border.focused_background(color: impl Into<ColorRef>"},{"t":"Button and Pressable","h":"","u":"widgets/button.html","s":"Widgets","x":"Button and Pressable"},{"t":"Button and Pressable","h":"Button","u":"widgets/button.html#button","s":"Widgets","x":"A button with a label, in one of five kinds. MethodWhat it doesnew(label: impl Into<Cow<str>>)A filled button in the theme's primary colour.on_press(message: M)The message a tap delivers.on_press_maybe(message: Option<M>)Enabled only with Some.primary()Filled with the primary colour.secondary()Filled with the quiet SurfaceVariant colour.outlined()Transparent with an outline.plain()Label only, in the primary colour.de"},{"t":"Button and Pressable","h":"Pressable","u":"widgets/button.html#pressable","s":"Widgets","x":"Anything can be tapped: .on_press(message) works on every box. Pressable::new(child) is a box around a child, ready for it. MethodWhat it doesnew<M>(child: impl Into<Element<M>>) -> Container<M>A box around child, ready for .on_press(..).MethodWhat it doeson_press(message: M)Makes the box tappable: a tap on it delivers message to Application::update.on_press_maybe(message: Option<M>)Like on_press, tappable only with "},{"t":"Divider","h":"","u":"widgets/divider.html","s":"Widgets","x":"DividerA line between rows, in the theme's divider colour. MethodWhat it doesnew()A horizontal divider.vertical()A vertical divider, for rows: 1 dp wide, filling the height.thickness(dp: f32)Thickness in dp.inset(dp: f32)Leaves dp clear at the leading end (left of a horizontal divider, top of a vertical one), so the line starts where a row's text does.color(color: impl Into<ColorRef>)Line colour.A horizontal divider "},{"t":"Picture and Avatar","h":"","u":"widgets/picture.html","s":"Widgets","x":"Picture and Avatar"},{"t":"Picture and Avatar","h":"Picture","u":"widgets/picture.html#picture","s":"Widgets","x":"A picture draws an image, a multi-colour vector picture, or a loaded SVG file. MethodWhat it doesnew(image: Image)A picture of image.vector(picture: VectorPicture)A multi-colour vector picture.svg(picture: &SvgPicture)A picture loaded from an SVG filefit(fit: ImageFit)How the image fills a box of a different shape.radius(dp: f32)Corner radius in dp. Half the side of a square picture gives a circle.tint(color: impl In"},{"t":"Picture and Avatar","h":"An SVG file","u":"widgets/picture.html#an-svg-file","s":"Widgets","x":"An SvgAsset is parsed once, the first time it is drawn. The loader accepts a static subset of SVG and refuses anything else with the line it is on; see Text, fonts and pictures."},{"t":"Picture and Avatar","h":"Avatar","u":"widgets/picture.html#avatar","s":"Widgets","x":"A person: an image cropped to a circle, or initials when there is none.MethodWhat it doesnew(name: &str)An avatar for a person called name: \"Amina Juma\" shows \"AJ\".size(dp: f32)Diameter in dp (40 by default).image(image: Image)Shows this image, cropped to the circle, instead of the initials.image_maybe(image: Option<Image>)Like image when there may be none: the initials are the fallback.background(color: impl Into<Co"},{"t":"Icon","h":"","u":"widgets/icon.html","s":"Widgets","x":"IconOne-colour vector artwork that takes its colour where it is drawn. icon(..) is short for a bare Icon::vector(..). MethodWhat it doesnew(glyph: impl Into<Cow<str>>)An icon slot showing glyph (a letter, a digit or a symbol the app's fonts contain), 40 dp square on a rounded tile.vector(icon: VectorIcon)A vector icon, 24 dp square, in the theme's text colour, with no tile behind it.svg(picture: &SvgPicture)An icon f"},{"t":"Icon","h":"Where an icon comes from","u":"widgets/icon.html#where-an-icon-comes-from","s":"Widgets","x":"SourceWritePath data in the sourceVectorIcon::stroked(\"M5 12l5 5L20 7\") or VectorIcon::filled(..), in a 24 by 24 boxWhole SVG markup in the sourceVectorIcon::svg(\"<svg ..>\")An SVG file of the appstatic BACK: SvgAsset = SvgAsset::new(include_str!(\"../assets/svg/back.svg\")); then Icon::svg(BACK.get())A letter or symbol as a stand-inIcon::new(\"?\")An icon says nothing to a screen reader. If it is a control, give the pres"},{"t":"LiveVideo","h":"","u":"widgets/live-video.html","s":"Widgets","x":"LiveVideoThe picture of a camera or a shared screen from a live room. MethodWhat it doesnew(track: VideoTrack)The video drawn by track.fit(fit: ImageFit)How the picture fills a box of a different shape.radius(dp: f32)Corner radius in dp.tint(color: impl Into<ColorRef>)Multiplies every pixel by a colour: white with an alpha of 0.5 shows the picture half transparent.The app draws a track, which it gets from LiveEvent::"},{"t":"Colours, gradients and shadows","h":"","u":"widgets/paint.html","s":"Widgets","x":"Colours, gradients and shadowsWhat a Container can be painted with."},{"t":"Colours, gradients and shadows","h":"Colours","u":"widgets/paint.html#colours","s":"Widgets","x":"A colour is a value or a role of the theme. Give values as the design writes them.WriteForhex(0x2F6BFF)An opaque colour from #RRGGBBhexa(0x2F6BFFCC)A colour with alpha from #RRGGBBAArgb8(47, 107, 255)An opaque colour from bytes, as CSS rgb(..)with_alpha(colour, 0.5)The same colour with another opacityThemeColor::PrimaryA role, which follows the themeColours are composited as sRGB-encoded values, as a browser does, so"},{"t":"Colours, gradients and shadows","h":"Gradients","u":"widgets/paint.html#gradients","s":"Widgets","x":"MethodWhat it doesvertical(top: impl Into<ColorRef>, bottom: impl Into<ColorRef>)From top at the top edge to bottom at the bottom edge.horizontal(left: impl Into<ColorRef>, right: impl Into<ColorRef>)From left at the left edge to right at the right edge.diagonal(top_left: impl Into<ColorRef>, bottom_right: impl Into<ColorRef>)From the top-left corner to the bottom-right corner.angled(degrees: f32, from: impl Into<Col"},{"t":"Colours, gradients and shadows","h":"Borders and corners","u":"widgets/paint.html#borders-and-corners","s":"Widgets","x":"MethodEffect.radius(dp)One radius for the background, border, shadow and clip.radii(tl, tr, br, bl)A radius per corner, for solid and bordered boxes.border(width, colour)A stroke along the inside of the edge.border_dashed(width, colour), .border_dash(length, gap)A dashed stroke.clip()Cuts the children to the box, following its radius"},{"t":"Colours, gradients and shadows","h":"Shadows","u":"widgets/paint.html#shadows","s":"Widgets","x":".shadow(..) takes one of three ready-made shadows or one built from a design's numbers.ShadowForelevation::LOWA hairline lift: list cards on a tinted backgroundelevation::MEDIUMA raised cardelevation::HIGHA floating element: hero cards, sheets, floating buttonsShadow::new(x, y, blur, colour)The numbers a design gives.shadows([..]) puts several on one box, in CSS order, and takes inner (inset) shadows too. ADR 0035."},{"t":"Colours, gradients and shadows","h":"Opacity and layers","u":"widgets/paint.html#opacity-and-layers","s":"Widgets","x":".opacity(value) draws a widget and everything inside it as one group at that opacity, so overlapping children do not show through each other. ADR 0028."},{"t":"Application","h":"","u":"reference/application.html","s":"Reference","x":"ApplicationThe trait an app implements. Only update and the Message type are required. MethodCalledGuidesetupOnce, before the first frameEffects and servicesthemeEvery frameThemes and motionviewEvery frame that is drawnThe application modelview_inInstead of view, with the screen's size, insets and keyboardLayoutupdateFor each messageThe application modelbackThe system's Back button or gestureNavigation and timetickTo"},{"t":"Application","h":"Registering","u":"reference/application.html#registering","s":"Reference","x":"Ui runs the application on the runtime; app! makes the crate the native library the host loads. One per crate."},{"t":"Application","h":"Setup","u":"reference/application.html#setup","s":"Reference","x":"What setup is given: the place to add fonts and images, and what the phone already knows.MethodWhat it doesinstall_id() -> &strAn identifier for this installation of the app: 32 lower-case hexadecimal digits (128 bits).stored(key: &str) -> Option<&str>The value stored under key on an earlier run (see ServiceRequest::Store), as it was when the app started.add_font(data: &[u8])Registers a TrueType/OpenType font from it"},{"t":"Application","h":"Element","u":"reference/application.html#element","s":"Reference","x":"What view returns. Every widget converts into one with .into().MethodWhat it doesmap<N>(convert: impl Fn(M) -> N + Copy + Send + Sync + 'static) -> Element<N>Converts the messages this subtree produces, so a view written for one message type can be embedded in a parent with another"},{"t":"Effects","h":"","u":"reference/effects.html","s":"Reference","x":"EffectsThe queue an application puts its requests on, from Application::effects. Each request that has an answer names the message the answer becomes. Effects and services is the guide."},{"t":"Effects","h":"Every method","u":"reference/effects.html#every-method","s":"Reference","x":"MethodWhat it doeshttp(request: HttpRequest, on_result: impl FnOnce(Result<HttpResponse, HttpError>) -> M + Send + 'static)Makes an HTTP or HTTPS request.start_recording(on_result: impl FnOnce(Result<(), AudioError>) -> M + Send + 'static)Starts recording from the microphone.stop_recording(on_result: impl FnOnce(Result<Recording, AudioError>) -> M + Send + 'static)Stops the recording. The message gets it as the bytes"},{"t":"Effects","h":"HttpRequest","u":"reference/effects.html#httprequest","s":"Reference","x":"MethodWhat it doesnew(method: HttpMethod, url: impl Into<String>)A request with no headers, no body and the default timeout.get(url: impl Into<String>)A GET request.post(url: impl Into<String>)A POST request.put(url: impl Into<String>)A PUT request.delete(url: impl Into<String>)A DELETE request.header(name: impl Into<String>, value: impl Into<String>)Adds a header. Carriage returns and line feeds are removed from the"},{"t":"Effects","h":"HttpResponse","u":"reference/effects.html#httpresponse","s":"Reference","x":"MethodWhat it doesnew(status: u16)A response with this status, no headers and an empty body.with_header(name: impl Into<String>, value: impl Into<String>)Adds a header.with_body(body: impl Into<Vec<u8>>)Sets the body.is_success() -> boolTrue for a status from 200 to 299.header(name: &str) -> Option<&str>The value of the first header called name, compared without regard to case.text() -> Option<&str>The body as text, "},{"t":"Effects","h":"HttpError","u":"reference/effects.html#httperror","s":"Reference","x":"VariantMeaningOfflineThe server could not be reached: no network, the name did not resolve, or the connection was refused or dropped.TimeoutThe exchange took longer than the request's timeout.InvalidUrlThe URL is not an absolute http or https URL.TooLargeThe response body is larger than MAX_HTTP_RESPONSE_BYTES.UnsupportedNothing can make requests here: there is no host (a test without a driver, a screenshot tool) or "},{"t":"Effects","h":"Recording and AudioClip","u":"reference/effects.html#recording-and-audioclip","s":"Reference","x":"MethodWhat it doesinto_clip() -> AudioClipThe recording as something to play.MethodWhat it doesnew(bytes: impl Into<Vec<u8>>, mime: impl Into<String>)A clip from the bytes of an audio file, for example the body of an HttpResponse, and its media type."},{"t":"Effects","h":"AudioError","u":"reference/effects.html#audioerror","s":"Reference","x":"VariantMeaningPermissionDeniedThe user has not allowed the microphone.BusyA recording is already running (for \"start\"), or the microphone or the audio output is held by something that would not give it up.UnsupportedNothing can record or play here: there is no host (a test without a driver, a screenshot tool), the host is older than this library, or the device has no microphone.NotRecording\"Stop recording\" with no re"},{"t":"Effects","h":"LiveJoin","u":"reference/effects.html#livejoin","s":"Reference","x":"MethodWhat it doesnew(url: impl Into<String>, token: impl Into<String>)Joins with the microphone live, sound in the earpiece, and no speaking reports.background(notice: LiveBackground)Lets the room go on while the app is not on screen, under this notice (ADR 0042).camera(on: bool)Whether the camera is on from the start.microphone(live: bool)Whether the microphone is live from the start.speaker(on: bool)Whether sound "},{"t":"Effects","h":"LiveEvent","u":"reference/effects.html#liveevent","s":"Reference","x":"VariantMeaningConnectedThe room is joined: sound from the others plays, and the microphone is heard by them unless the room was joined muted.ParticipantJoinedSomeone is in the room: they were there already, or have come in.ParticipantLeftSomeone left. Their identity.SpeakingWho is speaking now, loudest first, by identity (this device's own person included, under the identity of its ticket); empty when nobody is.Recon"},{"t":"Effects","h":"LiveDisconnect","u":"reference/effects.html#livedisconnect","s":"Reference","x":"VariantMeaningLeftThe application left it.ReplacedThe application joined another room: there is one at a time.HiddenThe app left the screen.HungUpThe user ended it from the notification of a room that was going on in the background (ADR 0042).EndedThe other side ended it: the room was closed, this person was removed, or the same ticket joined from somewhere else.LostThe connection was lost and could not be made again"},{"t":"Effects","h":"LiveError","u":"reference/effects.html#liveerror","s":"Reference","x":"VariantMeaningPermissionDeniedThe user has not allowed it: the microphone for a join, the camera or the screen in LiveEvent::MediaFailed.UnsupportedNothing can join a live room here: there is no host (a test without a driver, a screenshot tool), the host is older than this library, the app was built without the live service (it is opt-in, see ADR 0038), or the platform has none yet (iOS).FailedAnything else, describe"},{"t":"Effects","h":"LiveAudioRoute","u":"reference/effects.html#liveaudioroute","s":"Reference","x":"VariantMeaningEarpieceThe speaker at the ear.SpeakerThe loudspeaker.WiredA headset or headphones on a wire.BluetoothA Bluetooth headset."},{"t":"Ui and testing","h":"","u":"reference/ui.html","s":"Reference","x":"Ui and testingUi runs an Application. In an app it appears once, in morningstar::app!(Ui::new(..)). In a test it is the handle on everything: the app's state, the requests it made, the layout of the last frame, and the semantics tree. Testing is the guide."},{"t":"Ui and testing","h":"Creating one","u":"reference/ui.html#creating-one","s":"Reference","x":"MethodWhat it doesnew(app: A)Wraps an application.testing(app: A)Wraps an application for a test, with nothing behind it: every request it makes of the platform (Application::effects) is held until the test answers it.with_services(app: A, fake: impl FnMut(&ServiceRequest) -> Option<ServiceResult> + Send + 'static)As testing, with a stand-in for the hostUi::new answers every request with Unsupported when no host is a"},{"t":"Ui and testing","h":"The application","u":"reference/ui.html#the-application","s":"Reference","x":"MethodWhat it doesapp() -> &AThe application state.app_mut() -> &mut AThe application state, mutably.send(message: A::Message)Delivers a message as if a widget had been tapped.back() -> boolDelivers the system back gesture or key to the application (Application::back) and returns its answer."},{"t":"Ui and testing","h":"Answering effects","u":"reference/ui.html#answering-effects","s":"Reference","x":"MethodWhat it doespending_effects() -> &[ServiceCall]The requests the application has made that are waiting for the test to answer them, oldest first.effects_in_flight() -> usizeHow many requests the application is still waiting for an answer to, whoever is carrying them out.resolve(id: ServiceId, result: ServiceResult) -> boolAnswers the request with this id: the application gets its message at once.answer_http(resu"},{"t":"Ui and testing","h":"Live rooms","u":"reference/ui.html#live-rooms","s":"Reference","x":"MethodWhat it doeslive_room() -> Option<&LiveRoom>The live room the application has joined, under the test driver: what it joined with (the address and the ticket it used), and whether its microphone and loudspeaker are on now.live_event(event: LiveEvent) -> boolSomething happens in the live room the application has joined: \"the room connected\", \"Baraka came in\", \"the connection was lost\".live_app_hidden() -> boolThe"},{"t":"Ui and testing","h":"Layout and scrolling","u":"reference/ui.html#layout-and-scrolling","s":"Reference","x":"MethodWhat it doesrect_of(id: impl Hash) -> Option<Rect>Where the widget with this explicit .id(..) was laid out in the last frame, in physical pixels, or None if it was not in that frame's tree.visible_rect_of(id: impl Hash) -> Option<Rect>The part of the widget with this explicit .id(..) that the last frame showedscroll_to(to: ScrollTo)Asks for a scroll as Effects::scroll does; carried out in the next frame.set_red"},{"t":"Ui and testing","h":"Focus, selection and the clipboard","u":"reference/ui.html#focus-selection-and-the-clipboard","s":"Reference","x":"MethodWhat it doeshas_focus() -> boolTrue while a TextInput has focus (and so the keyboard is asked for).is_focused(id: impl Hash) -> boolTrue if the TextInput with this explicit .id(..) has focus.caret() -> Option<usize>Where the caret of the focused input is, as a byte offset into its value.composing() -> Option<(usize, usize)>The part of the focused input's value the keyboard is still composing (shown underlined),"},{"t":"Ui and testing","h":"Accessibility","u":"reference/ui.html#accessibility","s":"Reference","x":"MethodWhat it doesset_accessibility(on: bool)Has every frame from now on described for assistive technology (semantics), whatever the host says.semantics() -> &SemanticsTreeThe last frame as a screen reader finds it: the nodes it can land on, in reading order, with their roles, labels, values, states and actions (the crate documentation, \"Accessibility\", has the rules).accessibility_issues() -> Vec<AccessibilityIssue"},{"t":"Ui and testing","h":"HostState","u":"reference/ui.html#hoststate","s":"Reference","x":"What the phone knows before the app starts, for Frame::for_app_with_host.MethodWhat it doesnew()No stored values and the placeholder id NO_INSTALL_ID: what an application sees when there is no host.with_install_id(id: &str)Sets the installation id.with_stored(key: impl Into<String>, value: impl Into<String>)Adds a stored value.install_id() -> &strThe installation id: 32 lower-case hexadecimal digits (128 bits).stored"},{"t":"Navigator, Viewport and ScrollTo","h":"","u":"reference/navigator.html","s":"Reference","x":"Navigator, Viewport and ScrollTo"},{"t":"Navigator, Viewport and ScrollTo","h":"Navigator","u":"reference/navigator.html#navigator","s":"Reference","x":"The stack of screens as plain application state. Navigation and time is the guide.MethodWhat it doesnew(root: S)A stack holding only root, the first screen.current() -> &SThe screen on top: the one to show.current_mut() -> &mut SThe screen on top, mutably (to change the data a screen carries).root() -> &SThe first screen, at the bottom of the stack.stack() -> &[S]Every screen, the first at the bottom, the current one"},{"t":"Navigator, Viewport and ScrollTo","h":"Viewport","u":"reference/navigator.html#viewport","s":"Reference","x":"What Application::view_in is given: the screen the view is drawn on, in dp.MethodWhat it doeswidth() -> f32Width of the surface in dp.height() -> f32Height of the surface in dp.safe_size() -> SizeThe size, in dp, of what is neither under system bars nor under the keyboard: the room a SafeArea at the root gives its child.keyboard_visible() -> boolTrue while the soft keyboard covers part of the surface.has_focus() -> b"},{"t":"Navigator, Viewport and ScrollTo","h":"ScrollTo","u":"reference/navigator.html#scrollto","s":"Reference","x":"A request to move a scroll view, queued with out.scroll(..) and carried out in the next frame, after its layout.MethodWhat it doestop(scroll_view: impl Hash)The top of the scroll view with this .id(..).end(scroll_view: impl Hash)The end of the scroll view with this .id(..): its last content at the bottom of the viewport.offset(scroll_view: impl Hash, dp: f32)dp from the top of the scroll view with this .id(..), kept "},{"t":"Navigator, Viewport and ScrollTo","h":"Transition","u":"reference/navigator.html#transition","s":"Reference","x":"How a widget enters and leaves, for .transition(..).MethodWhat it doesnone()No transition: the widget is simply there, then simply gone.fade()Fades in and out.slide(x: f32, y: f32)Slides in from, and back out to, x widths to the right and y heights below where it belongs (its own width and height; negative for left and above).scale(from: f32)Grows in from, and shrinks out to, from times its size, about its centre, fa"},{"t":"Theme","h":"","u":"reference/theme.html","s":"Reference","x":"ThemeA theme is plain data with public fields. Start from Theme::LIGHT or Theme::DARK and change what the design changes; Themes and motion shows how."},{"t":"Theme","h":"Theme","u":"reference/theme.html#theme","s":"Reference","x":"FieldMeaningcolors: ColorsColour roles.type_scale: TypeScaleText styles.spacing: SpacingSpacing scale.radii: RadiiCorner-radius scale.fonts: FontsFont families.MethodWhat it doeslight()Same as Theme::LIGHT.dark()Same as Theme::DARK.color(token: ThemeColor) -> ColorThe value of a colour token.font(role: TextRole) -> FontFamilyThe font family of a step of the type scaletext(role: TextRole) -> TextSpecThe text style of "},{"t":"Theme","h":"Colors","u":"reference/theme.html#colors","s":"Reference","x":"FieldMeaningbackground: ColorThe screen background; also the frame's clear colour.surface: ColorCards, sheets and bars.surface_variant: ColorInput fields, chips, secondary buttons.primary: ColorBrand or accent colour.on_primary: ColorText on primary.primary_container: ColorA soft tint of primary.text: ColorMain text.text_muted: ColorSecondary text.divider: ColorHairlines and outlines.danger: ColorDestructive actions "},{"t":"Theme","h":"TypeScale","u":"reference/theme.html#typescale","s":"Reference","x":"FieldMeaningdisplay: TextSpecThe one big number or word on a screen (a balance).title: TextSpecScreen titles.headline: TextSpecSection and card headings.body: TextSpecRunning text and list rows.label: TextSpecButtons, tabs, field labels.caption: TextSpecMetadata, timestamps, hints.RoleSizeWeightLine heightdisplay34 dpBold1.2title24 dpBold1.25headline17 dpSemibold1.3body15 dpRegular1.4label14 dpSemibold1.3caption12 dp"},{"t":"Theme","h":"Fonts","u":"reference/theme.html#fonts","s":"Reference","x":"FieldMeaningbody: FontFamilyRunning text, labels and captions.heading: FontFamilyDisplay, title and headline text.MethodWhat it doesnew(body: FontFamily, heading: FontFamily)A body family and a heading family."},{"t":"Theme","h":"Spacing and Radii","u":"reference/theme.html#spacing-and-radii","s":"Reference","x":"SpacingdpRadiidpxs4sm8sm8md12md12lg16lg16xl24xl24full9999xxl32These are values an app may use; widgets take plain numbers, and nothing forces a design onto the scale."},{"t":"Theme","h":"ThemeColor","u":"reference/theme.html#themecolor","s":"Reference","x":"The roles a widget can name instead of a value.VariantMeaningBackgroundThe screen background.SurfaceCards, sheets, bars: surfaces that sit on the background.SurfaceVariantA quieter surface: input fields, chips, secondary buttons.PrimaryThe brand or accent colour.OnPrimaryText and icons drawn on Primary.PrimaryContainerA tint of the primary colour for soft highlights.TextMain text.TextMutedSecondary text: captions, hi"},{"t":"Tools and scripts","h":"","u":"reference/tools.html","s":"Reference","x":"Tools and scriptsEverything is run from the root of the repository, except the morningstar command, which is for apps outside it. The help shown for each tool is the tool's own output, captured when the site was last rebuilt.ToolDoesmorningstarThe released command, for an app outside this repository: create, preview, build for Androidmorningstar-newCreates a new apptools/preview.shRuns an app in a window on a Mac and"},{"t":"Tools and scripts","h":"The morningstar command","u":"reference/tools.html#the-morningstar-command","s":"Reference","x":"For an app outside this repository: cargo install morningstar-cli, then run it in the app's directory. It runs the same code as the tools below, and carries the Android host of its own release. ADR 0045. morningstar new --help: morningstar build android --help:"},{"t":"Tools and scripts","h":"morningstar-new","u":"reference/tools.html#morningstar-new","s":"Reference","x":"Your first app walks through what it writes. New apps go under apps/; the reason is in ADR 0041."},{"t":"Tools and scripts","h":"The live preview","u":"reference/tools.html#the-live-preview","s":"Reference","x":"A development host for a Mac. It drives the same engine the phone hosts drive, in a phone-shaped window, with the mouse as a finger and the keyboard for text fields. KeyDoesTyping, arrows, Cmd+A/C/X/V/ZGo to the focused fieldEscThe system's Back, or \"keyboard away\" while a field is focusedCmd+S or F2Save a screenshotCmd+R or F5Restart the app; with Shift, restart with stored values forgottenF3Toggle reduce motionF4To"},{"t":"Tools and scripts","h":"Screenshots","u":"reference/tools.html#screenshots","s":"Reference","x":"Every app has a small program that renders its screens through the real renderer on the development machine's GPU. The designed examples take screen names, and options to compare with the design (--diff), to render in a phone's frame (--phone, --small, --tall), to render other states (--states, --online) and to print the semantics tree (--semantics). The header of each examples/screenshot.rs lists what that app's pro"},{"t":"Tools and scripts","h":"morningstar-diff","u":"reference/tools.html#morningstar-diff","s":"Reference","x":"Design to code explains how to read its report."},{"t":"Tools and scripts","h":"morningstar-svg","u":"reference/tools.html#morningstar-svg","s":"Reference","x":""},{"t":"Tools and scripts","h":"morningstar-icon","u":"reference/tools.html#morningstar-icon","s":"Reference","x":"The build scripts call it; you run it yourself to look at an icon in every form before shipping it. ADR 0020."},{"t":"Tools and scripts","h":"tools/build-android.sh","u":"reference/tools.html#toolsbuild-androidsh","s":"Reference","x":"SettingMeaningDefaultMORNINGSTAR_APPThe package to buildhello-gpuMORNINGSTAR_ABISArchitectures, separated by spacesarm64-v8a x86_64ANDROID_HOMEThe Android SDKRequiredANDROID_NDK_HOMEThe NDKThe newest complete one under $ANDROID_HOME/ndk--installInstall on the connected phone and start the appBuild onlySee Build for Android."},{"t":"Tools and scripts","h":"tools/build-ios.sh","u":"reference/tools.html#toolsbuild-iossh","s":"Reference","x":"Builds the app as an XCFramework and generates its icon and names for the iOS host. It was written on a machine without Xcode and has never been run to the end. See iOS."},{"t":"Tools and scripts","h":"tools/adb-guarded.sh","u":"reference/tools.html#toolsadb-guardedsh","s":"Reference","x":"A phone connected over adb belongs to a person who may be using it. This script is the only way anything in the repository may send input to a phone or capture its screen. Before every single action it checks that the expected app (MORNINGSTAR_GUARD_PACKAGE) is in front, and refuses otherwise with exit status 3. Use it only when the phone's owner has agreed and is not using the app; prefer proving behaviour from adb "},{"t":"Tools and scripts","h":"The documentation","u":"reference/tools.html#the-documentation","s":"Reference","x":"The pages are Markdown under docs/, listed in docs/site.json. The code samples are regions of examples/docs-snippets, which is compiled and linted with the workspace, and the method tables are read from the source's doc comments. The builder needs only Python 3. ADR 0044."},{"t":"Tools and scripts","h":"Benchmarks","u":"reference/tools.html#benchmarks","s":"Reference","x":"Measures what building and drawing each example screen costs on the development machine. benchmarks/android/measure.sh is the method for a phone. See Performance."},{"t":"Opt-in services","h":"","u":"reference/services.html","s":"Reference","x":"Opt-in servicesSome platform services cost every app that carries them something: megabytes, or a permission in the store listing. Those are opt-in. An app names them in its Cargo.toml, the build script passes the list to the host's build, and an app that does not name a service carries none of it."},{"t":"Opt-in services","h":"What each name adds","u":"reference/services.html#what-each-name-adds","s":"Reference","x":"NameGives the appAdds to the APK(none)HTTP, storage, the clipboard, recording and playbackThe base host: the INTERNET and RECORD_AUDIO permissionsliveLive rooms: voice and data messagesLiveKit's client and WebRTC, about 20 MB. ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE, MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS, WAKE_LOCK, and BLUETOOTH up to Android 11live-videoPictures in a room: the others' cameras and shared screens, and this phone's cameraThe CAMERA per"},{"t":"Opt-in services","h":"Measured","u":"reference/services.html#measured","s":"Reference","x":"The sizes of three debug APKs built for arm64-v8a on 2026-11-17:AppServicesAPKAtlasnone9,952,682 bytesservices-checklive29,865,246 bytesBarua Talkall four34,307,878 bytesAtlas has one native library and no Kotlin or LiveKit classes. The apps differ in more than their services (Barua Talk carries photographs), so read the difference between the first two rows as the cost of live, about 20 MB, and not more precisely th"},{"t":"Opt-in services","h":"Checking an APK","u":"reference/services.html#checking-an-apk","s":"Reference","x":"The rule in the repository is to check by listing the APK, not by reading the build file:"},{"t":"Opt-in services","h":"On iOS","u":"reference/services.html#on-ios","s":"Reference","x":"Nothing here applies yet. The iOS host answers every live room with Unsupported, and has never been compiled.The decisions are ADR 0038, ADR 0039 and ADR 0042."},{"t":"Architecture","h":"","u":"reference/architecture.html","s":"Reference","x":"ArchitectureMorningstar is a workspace of small crates with a one-way dependency direction. An app depends on one of them."},{"t":"Architecture","h":"The crates","u":"reference/architecture.html#the-crates","s":"Reference","x":"CrateOwnsmorningstarThe public API. The only crate an app depends onmorningstar-coreTypes with no dependencies: colours, geometry, the display list, the data of platform services, a video's framesmorningstar-layoutThe layout engine: fixed, hug and fill boxes to rectangles. Depends only on coremorningstar-textFonts, shaping, the layout cache, the glyph atlas, vector icons, SVG. The only crate that uses cosmic-text and"},{"t":"Architecture","h":"The direction","u":"reference/architecture.html#the-direction","s":"Reference","x":"core, gpu, text and runtime never depend on a platform crate and contain no platform-specific code beyond the handle of the surface they draw on. That is why the whole of an app can be built, drawn and tested on a development machine."},{"t":"Architecture","h":"The hosts","u":"reference/architecture.html#the-hosts","s":"Reference","x":"templates/android and templates/ios are native projects with no application logic. They create the surface, forward input, and implement the platform services. The Java or Swift side and its Rust counterpart are two halves of one interface and change together; an app library and a host from different sides of such a change must never be combined."},{"t":"Architecture","h":"An app, at run time","u":"reference/architecture.html#an-app-at-run-time","s":"Reference","x":"The host starts and loads the app's native library.morningstar::app! hands the host an engine holding the app's Ui.The host forwards lifecycle, the surface, touches and the keyboard.The engine produces a frame when one is needed, and nothing otherwise.Requests for platform services go out to the host as data; answers come back and become messages.How rendering works follows one frame through the crates."},{"t":"Architecture","h":"Tools around it","u":"reference/architecture.html#tools-around-it","s":"Reference","x":"PathOwnstools/morningstar-devtoolsScreenshots without a device, the design comparison, the icon generator, the SVG renderer, the new-app generatortools/morningstar-previewThe development host for a Macbenchmarks/hostThe host benchmarkexamples/docs-snippetsThe code samples of these pages"},{"t":"Architecture","h":"Decisions","u":"reference/architecture.html#decisions","s":"Reference","x":"Every architectural decision is recorded: what was decided, why, and what it costs. An accepted record is not edited to change its decision; a new record supersedes it. The decision records page lists all of them, and tracks what has been verified and where.The ones to read first:0001: crate boundaries0003: thin native hosts0013: the declarative UI layer0019: effects and platform services0012: scale, insets and deman"},{"t":"Architecture Decision Records","h":"","u":"architecture/index.html","s":"Reference","x":"Architecture Decision RecordsEach record states one decision, why it was made, and what it costs. An accepted record is not edited to change its decision; a new record supersedes it.#DecisionStatus0001Six crates with a one-way dependency directionAccepted0002wgpu behind a Morningstar-owned GPU crateAccepted0003Thin native hosts, not NativeActivity or a windowing frameworkAccepted0004Dedicated render thread on Android"},{"t":"Architecture Decision Records","h":"Verification status","u":"architecture/index.html#verification-status","s":"Reference","x":"What has actually been run, as of 2026-11-15 (after the first device session, the first parallel wave, transforms and vector pictures on the host, effects and platform services on the host, and live rooms on the host and as built APKs). Update this table when it changes.ClaimStatusEvidenceShared code builds and passes tests on the dev hostVerifiedEvery package's tests, run package by package on 2026-11-15 (cargo test"},{"t":"Architecture Decision Records","h":"Checklist for the owner: TalkBack on CallReady (ADR 0032)","u":"architecture/index.html#checklist-for-the-owner-talkback-on-callready-adr-0032","s":"Reference","x":"Nothing below has been done. Build and install as usual (MORNINGSTAR_APP=callready tools/build-android.sh --install), turn TalkBack on (Settings, Accessibility, TalkBack; or hold both volume keys), and open CallReady. In adb logcat -s Morningstar the line accessibility on: frames are described should appear once, followed by accessibility: semantics update N bytes after frames that changed something and not after one"},{"t":"Platform status","h":"","u":"reference/platform-status.html","s":"Reference","x":"Platform statusWhat has actually been run, and where. \"Verified in tests\" means on the development machine; it is not a claim about a phone. The full table, with the evidence for every row, is kept with the decision records; this page is its summary."},{"t":"Platform status","h":"Android","u":"reference/platform-status.html#android","s":"Reference","x":"Run on one phone: a Galaxy A27, Android 16, Vulkan on an Adreno 710, 120 Hz.Seen on that phoneHowA frame is presentedFirst device sessionScale, insets, edge-to-edge and idling: one frame drawn in six idle secondsFrom the logThe full shader (borders, gradients, shadows, clips, images, text) on VulkanBy eye, against the desktop renderText renders and matches the desktop renderBy eyeTouch reaches the appTaps changed the"},{"t":"Platform status","h":"iOS","u":"reference/platform-status.html#ios","s":"Reference","x":"Never compiled. The shared code type-checks for aarch64-apple-ios. The Swift host, its services, the generated icon and build settings have not been through Xcode, so nothing has run on an iPhone or a simulator.ServiceiOSHTTP, storage, clipboard, recording, playbackWritten in HostServices.swift; unverifiedLive roomsAnswers Unsupported"},{"t":"Platform status","h":"The development machine","u":"reference/platform-status.html#the-development-machine","s":"Reference","x":"WhatStateThe shared code builds and passes its testsVerified on macOSThe renderer's pixel testsVerified on macOS with Metal onlyThe live previewRuns on macOS; driven by script, not yet by handCI on LinuxFormatting, lints, the tests that need no GPU, the Android lints and the servers' tests passed on their first runCI on a hosted Mac (every test, on a GPU)Started by hand only; has not run"},{"t":"Platform status","h":"How this page stays honest","u":"reference/platform-status.html#how-this-page-stays-honest","s":"Reference","x":"The rule in AGENTS.md is to state what was and was not verified on a real device, and the verification table is updated with each change. When this summary and that table disagree, the table is right."},{"t":"Performance","h":"","u":"reference/performance.html","s":"Reference","x":"PerformanceThe project's rule is that nothing is claimed about performance without a measurement. This page states what has been measured and nothing else."},{"t":"Performance","h":"On the development machine","u":"reference/performance.html#on-the-development-machine","s":"Reference","x":"A baseline for noticing regressions. It is not a device measurement and says nothing about a phone.MachineApple M4, 10 cores, 16 GB, macOS, Metal through wgpu 30Build--release, rustc 1.92.0Frame1080 x 2340 px at 2.8125 px per dp, the size of a Galaxy A27's screenScreens32 screens of the example apps, 120 frames each, three runsDate2026-11-17MeasureRun 1Run 2Run 3Building a frame, median screen (ms)0.0380.0340.036Buil"},{"t":"Performance","h":"On a phone","u":"reference/performance.html#on-a-phone","s":"Reference","x":"One early session on a Galaxy A27 (2026-11-11) recorded that a frame is presented at the display's 120 Hz and that an idle screen draws nothing: one frame in six seconds. It is in benchmarks/reports/2026-11-11-galaxy-a27.md.Not measured on any phone: frame time under scrolling or animation, memory, GPU load, battery, the cost of a video frame, and start-up time. The method for measuring is benchmarks/android/measure."},{"t":"Performance","h":"What the design does about it","u":"reference/performance.html#what-the-design-does-about-it","s":"Reference","x":"These are properties of the design, not measurements:Frames are produced on demand; an idle app draws nothing.Text shaping is cached for as long as the same text keeps being shown.The layout tree, hit regions and display list are reused and stop allocating once they reach their working size.A video keeps only its latest frame."},{"t":"Troubleshooting","h":"","u":"reference/troubleshooting.html","s":"Reference","x":"TroubleshootingProblems that have actually come up, and what fixed them."},{"t":"Troubleshooting","h":"The Android build cannot find a complete NDK","u":"reference/troubleshooting.html#the-android-build-cannot-find-a-complete-ndk","s":"Reference","x":"Seen as: Gradle prints NDK at .../ndk/28.2.13676358 did not have a source.properties file, or cargo ndk fails to find a compiler.A partially downloaded NDK has no source.properties. tools/build-android.sh already picks the newest complete one. When calling cargo ndk yourself, point it at a complete NDK:"},{"t":"Troubleshooting","h":"The disk fills up","u":"reference/troubleshooting.html#the-disk-fills-up","s":"Reference","x":"A debug build of every package, the Android cross-build and Gradle's output together take many gigabytes. When a build fails with \"No space left on device\", or with stranger errors such as \"multiple versions of crate morningstar_ui\", free space and rebuild.Everything under target/ and templates/android/app/build can be rebuilt and is safe to delete. To stay small:Build one package: cargo test -p my-app, not --workspa"},{"t":"Troubleshooting","h":"The app on the phone cannot reach the server","u":"reference/troubleshooting.html#the-app-on-the-phone-cannot-reach-the-server","s":"Reference","x":"Seen as: the app says the server did not answer, although the server runs on the development machine.The phone reaches 127.0.0.1 on your machine only through adb reverse, and the link is dropped whenever the cable is unplugged or the phone reconnects: Also check that the server itself is still running: curl http://127.0.0.1:8790/v1/health."},{"t":"Troubleshooting","h":"A request to http:// is refused","u":"reference/troubleshooting.html#a-request-to-http-is-refused","s":"Reference","x":"The hosts refuse plain http:// except to the development machine (localhost, 127.0.0.1, 10.0.2.2). A deployed server needs https://. The preview on a Mac does not refuse it, so this shows up only on a phone."},{"t":"Troubleshooting","h":"Name lookups fail on the development machine","u":"reference/troubleshooting.html#name-lookups-fail-on-the-development-machine","s":"Reference","x":"Seen as: cargo cannot fetch crates, or a server cannot reach its database or an API, while other machines on the same network are fine.This was the router's DNS. Checking against another resolver showed it: A server that starts while the database cannot be resolved keeps running and reconnects by itself; its health endpoint says \"database\": false until then."},{"t":"Troubleshooting","h":"A test of a designed screen fails after a harmless change","u":"reference/troubleshooting.html#a-test-of-a-designed-screen-fails-after-a-harmless-change","s":"Reference","x":"tests/fidelity.rs fails when more than a measured share of pixels differs from the design. Render the screen and look at the difference before touching the limit: For the app's own reference pictures (tests/screens.rs), accept a change you meant with MORNINGSTAR_UPDATE_REFERENCES=1 cargo test -p my-app."},{"t":"Troubleshooting","h":"A box has no size, or text overflows","u":"reference/troubleshooting.html#a-box-has-no-size-or-text-overflows","s":"Reference","x":"A fill_width() child of a box that hugs its width gets no width. Give the parent a width.In a row, the text that should wrap must fill_width().A ScrollView needs a height from its parents.Ui::overflows() lists boxes whose children do not fit. See Layout."},{"t":"Troubleshooting","h":"A text field loses focus when the screen changes","u":"reference/troubleshooting.html#a-text-field-loses-focus-when-the-screen-changes","s":"Reference","x":"Give every TextInput an .id(..). Without one its identity is its position in the tree, which changes when something appears above it."},{"t":"Troubleshooting","h":"The preview window shows the old app","u":"reference/troubleshooting.html#the-preview-window-shows-the-old-app","s":"Reference","x":"A build failed: the old window stays up and the errors are in the terminal that started the preview. Fix the error and save again."},{"t":"Troubleshooting","h":"Long test runs stop when the Mac sleeps","u":"reference/troubleshooting.html#long-test-runs-stop-when-the-mac-sleeps","s":"Reference","x":"Run them under caffeinate:"},{"t":"0001 — Crate boundaries","h":"","u":"architecture/0001-crate-boundaries.html","s":"Reference","x":"0001 — Crate boundariesStatus: Accepted (2026-11-11)"},{"t":"0001 — Crate boundaries","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0001-crate-boundaries.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"The roadmap sketches fourteen crates. Splitting that finely before the subsystems exist would fix boundaries we cannot yet justify, and the roadmap itself says to start with a handful."},{"t":"0001 — Crate boundaries","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0001-crate-boundaries.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":"Start with a handful of crates and one rule about direction: (morningstar-text was added later; see ADR 0009. gpu and text do not depend on each other: they meet through plain data types in core.)morningstar-core: no dependencies. Colour, lifecycle state machine, frame statistics.morningstar-gpu: the only user of wgpu. Device, surface, frame submission.morningstar-runtime: Engine, the single object a platform host dr"},{"t":"0001 — Crate boundaries","h":"Consequences","u":"architecture/0001-crate-boundaries.html#consequences","s":"Reference","x":"The shared crates build and test on a development host with no mobile toolchain.Platform crates cannot be unit-tested on the host; they are kept small enough to review by reading (Android: ~360 lines, iOS: ~190).runtime currently depends on gpu directly. When the display list arrives, runtime should depend on the renderer's interface, not on the GPU backend."},{"t":"0002 — GPU backend: wgpu behind a Morningstar-owned crate","h":"","u":"architecture/0002-gpu-backend-wgpu.html","s":"Reference","x":"0002 — GPU backend: wgpu behind a Morningstar-owned crateStatus: Accepted (2026-11-11)"},{"t":"0002 — GPU backend: wgpu behind a Morningstar-owned crate","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0002-gpu-backend-wgpu.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"Morningstar needs Vulkan on Android and Metal on iOS. Writing and maintaining two native backends is a large cost that buys nothing a user can see."},{"t":"0002 — GPU backend: wgpu behind a Morningstar-owned crate","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0002-gpu-backend-wgpu.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":"Use wgpu (currently 30.x) as the graphics abstraction, with WGSL for shaders, and confine it to morningstar-gpu. No wgpu type appears in that crate's public API: errors are carried as strings, handles as NativeSurface, GPU identity as AdapterInfo.Specific choices in the current implementation:Features: metal, vulkan, gles, wgsl only. GLES stays enabled as the fallback for Android devices with missing or broken Vulkan"},{"t":"0002 — GPU backend: wgpu behind a Morningstar-owned crate","h":"Consequences","u":"architecture/0002-gpu-backend-wgpu.html#consequences","s":"Reference","x":"wgpu's API changes materially between major versions (in 30: Queue::present, the CurrentSurfaceTexture enum, fallible get_mapped_range). Confinement to one crate bounds the cost of each upgrade.wgpu adds roughly 5 MB to the release .so per ABI before any Morningstar rendering code exists. Binary size must be tracked in the benchmark suite.We inherit wgpu's handling of platform details we have not yet validated oursel"},{"t":"0003 — Platform integration: thin native hosts","h":"","u":"architecture/0003-platform-hosts.html","s":"Reference","x":"0003 — Platform integration: thin native hostsStatus: Accepted (2026-11-11). iOS half is unverified on a device."},{"t":"0003 — Platform integration: thin native hosts","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0003-platform-hosts.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"There are three ways to get a Rust renderer onto a phone screen:Let a windowing framework (winit, or Android's NativeActivity/GameActivity) own the application.Drive everything from Rust through bindings (JNI calls into the Android framework; objc2 for UIKit).A small native host that owns the platform view and forwards callbacks to Rust.A mobile app is more than a surface. It needs the soft keyboard and IME compositi"},{"t":"0003 — Platform integration: thin native hosts","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0003-platform-hosts.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":"Option 3. Each platform has a host project under templates/ that contains no application logic and is identical for every app.Android — MorningstarActivity (Java, ~80 lines) owns a SurfaceView and forwards onStart/onStop and the three SurfaceHolder.Callback methods, plus touch events, to seven static native methods. The Rust side obtains an ANativeWindow from the Surface. The app's library name comes from manifest me"},{"t":"0003 — Platform integration: thin native hosts","h":"Consequences","u":"architecture/0003-platform-hosts.html#consequences","s":"Reference","x":"A small amount of Java and Swift exists and must be kept in step with its Rust counterpart. The pairs are named in AGENTS.md.Application developers still write no Kotlin or Swift.The host is the natural place to add an InputConnection, an accessibility node provider and their iOS equivalents later, which is the reason for choosing it.Using objc2 to remove the Swift host remains possible later; nothing here prevents i"},{"t":"0004 — Threading and frame pacing","h":"","u":"architecture/0004-threading-and-frame-pacing.html","s":"Reference","x":"0004 — Threading and frame pacingStatus: Accepted for Android. Open for iOS."},{"t":"0004 — Threading and frame pacing","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0004-threading-and-frame-pacing.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"The roadmap asks for three execution domains (main/UI, workers, render) and warns that the actual arrangement must be validated per platform. It also requires idle rendering for battery life."},{"t":"0004 — Threading and frame pacing","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0004-threading-and-frame-pacing.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":"Engine is single-threaded by contract. It is created on, and only called from, one thread. It contains no locks. Which thread that is belongs to the platform crate.Android: a dedicated render thread. JNI callbacks arrive on the main thread and each posts a message to a channel; the render thread owns the Engine and the ANativeWindow. The main thread never touches the GPU and never blocks, with one required exception "},{"t":"0004 — Threading and frame pacing","h":"Open questions","u":"architecture/0004-threading-and-frame-pacing.html#open-questions","s":"Reference","x":"Rendering every vsync is not idle rendering. Today a visible app redraws continuously even if nothing changed. Demand-driven frames need dirty tracking from the UI engine (ADR 0006) and, on Android, Choreographer for timing instead of blocking in acquire.iOS render thread. Decide with measurements once there is real per-frame work: main-thread hitches versus the complexity of presenting from another thread.High refre"},{"t":"0005 — Application entry point","h":"","u":"architecture/0005-application-entry-point.html","s":"Reference","x":"0005 — Application entry pointStatus: Accepted (2026-11-11)"},{"t":"0005 — Application entry point","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0005-application-entry-point.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"On both platforms the operating system starts the native host, not Rust's main. The host must end up running the developer's application type, and the developer should write one line to make that happen, with no platform cfg."},{"t":"0005 — Application entry point","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0005-application-entry-point.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":"The application crate is the native library: a cdylib on Android (libmy_app.so) and a staticlib on iOS. It depends only on morningstar.morningstar::app!(expr) expands to an exported Rust function, __morningstar_create_app() -> Box<dyn App>. The platform crates declare that symbol as extern \"Rust\" and call it once when the host starts. The platform crates' own entry points (Java_…, morningstar_ios_*) are #[no_mangle] "},{"t":"0005 — Application entry point","h":"Consequences","u":"architecture/0005-application-entry-point.html#consequences","s":"Reference","x":"One source builds for both platforms; verified by symbol inspection of both artifacts.Forgetting app! is a link error, not a runtime failure. Using it twice is a duplicate-symbol error.The roadmap's fn main() { Morningstar::new().run::<Counter>() } is not how mobile apps start. A main-style entry can be offered later for a desktop development host; on mobile it would be decoration.extern \"Rust\" has no stable ABI, whi"},{"t":"0006 — UI architecture direction","h":"","u":"architecture/0006-ui-architecture-direction.html","s":"Reference","x":"0006 — UI architecture directionStatus: Proposed. Implemented so far: the display list with two operations (filled, optionally rounded rectangle; glyph), its GPU renderer, text (ADR 0009), and raw pointer input. Everything else records intent so Phase 2 and 3 work pulls in one direction."},{"t":"0006 — UI architecture direction","h":"Direction","u":"architecture/0006-ui-architecture-direction.html#direction","s":"Reference","x":"The display list is the contract between UI and rendering, and is the first thing to build (Phase 2). It is a flat, ordered list of draw operations (rect, rounded rect, image, glyph run, clip, transform). Frame (ADR 0005) grows from a clear colour into a display list. The renderer can then be developed and benchmarked with hand-written display lists, before any widget exists.Retained tree, declarative API. The develo"},{"t":"0006 — UI architecture direction","h":"Decisions deliberately not made yet","u":"architecture/0006-ui-architecture-direction.html#decisions-deliberately-not-made-yet","s":"Reference","x":"The state model (signals, an Elm-style update loop, or immediate-mode closures). The roadmap's illustrative State<T> snippet has a button callback that borrows &self from build, which a stored callback cannot do; the real design has to resolve ownership first.Layout algorithm: single-pass box constraints, or flexbox via an existing crate.Batching and text atlas strategy. These need measurements on real devices."},{"t":"0006 — UI architecture direction","h":"Acceptance criterion for Phase 2","u":"architecture/0006-ui-architecture-direction.html#acceptance-criterion-for-phase-2","s":"Reference","x":"A hand-written display list with rectangles, an image and a line of text renders identically (by pixel comparison of offscreen output) on Metal and Vulkan, with frame statistics recorded on at least one low-end Android device."},{"t":"0007 — Dependency policy","h":"","u":"architecture/0007-dependency-policy.html","s":"Reference","x":"0007 — Dependency policyStatus: Accepted (2026-11-11)"},{"t":"0007 — Dependency policy","h":"Policy","u":"architecture/0007-dependency-policy.html#policy","s":"Reference","x":"A dependency is added only when writing the equivalent ourselves would be a significant, ongoing cost (a GPU abstraction, text shaping) and not for convenience (logging macros, error derive, a handful of FFI declarations).A dependency that defines a subsystem's behaviour is confined to one crate and kept out of every public API.Default features are off unless needed.Each addition is recorded here with its reason."},{"t":"0007 — Dependency policy","h":"Inventory","u":"architecture/0007-dependency-policy.html#inventory","s":"Reference","x":"CrateVersionUsed byWhywgpu30morningstar-gpuVulkan, Metal and GLES behind one API (ADR 0002)pollster1morningstar-gpuBlocks on wgpu's two async setup calls; avoids an async runtimecosmic-text0.17morningstar-textShaping, bidi, line breaking, font fallback, glyph rasterisation (ADR 0009). fontconfig feature off. 0.17 is what resolves on Rust 1.92; newer releases need a newer compiler.| zeno | 0.3 | morningstar-text | Ras"},{"t":"0007 — Dependency policy","h":"Deliberately absent","u":"architecture/0007-dependency-policy.html#deliberately-absent","s":"Reference","x":"winit, android-activity: would own the application (ADR 0003).jni, ndk, objc2: the hosts need four C functions on Android and none on iOS.An HTTP or TLS stack (reqwest, ureq, rustls), an audio stack (cpal), a random-number crate: the platform has all three, with the system's certificates, codecs and audio policy. Applications reach them through the hosts (ADR 0019).log: wgpu logs through it, so its warnings are curre"},{"t":"0007 — Dependency policy","h":"Toolchain note","u":"architecture/0007-dependency-policy.html#toolchain-note","s":"Reference","x":"rust-toolchain.toml pins the channel but does not list targets. Listing targets makes rustup synchronise the whole channel on any cargo invocation, which silently upgrades a developer's compiler. Targets are added explicitly (rustup target add …; tools/build-ios.sh does this)."},{"t":"0008 — Design-to-code loop","h":"","u":"architecture/0008-design-to-code-loop.html","s":"Reference","x":"0008 — Design-to-code loopStatus: Accepted (2026-11-11). The loop exists; what it can express is limited by what the renderer can draw."},{"t":"0008 — Design-to-code loop","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0008-design-to-code-loop.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"A goal for Morningstar is that a developer, or an AI agent, can be handed a picture of a screen and produce that screen in Rust. Generating plausible UI code from a picture is the easy half. The hard half is knowing whether the result matches, and correcting it. On mobile that normally means build, install, launch, navigate and photograph: minutes per attempt, and not something an agent can do unattended."},{"t":"0008 — Design-to-code loop","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0008-design-to-code-loop.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":"Make rendering an app to an image a first-class, device-free operation.morningstar_devtools::screenshot(app, size, elapsed) runs one frame of any App through the real renderer on the development machine's GPU and returns a PNG. It uses the same display list and the same shader as a phone does. The app can be driven into any state first by calling App::pointer.The intended loop:Read the design image.Write or edit the "},{"t":"0008 — Design-to-code loop","h":"What is still missing for this to be useful on real designs","u":"architecture/0008-design-to-code-loop.html#what-is-still-missing-for-this-to-be-useful-on-real-designs","s":"Reference","x":"In rough order of how much each unlocks:~~Text.~~ Done (ADR 0009), without emoji or mixed styles.~~Layout~~ Done as an engine (ADR 0010); the widget layer that uses it is ADR 0013.Borders, shadows, gradients and clipping: done (ADR 0011). Images: ADR 0014.~~Density-independent units.~~ The scale factor reaches the app (ADR 0012).A widget vocabulary small and regular enough that an agent maps design elements onto it r"},{"t":"0008 — Design-to-code loop","h":"Consequences","u":"architecture/0008-design-to-code-loop.html#consequences","s":"Reference","x":"Visual regression tests come almost free: a screenshot compared with a stored reference.The Metal output on a Mac is not guaranteed identical to Vulkan on a phone, particularly for anti-aliased edges and, later, text. Screenshots prove layout and colour, not device-exact pixels.Screenshot PNGs are compressed (about 165 KB at 1080x2400)."},{"t":"0009 — Text","h":"","u":"architecture/0009-text.html","s":"Reference","x":"0009 — TextStatus: Accepted (2026-11-11). Verified on macOS/Metal and on one Android device (Vulkan)."},{"t":"0009 — Text","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0009-text.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"Text is the largest single piece of a UI framework and the one most often underestimated. Correct text needs shaping (kerning, ligatures, the joined forms of Arabic), bidirectional layout, line breaking, and fallback across fonts. Writing these from scratch is years of work and not where Morningstar's value lies."},{"t":"0009 — Text","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0009-text.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":"A Morningstar-owned text crate, morningstar-text, with cosmic-text doing shaping, bidi, line breaking, fallback and rasterisation behind it. No cosmic-text type appears in the crate's public API (TextContext, TextStyle, TextAlign, TextWeight).Glyphs are display-list operations. DrawOp::Glyph { rect, atlas, color } refers to a region of one single-channel coverage atlas. The same shader and the same instanced draw cal"},{"t":"0009 — Text","h":"Verified","u":"architecture/0009-text.html#verified","s":"Reference","x":"Latin and Arabic layout, wrapping, alignment, weights, caching and eviction: 13 tests in crates/morningstar-text/tests/layout.rs, using Noto Sans and Noto Sans Arabic.Arabic letters take joined forms (joined text measures narrower than the same letters separated by zero-width non-joiners) and right-to-left paragraphs align to the right.Glyph quads sample exactly their atlas region with no bleed, and keep paint order "},{"t":"0009 — Text","h":"Known limitations","u":"architecture/0009-text.html#known-limitations","s":"Reference","x":"No colour glyphs. Emoji are skipped. Needs an RGBA atlas or a second texture.One style per call. No mixed bold/regular or mixed colours within a paragraph.One font family. Every call uses the fonts added in setup, matched by weight; there is no way to name a family yet.Sizes are in physical pixels, like everything else until the hosts pass a scale factor.Whole-atlas upload whenever a new glyph appears (0.5 to 2 MB). "},{"t":"0010 — Layout","h":"","u":"architecture/0010-layout.html","s":"Reference","x":"0010 — LayoutStatus: Accepted (2026-11-11). Implemented in crates/morningstar-layout and verified by host tests only. Nothing consumes it yet; no timings have been taken on any machine."},{"t":"0010 — Layout","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0010-layout.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"ADR 0006 left the layout algorithm open (\"single-pass box constraints, or flexbox via an existing crate\"), and ADR 0008 lists layout as the next thing the design-to-code loop needs, \"so code describes structure rather than pixel arithmetic\".The consumer is a declarative widget layer (Column, Row, Stack, Padding, Text, Button) whose test is whether an AI agent can look at a picture of a screen and reproduce it. That s"},{"t":"0010 — Layout","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0010-layout.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":"Morningstar owns its layout engine; it is not flexbox and does not wrap a crate. morningstar-layout depends only on morningstar-core and reuses its Point, Size and Rect. The engine is about 1800 lines of safe Rust including documentation (#![forbid(unsafe_code)]), roughly 600 of them the algorithm itself. Owning it adds no dependency and keeps the model under our control (AGENTS.md, Architectural Discipline).The mode"},{"t":"0010 — Layout","h":"Rules that are deliberate and may surprise","u":"architecture/0010-layout.html#rules-that-are-deliberate-and-may-surprise","s":"Reference","x":"Fill inside hug collapses. A Fill child adds only its minimum size to a hugging parent's natural size. A hugging row containing only a fill-width text is zero wide. Figma forbids the combination; Flutter asserts; here it is defined and shows up as overflow.Hugging children of a row are each offered everything left by fixed siblings and gaps, independently. An icon, a hugging text and a fixed badge wrap correctly. Two"},{"t":"0010 — Layout","h":"Consequences","u":"architecture/0010-layout.html#consequences","s":"Reference","x":"The widget layer gets a small, regular target: every design element maps to one of five node kinds and one Style.Text costs one measurement when it is Fill or fits, two when a hugging paragraph has to wrap. Widgets should make body text Fill.The whole subtree is recomputed on every compute. There is no dirty tracking; ADR 0006's \"which subtrees re-lay-out\" is not addressed here. Whether it is needed depends on measur"},{"t":"0010 — Layout","h":"Verified","u":"architecture/0010-layout.html#verified","s":"Reference","x":"All on the development host (macOS, cargo test -p morningstar-layout); 77 tests pass, cargo clippy -p morningstar-layout --all-targets -- -D warnings is clean, cargo fmt --check is clean.tests/layout.rs (72 tests), expected rectangles computed by hand: root sizing against the available size; padding, gap, nesting; all six main alignments in rows and columns; all four cross alignments, align_self, stretch limits; fill"},{"t":"0010 — Layout","h":"Known limitations","u":"architecture/0010-layout.html#known-limitations","s":"Reference","x":"No right-to-left. Start is always left. Arabic is a first-class target (ADR 0006), so mirroring rows and alignments is required before this is complete; it should be a per-tree or per-container direction applied at placement, which the algorithm leaves room for.No wrapping containers and no grid. A row never breaks onto a second line. Chip groups and photo grids have to be built from nested rows and columns.No baseli"},{"t":"0011 — Renderer vocabulary: borders, gradients, shadows, clipping, partial atlas upload","h":"","u":"architecture/0011-renderer-vocabulary.html","s":"Reference","x":"0011 — Renderer vocabulary: borders, gradients, shadows, clipping, partial atlas uploadStatus: Accepted (2026-11-11). Verified on macOS/Metal only. Not yet run on Vulkan or GLES, nor on a device."},{"t":"0011 — Renderer vocabulary: borders, gradients, shadows, clipping, partial atlas upload","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0011-renderer-vocabulary.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"The renderer drew two things: filled rounded rectangles and glyphs. Real designs (cards, buttons, lists) also need outlines, gradient fills, drop shadows and clipping. Separately, ADR 0009 recorded that the whole glyph atlas (0.5 to 2 MB) was re-uploaded whenever one glyph was added, the suspected cause of a worse p95 frame interval on the phone.The existing design is one instanced draw call in painter's order (ADR 0"},{"t":"0011 — Renderer vocabulary: borders, gradients, shadows, clipping, partial atlas upload","h":"Decisions","u":"architecture/0011-renderer-vocabulary.html#decisions","s":"Reference","x":"Everything stays in the one draw call. Borders, gradients and shadows are new kinds of the same instanced quad, selected per instance in the fragment shader. Clipping is carried per instance and applied as coverage in the fragment shader, not as scissor state, so a clip does not split the draw call and clipped and unclipped operations keep their paint order.The display list gains five operations (DrawOp is #[non_exha"},{"t":"0011 — Renderer vocabulary: borders, gradients, shadows, clipping, partial atlas upload","h":"Instance layout","u":"architecture/0011-renderer-vocabulary.html#instance-layout","s":"Reference","x":"112 bytes per instance (was 64): seven vec4<f32>, one vertex attribute each.LocationFieldContents0rectx, y, width, height1colorfill; gradient start; shadow or glyph colour (premultiplied)2color2border colour; gradient end (premultiplied)3paramscorner radius, kind, border width or shadow sigma, rounded-clip radius4extraglyph: atlas region; gradient: start and end points5scissorrectangular clip as min and max6clipround"},{"t":"0011 — Renderer vocabulary: borders, gradients, shadows, clipping, partial atlas upload","h":"Shader approach","u":"architecture/0011-renderer-vocabulary.html#shader-approach","s":"Reference","x":"Rectangle and border. One signed distance d to the rounded box. Outer coverage is clamp(0.5 - d); inner coverage is clamp(0.5 - (d + border_width)), which is the exact inset shape, including the inner corner radius r - width and square inner corners once the border is wider than the radius. The pixel is split into fill-only (inner), border-over-fill (outer - inner) and empty, and composited in that one expression. Bo"},{"t":"0011 — Renderer vocabulary: borders, gradients, shadows, clipping, partial atlas upload","h":"Partial atlas upload","u":"architecture/0011-renderer-vocabulary.html#partial-atlas-upload","s":"Reference","x":"morningstar-text's atlas keeps a dirty row range and the generation it is relative to. TextContext::begin_frame starts a new record (Atlas::mark_clean), since by then the renderer has had the chance to upload the previous frame. Growing or clearing the atlas marks the whole image as replaced (dirty: None) until the next frame.Generations now come from one process-wide counter, so a generation identifies one state of "},{"t":"0011 — Renderer vocabulary: borders, gradients, shadows, clipping, partial atlas upload","h":"Verified","u":"architecture/0011-renderer-vocabulary.html#verified","s":"Reference","x":"All on macOS/Metal, on the real GPU. 29 + 4 + 42 + 6 + 15 tests pass in morningstar-core, morningstar-gpu (unit, then offscreen.rs 11, vocabulary.rs 26, atlas_upload.rs 5) and morningstar-text (unit 6, layout.rs 15).The 11 existing pixel tests pass with only the new dirty: None field added to their two AtlasView literals; the 13 existing text tests pass unchanged.Borders: exact pixels for aligned strokes and fill-plu"},{"t":"0011 — Renderer vocabulary: borders, gradients, shadows, clipping, partial atlas upload","h":"Known limitations","u":"architecture/0011-renderer-vocabulary.html#known-limitations","s":"Reference","x":"Two gradient stops, linear only. No multi-stop, radial or conic gradients.Gradient with a border is two operations (fill_gradient_rect, then stroke_rounded_rect), because an instance has two colours. Drawn that way the fill can show faintly through the anti-aliased outer edge of rounded corners.Gradients are not sRGB-interpolated, so they do not match CSS or design tools exactly (see above).No dithering. Wide gradien"},{"t":"0011 — Renderer vocabulary: borders, gradients, shadows, clipping, partial atlas upload","h":"What an image operation would need","u":"architecture/0011-renderer-vocabulary.html#what-an-image-operation-would-need","s":"Reference","x":"Images were deliberately left out. They need:Texture resource management, which does not exist: handles in morningstar-core (plain IDs, like AtlasView is plain data), creation, upload and eviction in morningstar-gpu, decoding somewhere that is not the render thread, and a lifetime rule across surface loss (the device survives; textures should too).A way to bind more than one texture. With one draw call there is one b"},{"t":"0012 — Display scale, safe-area insets and demand-driven rendering","h":"","u":"architecture/0012-scale-insets-and-demand-rendering.html","s":"Reference","x":"0012 — Display scale, safe-area insets and demand-driven renderingStatus: Accepted (2026-11-11). Verified on the development host only: not yet run on an Android device, and the Swift has never been compiled.Supersedes the open question \"Rendering every vsync is not idle rendering\" of ADR 0004, and its statement that a visible app draws at every refresh. Everything else in 0004 stands. Resolves the limitation \"sizes "},{"t":"0012 — Display scale, safe-area insets and demand-driven rendering","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0012-scale-insets-and-demand-rendering.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"Three things a real application needs were missing from the host interface:Scale. Designs are specified in density-independent units (dp on Android, points on iOS). The app only knew physical pixels, so the example sized everything as a fraction of the screen, which makes a button physically larger on a tablet than on a phone.Safe area. The surface covers the whole window. The status bar, a display cutout, the naviga"},{"t":"0012 — Display scale, safe-area insets and demand-driven rendering","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0012-scale-insets-and-demand-rendering.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":""},{"t":"0012 — Display scale, safe-area insets and demand-driven rendering","h":"The display list stays in physical pixels","u":"architecture/0012-scale-insets-and-demand-rendering.html#the-display-list-stays-in-physical-pixels","s":"Reference","x":"Scale is information handed to the app, not a transform applied by the renderer. FrameInfo gains scale: f32 (physical pixels per dp) with the helpers dp(length), logical_size() and safe_area(). An app, and the widget layer that will be built on App, works in dp and multiplies by the scale when it emits drawing operations and when it reads pointer positions, which also stay in pixels.Reasons: the renderer, the text cr"},{"t":"0012 — Display scale, safe-area insets and demand-driven rendering","h":"Insets are per-edge, in physical pixels, and the surface is edge to edge","u":"architecture/0012-scale-insets-and-demand-rendering.html#insets-are-per-edge-in-physical-pixels-and-the-surface-is-edge-to-edge","s":"Reference","x":"FrameInfo.insets is an Insets { left, top, right, bottom }, a new plain type in morningstar-core next to Rect. It is the union of the system bars and the display cutout on Android (WindowInsets), and safeAreaInsets on iOS. The soft keyboard is deliberately not part of it: it is a different concern with different behaviour, and will be reported separately when text input exists.The app paints its background over the w"},{"t":"0012 — Display scale, safe-area insets and demand-driven rendering","h":"Scale and insets are engine state, set by the host, and can change at any time","u":"architecture/0012-scale-insets-and-demand-rendering.html#scale-and-insets-are-engine-state-set-by-the-host-and-can-change-at-any-time","s":"Reference","x":"Engine::set_scale and Engine::set_insets may be called at any point and as often as the host likes; each returns whether the value changed. A change produces a frame. Hosts therefore resend on every event that might have changed them (configuration change, layout, surface resize) without tracking the previous value.During a rotation the new size and the new insets arrive as separate calls, so one frame can combine th"},{"t":"0012 — Display scale, safe-area insets and demand-driven rendering","h":"Frames are produced on demand","u":"architecture/0012-scale-insets-and-demand-rendering.html#frames-are-produced-on-demand","s":"Reference","x":"The engine holds one flag: a frame is wanted. Engine::needs_frame() is that flag and visibility together. The flag is set bybecoming visible, and a surface being created or resized;a scale or inset change;every pointer event;the app calling frame.request_redraw() while building a frame;the host calling Engine::request_redraw() (Android forwards surfaceRedrawNeeded);a frame that did not reach the display (skipped by t"},{"t":"0012 — Display scale, safe-area insets and demand-driven rendering","h":"How each host idles","u":"architecture/0012-scale-insets-and-demand-rendering.html#how-each-host-idles","s":"Reference","x":"Android. The render thread's loop asks needs_frame(). If true it drains pending messages without blocking and calls frame(); vsync pacing is still the blocking acquire of the next drawable, so Choreographer was not needed. If false it blocks in recv() on its message channel. Only a message from the main thread can make a frame wanted, so nothing is missed by sleeping, and a blocked thread uses no CPU. This is the sam"},{"t":"0012 — Display scale, safe-area insets and demand-driven rendering","h":"Statistics describe frames, not waiting","u":"architecture/0012-scale-insets-and-demand-rendering.html#statistics-describe-frames-not-waiting","s":"Reference","x":"The frame interval is recorded only between two frames that were produced back to back: the earlier one asked for the later one (an animation, or continuous mode). A frame produced after an idle period has no interval, since the time since the previous frame says nothing about how fast it was. CPU time and present time are recorded for every frame. EngineReport.interval is therefore optional.A consequence: frames dri"},{"t":"0012 — Display scale, safe-area insets and demand-driven rendering","h":"Continuous mode, for benchmarks only","u":"architecture/0012-scale-insets-and-demand-rendering.html#continuous-mode-for-benchmarks-only","s":"Reference","x":"A steady stream of frames is still needed to measure frame pacing. Engine::set_continuous(true) makes every frame ask for another. Hosts expose it as a launch switch (--ez dev.morningstar.continuous true on Android, the -morningstar-continuous launch argument on iOS); benchmarks/android/measure.sh uses it for its frame statistics and measures idle behaviour separately. It is not an API for applications."},{"t":"0012 — Display scale, safe-area insets and demand-driven rendering","h":"Consequences","u":"architecture/0012-scale-insets-and-demand-rendering.html#consequences","s":"Reference","x":"FrameInfo is #[non_exhaustive] and built with FrameInfo::new(size) and with_* methods, so adding a field no longer breaks every test and tool that constructs one.The host interfaces grew: four JNI functions (nativeSetScale, nativeSetInsets, nativeRequestRedraw, nativeSetContinuous) and four C functions (morningstar_ios_set_scale, _set_insets, _needs_frame, _set_continuous). ADR 0003's counts of seven and eight are ou"},{"t":"0012 — Display scale, safe-area insets and demand-driven rendering","h":"Verified","u":"architecture/0012-scale-insets-and-demand-rendering.html#verified","s":"Reference","x":"Scheduling, scale and inset propagation, and statistics: 28 unit tests in crates/morningstar-runtime/src/engine.rs against a fake presenter, on the development host.The example lays out in dp, stays inside the safe area across ten screen configurations, and asks for frames only during its tap animation: 25 tests in examples/hello-gpu, and screenshots (portrait and landscape, safe area outlined) inspected by eye.The A"},{"t":"0012 — Display scale, safe-area insets and demand-driven rendering","h":"Not verified","u":"architecture/0012-scale-insets-and-demand-rendering.html#not-verified","s":"Reference","x":"Anything on a device. The phone was disconnected before this change could be installed, so scale and inset values, edge-to-edge layout and idling on real hardware are unproven. The idle log lines described above exist so that this can be checked without looking at the screen: after launch, expect scale, insets, then two idle lines with the same frames= count.All Swift (templates/ios/Sources/HostViewController.swift):"},{"t":"0012 — Display scale, safe-area insets and demand-driven rendering","h":"Known gaps","u":"architecture/0012-scale-insets-and-demand-rendering.html#known-gaps","s":"Reference","x":"A frame the renderer presents on a suboptimal swapchain (for example after a 180-degree rotation, which changes no size) used to be corrected by the next vsync's frame. Now there may be no next frame, so the compositor keeps transforming that one until something else causes a redraw. The picture is right; the composition is not the cheapest. The renderer should report this so the engine can ask for one more frame.mor"},{"t":"0013 — The declarative UI layer","h":"","u":"architecture/0013-ui-layer.html","s":"Reference","x":"0013 — The declarative UI layerStatus: Accepted (2026-11-11). Implemented in crates/morningstar-ui, re-exported as morningstar::ui. Verified on the development host only (macOS, Metal, offscreen): never run on a device.Resolves the state-model question left open in ADR 0006, and is the \"widget vocabulary\" of ADR 0008, item 5. It does not implement 0006's retained tree with reconciliation and dirty tracking; see \"What"},{"t":"0013 — The declarative UI layer","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0013-ui-layer.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"Applications painted a display list by hand and hit-tested raw pointer events (examples/hello-gpu). Everything needed for something better existed underneath: the renderer vocabulary (0011), text (0009), layout (0010), scale, insets and demand-driven frames (0012), images (0014).The product goal sets the requirements: a developer or an AI agent is handed a picture of a screen and must reproduce it quickly and predict"},{"t":"0013 — The declarative UI layer","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0013-ui-layer.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":""},{"t":"0013 — The declarative UI layer","h":"An Elm-style application","u":"architecture/0013-ui-layer.html#an-elm-style-application","s":"Reference","x":"Widgets carry message values (Button::new(\"Send\").on_press(Message::Send)), not closures. This is what resolves the ownership problem recorded in 0006: view borrows &self and returns plain data; nothing in the tree borrows the state, so nothing needs Rc, RefCell or a lifetime parameter, and Element<M> has no lifetime. Signals and immediate-mode closures were the alternatives; both put borrowed or shared state inside "},{"t":"0013 — The declarative UI layer","h":"One box type, a handful of leaves","u":"architecture/0013-ui-layer.html#one-box-type-a-handful-of-leaves","s":"Reference","x":"Container<M> is the only widget with children, padding and decoration; it is a frame with auto-layout. Column, Row, Stack, ScrollView, SafeArea, Pressable, Spacer and the macros col!, row!, stack! are presets of it, so every modifier works on all of them. Leaves: Text, Picture (an image). Composites that lower to those: Button, Divider, Avatar, Icon.Rejected: a Widget trait with boxed trait objects (an allocation per"},{"t":"0013 — The declarative UI layer","h":"Units are dp, everywhere","u":"architecture/0013-ui-layer.html#units-are-dp-everywhere","s":"Reference","x":"Every length in the API, font sizes included, is in dp. Layout runs in dp on the unit-agnostic layout engine; text is measured in physical pixels (dp × scale) and reported back in dp; each rectangle's edges are rounded to device pixels on the way out, so siblings that share an edge in dp share it in pixels at any scale, and anything with a size keeps at least one pixel. Border widths round to whole pixels (minimum on"},{"t":"0013 — The declarative UI layer","h":"Theme tokens resolved at paint time","u":"architecture/0013-ui-layer.html#theme-tokens-resolved-at-paint-time","s":"Reference","x":"Theme { colors, type_scale, spacing, radii } is plain Copy data with Theme::LIGHT and Theme::DARK. Every colour parameter is impl Into<ColorRef>: a literal Color or a ThemeColor token. Tokens, and Text roles (.title(), .caption(), …), are stored in the tree and resolved against Application::theme() when the frame is painted. That is why view needs no theme parameter and built-in widgets follow the theme. Spacing and "},{"t":"0013 — The declarative UI layer","h":"The frame pipeline","u":"architecture/0013-ui-layer.html#the-frame-pipeline","s":"Reference","x":"Ui<A> implements the runtime's App. Per frame:theme(), view().Lower the element tree into the reused LayoutTree (cleared, not reallocated). The root is forced to fill the surface. SafeArea adds the insets (converted to dp) to a box's padding. A Spacer takes its axis from its parent.Compute in dp. The measure callback calls Frame::measure_text.Paint: walk the element tree again in the same order, emitting display-list"},{"t":"0013 — The declarative UI layer","h":"Input","u":"architecture/0013-ui-layer.html#input","s":"Reference","x":"Messages are delivered in App::pointer, immediately, in event order. Rules:A tap is a finger down and up on the same pressable. Sliding off cancels; sliding back before lifting re-arms; a system cancel abandons.One gesture at a time. Fingers that land while one is in progress are ignored entirely.The innermost pressable under the finger wins. block_touches() makes a box swallow touches (a scrim).Inside a ScrollView, "},{"t":"0013 — The declarative UI layer","h":"Scrolling","u":"architecture/0013-ui-layer.html#scrolling","s":"Reference","x":"Vertical only. The content is laid out at its natural height (the layout engine lets height overflow, as 0010 anticipated) and painted translated and clipped. Subtrees more than 96 dp outside the viewport are skipped at paint time, so a long list costs layout for all rows but drawing for the visible ones.Fling: exponential decay with a 0.325 s time constant, integrated in closed form over each frame's elapsed step, s"},{"t":"0013 — The declarative UI layer","h":"The identity rule","u":"architecture/0013-ui-layer.html#the-identity-rule","s":"Reference","x":"The tree returned by view is discarded after every frame. Two pieces of state outlive it, held in Ui and keyed by widget identity: which pressable is held, and each scroll view's position and motion.Identity is position: the chain of child indices from the root..id(value) replaces it with the hash of value. Ids are global to the screen, not scoped by the parent; two live widgets must not share one. Descendants of a w"},{"t":"0013 — The declarative UI layer","h":"What allocates","u":"architecture/0013-ui-layer.html#what-allocates","s":"Reference","x":"Per rebuild:the element tree: one Vec per box that has children, plus the Strings the application formats (Text takes Cow<'static, str>; a literal is not copied);one Vec of text references built during lowering.Reused across frames, allocation-free once at working size: the layout tree, the node records, text metadata, touch regions, the message table, the scroll-state map, the display list. Shaping is cached by the "},{"t":"0013 — The declarative UI layer","h":"What this is not","u":"architecture/0013-ui-layer.html#what-this-is-not","s":"Reference","x":"Not retained. ADR 0006 sketched a retained tree with reconciliation and three levels of dirty tracking. This layer rebuilds, re-lays-out and repaints the whole screen for each frame. Frames are demand-driven (0012), so the cost is paid per input event or animation frame, not per vsync. Whether that is fast enough on a low-end phone is unmeasured. The public API (state, messages, view) does not preclude diffing undern"},{"t":"0013 — The declarative UI layer","h":"Limitations","u":"architecture/0013-ui-layer.html#limitations","s":"Reference","x":"No text input, focus or keyboard.No animation API. The only motion is fling. No transitions, no animated pressed state.Scrolling: vertical only; no overscroll; no scroll bars; no programmatic scroll-to; nested scroll views do not hand off (the innermost takes the drag); all rows are laid out and measured even when off screen (no virtualised list).Culling assumes containment: a descendant that overflows its ancestor b"},{"t":"0013 — The declarative UI layer","h":"Gaps found in lower layers, and the guards kept here","u":"architecture/0013-ui-layer.html#gaps-found-in-lower-layers-and-the-guards-kept-here","s":"Reference","x":"These are worked around in morningstar-ui and should be fixed where they belong:Huge font sizes panic in the text engine. A font size around 1e9 px overflows glyph positions inside cosmic-text (glyph_cache.rs, \"attempt to add with overflow\"). Guard here: font size is capped at 2048 px (MAX_FONT_PIXELS). Fix: clamp font_size in TextContext::shape (crates/morningstar-text/src/context.rs).Huge text origins can overflow."},{"t":"0013 — The declarative UI layer","h":"ADR 0010's unchecked assumption","u":"architecture/0013-ui-layer.html#adr-0010s-unchecked-assumption","s":"Reference","x":"0010 assumes that text reporting natural width w reports the same size under any constraint of at least w, and notes it was never checked against the real text engine. It holds: text_measured_at_its_natural_width_does_not_wrap measures seven strings (Latin, Swahili, Arabic) at 311 sizes each, at their natural width, its ceiling and one pixel more, and none wraps.The remaining hazard is arithmetic, not text: dp round-"},{"t":"0013 — The declarative UI layer","h":"Verified","u":"architecture/0013-ui-layer.html#verified","s":"Reference","x":"All on macOS; GPU tests on Metal through the offscreen renderer.crates/morningstar-ui: 2 unit tests, 48 integration tests (tests/ui.rs), 7 doctests. Layout in dp and px at seven scales, shared edges, safe area, spacers, stack anchors, overflow reporting; text sizing, wrapping, max_lines, centring; pictures (sizing, the emitted operation, avatar fallback); tap, slide-off and re-arm, cancel, second finger, message orde"},{"t":"0013 — The declarative UI layer","h":"Not verified","u":"architecture/0013-ui-layer.html#not-verified","s":"Reference","x":"Anything on a device, on Vulkan or GLES, or on iOS.Performance: no timings, no allocation counts.Touch feel. Slop, decay and velocity sampling are reasoned values exercised only by synthetic events."},{"t":"0014 — Images","h":"","u":"architecture/0014-images.html","s":"Reference","x":"0014 — ImagesStatus: Accepted (2026-11-11). Verified on macOS/Metal only. Not yet run on Vulkan or GLES, nor on a device.Builds on ADR 0011, which listed what an image operation would need, and resolves the suboptimal swapchain risk recorded in ADR 0012."},{"t":"0014 — Images","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0014-images.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"The renderer drew rounded rectangles (solid, bordered, gradient), shadows, clips and glyphs. Real designs also need photos, avatars, logos and icons. ADR 0011 named the missing pieces: texture resources with a lifetime rule, a way to bind more than one texture, an RGBA sampling kind in the shader, and filtering with correct colour.Constraints carried over from earlier decisions: no wgpu type in a public API (0002); p"},{"t":"0014 — Images","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0014-images.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":""},{"t":"0014 — Images","h":"Resources: a store of pixels, a view for the renderer, textures created on first draw","u":"architecture/0014-images.html#resources-a-store-of-pixels-a-view-for-the-renderer-textures-created-on-first-draw","s":"Reference","x":"morningstar-core gains ImageStore, which owns the pixels of every registered image, and Image, a 16-byte Copy handle carrying an ImageId and the image's width and height. Ids come from one process-wide counter and are never reused, so a handle that outlives its image names nothing rather than some other image.An app registers images through Setup::add_image_rgba8 / add_image_png in App::setup, or the same two methods"},{"t":"0014 — Images","h":"Drawing: one new operation","u":"architecture/0014-images.html#drawing-one-new-operation","s":"Reference","x":"DrawOp::Image { image, rect, source, corner_radius, tint } draws the region source of an image (in the image's own pixels) into rect, cut to rounded corners, multiplied by tint. DisplayList has three ways to record it:MethodUsedraw_image(image, rect)the whole image, stretcheddraw_image_fit(image, bounds, fit, corner_radius, tint)keep the aspect ratio: ImageFit::Cover, Contain, or Stretchdraw_image_region(image, sourc"},{"t":"0014 — Images","h":"GPU design: one texture per image, draw calls split where the image changes","u":"architecture/0014-images.html#gpu-design-one-texture-per-image-draw-calls-split-where-the-image-changes","s":"Reference","x":"Three designs were considered.A. One texture per image; the instance stream is cut into runs (chosen). All instances stay in one buffer in paint order. A run ends only where an image instance needs a different image texture than the one the current run has bound. Rectangles, shadows and glyphs sample no image, so they join whatever run is open and never cause a split. Each run is one draw call with its own bind group"},{"t":"0014 — Images","h":"Instance layout: unchanged, 112 bytes","u":"architecture/0014-images.html#instance-layout-unchanged-112-bytes","s":"Reference","x":"An image needs nothing a rectangle's instance does not already have room for:LocationFieldImage meaning1colortint (premultiplied)2color2the texture coordinates sampling is confined to: min and max3params.zmip level4extrasource region in texture coordinates: x, y, width, heightKind 4 is the image. The instance is 112 bytes before and after; rectangles and glyphs carry nothing new. The vertex-to-fragment interface is s"},{"t":"0014 — Images","h":"Filtering and mipmaps","u":"architecture/0014-images.html#filtering-and-mipmaps","s":"Reference","x":"The sampler is bilinear within a level and linear between levels, clamped at the edges.Every image texture has a full mip chain, generated on the CPU when it is uploaded: each level is a box filter of the one before, averaged in linear light on premultiplied values. This is the same result on every backend, needs no render-target support for the texture format and no second pipeline, and was measured as cheap (below)"},{"t":"0014 — Images","h":"Colour","u":"architecture/0014-images.html#colour","s":"Reference","x":"A PNG holds sRGB-encoded colour with straight alpha. The pipeline blends linear colour with premultiplied alpha. Two conversions are needed, and their order matters.Premultiply in linear light, at registration. Each colour byte is decoded to linear, multiplied by alpha, and encoded back to an sRGB byte. Opaque pixels are untouched. Fully transparent pixels become transparent black, so whatever colour they carried (ex"},{"t":"0014 — Images","h":"Limits","u":"architecture/0014-images.html#limits","s":"Reference","x":"Stored size: longest side at most 2048 pixels (MAX_IMAGE_DIMENSION), the downlevel_defaults texture limit and so the largest texture every target device can hold. A larger image is scaled down at registration, with a box filter in linear light, keeping its aspect ratio, so that its longer side is exactly 2048. The handle still reports the original width and height and source rectangles stay in original pixels, so app"},{"t":"0014 — Images","h":"Decoding: png, in the runtime, behind the Morningstar API","u":"architecture/0014-images.html#decoding-png-in-the-runtime-behind-the-morningstar-api","s":"Reference","x":"Frame::add_image_png and Setup::add_image_png decode with the png crate in morningstar-runtime/src/image.rs, the only place that names it. All PNG colour types and bit depths are accepted and normalised to RGBA8 (palettes and transparency chunks expanded, grey to RGB, 16-bit reduced to 8); only the first frame of an animated PNG is read. Malformed, truncated or non-PNG data is ImageError::Decode carrying the decoder'"},{"t":"0014 — Images","h":"Dependency","u":"architecture/0014-images.html#dependency","s":"Reference","x":"CrateVersionUsed byWhypng0.18morningstar-runtimePNG decoding for add_image_png. Inflate and the PNG filters are not worth owning.This is the first image dependency linked into apps; until now png was only in the development tool. It has no optional default features to turn off. It brings bitflags, crc32fast, fdeflate, flate2 and miniz_oxide (with simd-adler32, adler2, cfg-if, zlib-rs), all already in Cargo.lock throu"},{"t":"0014 — Images","h":"Demand-driven rendering","u":"architecture/0014-images.html#demand-driven-rendering","s":"Reference","x":"Images are registered and released only through Setup (before the first frame) and Frame (while a frame is being built). The frame being built carries the changed image set to the renderer when it is presented, so a registration or release never needs a frame of its own: an image registered and drawn in one App::frame is on screen with that frame, and a released image's texture is freed by that frame's render. If tha"},{"t":"0014 — Images","h":"Suboptimal swapchain (ADR 0012, Risks)","u":"architecture/0014-images.html#suboptimal-swapchain-adr-0012-risks","s":"Reference","x":"A frame acquired from a suboptimal swapchain was presented and the swapchain reconfigured \"for the next frame\", which demand-driven rendering no longer guarantees.Renderer::take_frame_request() (new) returns true, once, after a presented frame that was drawn into a suboptimal swapchain. The request is made once per unbroken run of suboptimal frames: a swapchain that reports suboptimal on every frame cannot keep the a"},{"t":"0014 — Images","h":"Public API added","u":"architecture/0014-images.html#public-api-added","s":"Reference","x":"morningstar-core: Image, ImageId, ImageStore, ImagesView, ImagePixels, ImageError, ImageFit, ImagePlacement, MAX_IMAGE_DIMENSION, MAX_IMAGE_BYTES; DrawOp::Image; DisplayList::{draw_image, draw_image_fit, draw_image_region}.morningstar-gpu: Renderer::{render_with_images, last_stats, take_frame_request}, OffscreenRenderer::{render_with_images, last_stats}, render_offscreen_with_images, RenderStats { instances, draw_cal"},{"t":"0014 — Images","h":"Verified","u":"architecture/0014-images.html#verified","s":"Reference","x":"All on macOS/Metal, on the real GPU, in debug builds.Registration and release (morningstar-core, 13 tests; morningstar-gpu/tests/images.rs; morningstar-runtime): ids never reused, across stores too; double release harmless; textures created on first draw only, not for registered-but-undrawn or fully clipped images; a released image's texture freed on the next frame, including a frame that draws nothing; drawing a sta"},{"t":"0014 — Images","h":"Not verified","u":"architecture/0014-images.html#not-verified","s":"Reference","x":"Vulkan and GLES. The shader change (a second bind group, a texture sampled inside a branch at an explicit level) and per-run vertex-buffer slices passed wgpu's validation on Metal only. sRGB texture formats, non-power-of-two textures with full mip chains and explicit-level sampling are all core in GLES 3.0, which is the reason for the choices above, but none of it has run there.On a device. Nothing here has run on An"},{"t":"0014 — Images","h":"Known limitations","u":"architecture/0014-images.html#known-limitations","s":"Reference","x":"Pixels are held twice, in the store and on the GPU (where mipmaps add a third more). A 2048 x 2048 image is 16 MB in the store and 21 MB of texture. Dropping the CPU copy after upload would need the renderer to tell the store, and a way to recover after texture loss; neither exists yet.No texture eviction. A texture lives until its image is released, even if it has not been drawn for a long time. Apps showing many ph"},{"t":"0015 — Font families, vector icons, letter spacing, dashed borders, and what a real design measured","h":"","u":"architecture/0015-fonts-icons-and-design-fidelity.html","s":"Reference","x":"0015 — Font families, vector icons, letter spacing, dashed borders, and what a real design measuredStatus: Accepted (2026-11-11). Verified on the development host only (macOS, Metal, offscreen). Nothing here has run on a device, on Vulkan or on GLES.Extends 0009 (text), 0011 (renderer vocabulary) and 0013 (UI layer). It removes three limitations those records list: \"one font family\", \"no vector icons\" and \"no dashes\""},{"t":"0015 — Font families, vector icons, letter spacing, dashed borders, and what a real design measured","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0015-fonts-icons-and-design-fidelity.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"The framework's purpose is to turn a design into Rust that renders the same screen. The first real test was FundiDesk (examples/fundidesk): twelve screens exported from a design tool as HTML, with a Chrome rendering of each as the reference. Surveying the twelve files before writing any screen showed what the UI layer could not say:two typefaces (Inter for text, Space Grotesk for headings and figures), where text cou"},{"t":"0015 — Font families, vector icons, letter spacing, dashed borders, and what a real design measured","h":"Decisions","u":"architecture/0015-fonts-icons-and-design-fidelity.html#decisions","s":"Reference","x":""},{"t":"0015 — Font families, vector icons, letter spacing, dashed borders, and what a real design measured","h":"Font families are named; the weight is matched within the family","u":"architecture/0015-fonts-icons-and-design-fidelity.html#font-families-are-named-the-weight-is-matched-within-the-family","s":"Reference","x":"TextStyle gains family: FontFamily (Default or Named(&'static str)) and the layout cache key includes it. A named family is handed to the shaper as such; Default keeps the previous behaviour exactly (the fonts in the order added), so existing applications and tests are unaffected. Names are matched as the font file names itself, ignoring case; the typographic family name is used, so Inter-SemiBold.ttf is \"Inter\" at w"},{"t":"0015 — Font families, vector icons, letter spacing, dashed borders, and what a real design measured","h":"Rich text: spans within one paragraph","u":"architecture/0015-fonts-icons-and-design-fidelity.html#rich-text-spans-within-one-paragraph","s":"Reference","x":"TextContext::draw_spans / measure_spans take &[TextSpan], each span optionally overriding weight, colour and family; size, line height, alignment and letter spacing belong to the paragraph. The spans are shaped as one text, so a paragraph wraps inside a span as it would anywhere. Per-span colour is stored per glyph in the cached layout and is part of the cache key. The UI has Text::rich([span(\"..\").semibold().color(."},{"t":"0015 — Font families, vector icons, letter spacing, dashed borders, and what a real design measured","h":"Letter spacing is part of the text style","u":"architecture/0015-fonts-icons-and-design-fidelity.html#letter-spacing-is-part-of-the-text-style","s":"Reference","x":"TextStyle::letter_spacing in pixels (dp in the UI: Text::letter_spacing), with the CSS meaning: added after every character, the last included, so it changes the measured width. Text::uppercase() is a string operation, as text-transform is. Text::line_height_dp takes a line height as designs give it."},{"t":"0015 — Font families, vector icons, letter spacing, dashed borders, and what a real design measured","h":"Baselines round to the nearest pixel","u":"architecture/0015-fonts-icons-and-design-fidelity.html#baselines-round-to-the-nearest-pixel","s":"Reference","x":"The shaper's baseline was truncated to a whole pixel, which set a line whose baseline fell at 32.9 a pixel higher than the browser's (and higher than its neighbour at 33.0). It is now rounded. This is the one change that alters existing output: text can move by one pixel. The showcase's visual-regression references still pass within their tolerance."},{"t":"0015 — Font families, vector icons, letter spacing, dashed borders, and what a real design measured","h":"Vector icons: a small SVG subset, rasterised by zeno into the glyph atlas","u":"architecture/0015-fonts-icons-and-design-fidelity.html#vector-icons-a-small-svg-subset-rasterised-by-zeno-into-the-glyph-atlas","s":"Reference","x":"VectorIcon is Copy data naming a 'static description: VectorIcon::svg(markup) for markup pasted from a design, VectorIcon::stroked(path) / filled(path) for path data in the usual 24-unit box. An icon set is a file of constants (examples/fundidesk/src/icons.rs). Frame::draw_icon(icon, rect, color, stroke_width) draws one; the UI has Icon::vector(icon).size(dp).color(..).stroke_width(..).Where the masks live: in the gl"},{"t":"0015 — Font families, vector icons, letter spacing, dashed borders, and what a real design measured","h":"Dashed borders are a mode of the rectangle, in the shader","u":"architecture/0015-fonts-icons-and-design-fidelity.html#dashed-borders-are-a-mode-of-the-rectangle-in-the-shader","s":"Reference","x":"DrawOp::DashedRect { rect, corner_radius, fill, border, dash } with Dash { length, gap }, drawn by the same instance as a bordered rectangle: for a rectangle, the instance's spare extra field carries the dash length and period, and the fragment shader multiplies the border's coverage by a dash pattern laid along the middle line of the stroke, corners included (arc length on the arcs), with a pixel of falloff at dash "},{"t":"0015 — Font families, vector icons, letter spacing, dashed borders, and what a real design measured","h":"What the fidelity loop measured","u":"architecture/0015-fonts-icons-and-design-fidelity.html#what-the-fidelity-loop-measured","s":"Reference","x":"Four screens were built to the highest fidelity reached and compared with their references at 780 px wide, 2 px per dp, full artboard height, default morningstar-diff settings (threshold 2, anti-aliasing radius 1), with the four 56 px corner squares the design tool rounds off left out.ScreenSize (px)Pixels differingMean errorMax error01 splash780 x 16880.2868%0.13949.3903 login780 x 16880.1671%0.04767.6307 dashboard7"},{"t":"0015 — Font families, vector icons, letter spacing, dashed borders, and what a real design measured","h":"Not done, and approximations","u":"architecture/0015-fonts-icons-and-design-fidelity.html#not-done-and-approximations","s":"Reference","x":"Gamma of anti-aliased edges. The dominant remaining difference, and visible: body text is lighter than the design's. Closing it means either blending in encoded space throughout (which reverses 0011's choice and would make gradients match CSS too) or correcting coverage per glyph from the text colour. Neither was attempted; it deserves its own record and measurements on a device.No wrapping rows (flex-wrap). Chips ar"},{"t":"0015 — Font families, vector icons, letter spacing, dashed borders, and what a real design measured","h":"Public API added","u":"architecture/0015-fonts-icons-and-design-fidelity.html#public-api-added","s":"Reference","x":"morningstar-core: Dash (new, css, fitted), DrawOp::DashedRect, DisplayList::{stroke_dashed_rect, fill_dashed_rect}.morningstar-text: FontFamily, TextSpan, VectorIcon (svg, stroked, filled, check, aspect_ratio), IconError; TextStyle::{family, letter_spacing} (fields and builders); TextContext::{measure_spans, draw_spans, draw_icon, has_family}.morningstar-runtime: Frame::{draw_text_spans, measure_text_spans, draw_icon"},{"t":"0015 — Font families, vector icons, letter spacing, dashed borders, and what a real design measured","h":"Verified","u":"architecture/0015-fonts-icons-and-design-fidelity.html#verified","s":"Reference","x":"All on macOS, GPU tests on Metal through the offscreen renderer.morningstar-core (4 new tests): the dash operation, its fallbacks to a solid border, the fitted pattern dividing the perimeter, the perimeter of a square and a circle.morningstar-gpu vocabulary.rs (4 new): exact pixels of dashes on a straight edge; the fill showing in the gaps; a dashed ring covering its share of the solid ring within 3% for four shapes "},{"t":"0016 — Text input","h":"","u":"architecture/0016-text-input.html","s":"Reference","x":"0016 — Text inputStatus: Accepted (2026-11-11). Android: built, installed and typed into on a Galaxy A27 (Android 16, Samsung Keyboard); what that run did and did not cover is under \"Verified\". iOS: the Rust half type-checks; the Swift has never been compiled.Lifts the limitations \"No text input, focus or keyboard\" and \"No request_redraw from … timers\" of ADR 0013, and supplies the two things ADR 0012 left out on pur"},{"t":"0016 — Text input","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0016-text-input.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"An application could draw a box that looked like a field, and nothing could be typed into it. Typing needs four things the framework did not have: a host view the platform's input method will talk to; a way for Rust, which decides what has focus, to raise and lower the keyboard; the keyboard's size, so content can get out from under it; and a frame at a future time, so a caret can blink in an app that otherwise draws"},{"t":"0016 — Text input","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0016-text-input.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":""},{"t":"0016 — Text input","h":"Text input is declared per frame","u":"architecture/0016-text-input.html#text-input-is-declared-per-frame","s":"Reference","x":"Whether the keyboard is up is part of what a frame says, like its display list. Frame::set_text_input(TextInputRequest { session, traits, text, caret }) in a frame means \"this field has focus and holds this\"; a frame that does not call it means \"no field has focus\". There is no show_keyboard() to forget to pair with a hide_keyboard(): a field that disappears with its screen takes the keyboard with it.The engine keeps"},{"t":"0016 — Text input","h":"Android: the host keeps a copy and the input method edits it","u":"architecture/0016-text-input.html#android-the-host-keeps-a-copy-and-the-input-method-edits-it","s":"Reference","x":"Android's input methods do not send keystrokes. They read the text around the cursor, mark a region as \"composing\", replace it, move the selection, and expect the editor to hold all of that. An editor that only forwards \"insert this\" works until the first autocorrection, which is expressed as \"make characters 4 to 9 the composing region, then set the composing text\", and then duplicates words.So the host keeps an Edi"},{"t":"0016 — Text input","h":"Rust asks the host through a JNI upcall","u":"architecture/0016-text-input.html#rust-asks-the-host-through-a-jni-upcall","s":"Reference","x":"The application decides what has focus while building a frame, on the render thread; Android shows a keyboard only from its main thread. Rust must reach Java.Chosen: an upcall. MorningstarNative.onTextInputCommand(command, serial, keyboard, capitalization, action, flags, text, caret) is a static Java method that posts to the main thread and returns. The render thread calls it after a frame when take_text_input_comman"},{"t":"0016 — Text input","h":"iOS: UIKeyInput, and a pull","u":"architecture/0016-text-input.html#ios-uikeyinput-and-a-pull","s":"Reference","x":"On iOS the engine runs on the main thread and every change happens inside a call the host makes, so the existing pattern holds: after each call the host asks. morningstar_ios_take_text_input(host, &out) fills a small struct (command, keyboard, capitalisation, action, secure, multiline, autocorrect, has_text) when there is something to do.MetalView adopts UIKeyInput (which includes UITextInputTraits): insertText → mor"},{"t":"0016 — Text input","h":"The keyboard inset","u":"architecture/0016-text-input.html#the-keyboard-inset","s":"Reference","x":"FrameInfo::keyboard_inset is the height, in physical pixels, of the part of the surface the keyboard covers, measured from the surface's bottom edge (so it includes the gesture bar beneath the keyboard). It is separate from insets, as ADR 0012 said it would be. Engine::set_keyboard_inset produces a frame when it changes.Android: android:windowSoftInputMode=\"adjustResize|stateHidden\". The window is edge to edge (setDe"},{"t":"0016 — Text input","h":"Timed redraw","u":"architecture/0016-text-input.html#timed-redraw","s":"Reference","x":"Frame::request_redraw_after(delay) asks for one frame after delay. Like request_redraw it lasts one frame: whatever frame comes next, the timer or an earlier one caused by input, clears it, and the app asks again if it still depends on time. The shortest delay asked in a frame wins.The engine stores a deadline. needs_frame() is true once it has passed; Engine::redraw_due_in() is how long a host may sleep. A hidden ap"},{"t":"0016 — Text input","h":"The editing model","u":"architecture/0016-text-input.html#the-editing-model","s":"Reference","x":"crates/morningstar-ui/src/edit.rs: pure functions from (value, caret, composing range, event, field rules) to a new value. No widget or platform types.Offsets are bytes and always on extended grapheme cluster boundaries (unicode-segmentation, a direct dependency of morningstar-ui at the version cosmic-text already brings in). Backspace removes one cluster: an Arabic letter with its shadda and vowel, e + combining acu"},{"t":"0016 — Text input","h":"The widget","u":"architecture/0016-text-input.html#the-widget","s":"Reference","x":"TextInput<M> is a leaf, like Text: only the text. Padding, border and background belong to the Container around it, which keeps ADR 0013's \"one box type\" and lets a design's field be built from the same pieces as everything else. Three Container methods connect the box to the input inside it: input_frame() (a tap anywhere on the box focuses its first TextInput), focused_border(width, color) and focused_background(col"},{"t":"0016 — Text input","h":"Keeping the field in view","u":"architecture/0016-text-input.html#keeping-the-field-in-view","s":"Reference","x":"Three mechanisms, chosen by where the field is:Bottom safe area includes the keyboard. A box that pads for the bottom safe edge (SafeArea, safe_area(SafeEdges::BOTTOM)) pads for max(bottom inset, keyboard inset). A screen built as \"header, scroll view, bottom bar\" therefore resizes like a native one: the bar's content rides above the keyboard and the scroll view shrinks. This is the behaviour for a fixed bottom butto"},{"t":"0016 — Text input","h":"Consequences","u":"architecture/0016-text-input.html#consequences","s":"Reference","x":"The host interfaces grew. JNI: nativeSetKeyboardInset, nativeTextEdited, nativeTextCommit, nativeTextCommand (15 native methods in all) and the first Rust-to-Java call, onTextInputCommand. C: morningstar_ios_set_keyboard_inset, _text_insert, _text_delete_backward, _text_focus_lost, _take_text_input, _redraw_delay.morningstar-android now contains JNI function-table access, where before it only passed JNIEnv* through.E"},{"t":"0016 — Text input","h":"Limitations","u":"architecture/0016-text-input.html#limitations","s":"Reference","x":"No selection: no range selection, drag handles, long-press menu, copy, cut or paste (an input method's own paste arrives as text and works). The seam: TextEditState and TextInputRequest carry a caret where a range would go, the Android host already tracks selectionStart/selectionEnd and sends both, and EditState is where an anchor would be added.No caret movement by key: arrow keys, Home and End do nothing. The caret"},{"t":"0016 — Text input","h":"Verified","u":"architecture/0016-text-input.html#verified","s":"Reference","x":"On the development host (macOS):Engine: session diffing (show once, restart on another field or traits, no echo of the host's own edits, correction of a filtered edit, update after an app-side edit, no hide after a user dismissal), keyboard inset, and timed redraw (not before due, one frame when due, lasts one frame, dropped while hidden, shortest wins): 12 tests in crates/morningstar-runtime/src/engine.rs.UTF-16 con"},{"t":"0016 — Text input","h":"Not verified","u":"architecture/0016-text-input.html#not-verified","s":"Reference","x":"Composition on a device. Every edited line observed had composing=false: Samsung Keyboard committed each character. Composing text, setComposingRegion (autocorrect of an earlier word) and swipe typing have not been seen to work on hardware. Gboard, which composes by default, has not been tried.Dismissal by the user: the Back gesture with the keyboard up, the keyboard's hide key, and onBackPressed without a soft keybo"},{"t":"0017 — Affine transforms, multi-colour vector pictures, and four text effects","h":"","u":"architecture/0017-transforms-and-vector-pictures.html","s":"Reference","x":"0017 — Affine transforms, multi-colour vector pictures, and four text effectsStatus: Accepted (2026-11-12). Verified on the development host only (macOS, Metal, offscreen). Nothing here has run on a device, on Vulkan or on GLES, and nothing here has been timed.Extends 0011 (renderer vocabulary: the instance grows again, and clips gain a second space), 0013 (UI layer), 0014 (images: pictures are drawn through that pat"},{"t":"0017 — Affine transforms, multi-colour vector pictures, and four text effects","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0017-transforms-and-vector-pictures.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"A second real design (examples/callready, five screens) uses things the renderer could not draw:transform: rotate(-8deg), rotate(5deg) translateY(-8px), rotate(135deg) on filled boxes, on bordered boxes with text inside, on text and on icons: about thirty occurrences;font-style: italic in a family that has no italic face, which a browser synthesises by slanting the glyphs;inline SVG illustrations with several fills a"},{"t":"0017 — Affine transforms, multi-colour vector pictures, and four text effects","h":"Decisions","u":"architecture/0017-transforms-and-vector-pictures.html#decisions","s":"Reference","x":""},{"t":"0017 — Affine transforms, multi-colour vector pictures, and four text effects","h":"A transform is display-list state, carried per instance","u":"architecture/0017-transforms-and-vector-pictures.html#a-transform-is-display-list-state-carried-per-instance","s":"Reference","x":"morningstar-core gains Transform, a 2x3 affine matrix (a b c d tx ty, the convention of CSS matrix(); y down, so positive rotation is clockwise, as in CSS), and the display list gains PushTransform { transform } / PopTransform (DisplayList::push_transform, pop_transform). The transform is in the same physical-pixel space as rectangles: an operation is recorded exactly as before, and each of its points then goes throu"},{"t":"0017 — Affine transforms, multi-colour vector pictures, and four text effects","h":"Instance layout: 144 bytes (was 112)","u":"architecture/0017-transforms-and-vector-pictures.html#instance-layout-144-bytes-was-112","s":"Reference","x":"Nine vec4<f32> (was seven), 9 of 16 vertex attributes, stride 144 of 2048; eleven varyings (limit 16; 15 on WebGL2-class GLES). All within Limits::downlevel_defaults().LocationFieldContents0 to 6unchanged (0011, 0014)7matrixa, b, c, d: the linear part8placementtx, ty, edge scale, flags (1: the transform does more than move; 2: the shaped clip is in local space)At 1,000 instances the per-frame upload is 144 KB against"},{"t":"0017 — Affine transforms, multi-colour vector pictures, and four text effects","h":"Clips and transforms","u":"architecture/0017-transforms-and-vector-pictures.html#clips-and-transforms","s":"Reference","x":"0011's clip state is a screen rectangle intersected with at most one rounded rectangle. That state now has the shaped slot in one of two spaces.A transform inside a clip (a rotated tile inside a scroll view or a clipped card): the clip stays where it is on screen and cuts the transformed content. Exact for square and rounded clips, with no new mechanism: the fragment's screen position is what clips are tested against"},{"t":"0017 — Affine transforms, multi-colour vector pictures, and four text effects","h":"The UI layer: a modifier on every widget, about its centre, outside layout","u":"architecture/0017-transforms-and-vector-pictures.html#the-ui-layer-a-modifier-on-every-widget-about-its-centre-outside-layout","s":"Reference","x":"Every widget (Container and all its presets, Text, TextInput, Button, Icon, Picture, Avatar, Element) gains .transform(Transform) and .rotate(degrees). Lengths are dp; the transform is applied about the centre of the widget's laid-out rectangle (CSS's default transform-origin), to the widget and its whole subtree. Layout does not see it: the widget keeps its room, and the rectangles painted inside are the same ones, "},{"t":"0017 — Affine transforms, multi-colour vector pictures, and four text effects","h":"Synthetic italic is done by the rasteriser, not by the transform","u":"architecture/0017-transforms-and-vector-pictures.html#synthetic-italic-is-done-by-the-rasteriser-not-by-the-transform","s":"Reference","x":"TextStyle::italic (and TextSpan::italic; Text::italic(), Span::italic() in the UI) asks the shaper for an italic face. When the family has none, cosmic-text marks the glyphs and its rasteriser slants the outlines by 14 degrees (tan 14° = 0.2493; Skia uses 0.25) before filling them, which is what a browser does. The slanted glyphs are rasterised sharp at their final shape, per glyph about its own baseline, with unchan"},{"t":"0017 — Affine transforms, multi-colour vector pictures, and four text effects","h":"Multi-colour vector pictures: composited on the CPU into one image","u":"architecture/0017-transforms-and-vector-pictures.html#multi-colour-vector-pictures-composited-on-the-cpu-into-one-image","s":"Reference","x":"morningstar-text (the crate that owns zeno) gains VectorPicture: a view box and a 'static slice of Layers. A Layer is a Shape (Path in SVG d syntax, Circle, Ellipse, Rect with a radius, Line) with an optional fill colour and fill rule, an optional stroke (colour, width, cap, join), an opacity, an optional transform (a matrix, or rotate(deg, cx, cy) kept symbolic so it can be written in a constant) and an optional cli"},{"t":"0017 — Affine transforms, multi-colour vector pictures, and four text effects","h":"Text effects","u":"architecture/0017-transforms-and-vector-pictures.html#text-effects","s":"Reference","x":"Hard shadow (Text::shadow(x, y, colour)): the same glyphs drawn once more first, moved by whole pixels, all in the shadow's colour. No blur exists, and none is implied by the name.Outline (Text::outline(width, colour), Frame::draw_text_outlined): glyph outlines are available from the shaper's scaler for free, so TextContext::rasterise_outlined collects them for a laid-out text and paints them with the picture composi"},{"t":"0017 — Affine transforms, multi-colour vector pictures, and four text effects","h":"Not done, and approximations","u":"architecture/0017-transforms-and-vector-pictures.html#not-done-and-approximations","s":"Reference","x":"Rotated text and pictures are resampled bitmaps. Glyphs are rasterised upright and turned by bilinear sampling, so text at an angle is a little softer than a browser's. Rasterising glyphs per angle would need the angle in the atlas key; not attempted. Small angles (the design's ±1 to 8 degrees) look right by eye on the host.Shadows under a non-uniform scale keep a circular blur in local space, so the blur is stretche"},{"t":"0017 — Affine transforms, multi-colour vector pictures, and four text effects","h":"Public API added","u":"architecture/0017-transforms-and-vector-pictures.html#public-api-added","s":"Reference","x":"morningstar-core: Transform (IDENTITY, matrix, translate, scale, rotate, skew_x, skew_y, shear_x, then, and_rotate, and_translate, and_scale, and_skew_x, about, with_translation_scaled, apply, inverse, determinant, bounding_rect, is_identity, is_translation, is_finite); DrawOp::{PushTransform, PopTransform}; DisplayList::{push_transform, pop_transform}; ImageStore::add_premultiplied_rgba8_vec; srgb_encode.morningstar"},{"t":"0017 — Affine transforms, multi-colour vector pictures, and four text effects","h":"Verified","u":"architecture/0017-transforms-and-vector-pictures.html#verified","s":"Reference","x":"All on macOS; GPU tests on Metal through the offscreen renderer. 640 tests pass in the workspace without examples/callready (563 before; 77 new).morningstar-core (8 new): rotation direction and exact quarter turns, then against CSS list order, about, inverse, shear, bounding rectangle; the two operations recorded; premultiplied registration and srgb_encode.morningstar-gpu unit (5 new): composition order, undrawable t"},{"t":"0017 — Affine transforms, multi-colour vector pictures, and four text effects","h":"Not verified","u":"architecture/0017-transforms-and-vector-pictures.html#not-verified","s":"Reference","x":"Anything on a device: Vulkan, GLES, the larger instance on a mobile GPU, frame times, picture rasterisation time in a release build, memory with pictures.The Android and iOS cross type-checks.The design's five screens themselves, which are built on this by others."},{"t":"0018 — System back and a repeating timer message","h":"","u":"architecture/0018-back-and-timers.html","s":"Reference","x":"0018 — System back and a repeating timer messageStatus: Accepted (2026-11-12). Verified on the development host only. Nothing here has run on a device: the Java compiles against the API 36 SDK, the Rust type-checks for Android and iOS, and no Back gesture has been made on a phone. What that leaves open is under \"Not verified\".Adds two things to the UI layer of ADR 0013 and lifts its limitation \"No request_redraw from"},{"t":"0018 — System back and a repeating timer message","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0018-back-and-timers.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"An application with more than one screen could not react to the system's Back: the Android host closed a keyboard if a field had focus and otherwise let the activity go, whatever screen the application was on. And an Elm-style application had no source of time: view is a function of the state, the state changes only in update, and nothing delivered a message without a touch. A countdown, a resend-code delay or a call"},{"t":"0018 — System back and a repeating timer message","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0018-back-and-timers.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":""},{"t":"0018 — System back and a repeating timer message","h":"Back is a question the application answers","u":"architecture/0018-back-and-timers.html#back-is-a-question-the-application-answers","s":"Reference","x":"The system back gesture or key. true: the application handled it (it went to its previous screen). false: it had nowhere to go, and the system does what it does then. The default is false, so existing applications behave as before.The same method, with the same default, is on the runtime's App trait, and Engine::back() calls it: a true schedules a frame, as input does; a false changes nothing. Ui<A> forwards App::bac"},{"t":"0018 — System back and a repeating timer message","h":"Android: ask without waiting, answer by upcall","u":"architecture/0018-back-and-timers.html#android-ask-without-waiting-answer-by-upcall","s":"Reference","x":"The application lives on the render thread (ADR 0004); Back arrives on the main thread. Two ways to connect them were considered.A synchronous question: the JNI entry point sends a message with a reply channel and blocks until the render thread answers, as nativeSurfaceDestroyed does. The answer would be available where Android asks for it, but the main thread would wait for whatever the render thread is doing: norma"},{"t":"0018 — System back and a repeating timer message","h":"Android 16: OnBackInvokedCallback","u":"architecture/0018-back-and-timers.html#android-16-onbackinvokedcallback","s":"Reference","x":"The template has targetSdk = 36. On Android 16, for an app targeting 36, predictive back is on by default: onBackPressed is no longer called and KEYCODE_BACK is not dispatched. An OnBackInvokedCallback is the only way to hear Back there. So:onCreate registers one callback at PRIORITY_DEFAULT on API 33 and later (in a nested class, Api33, so the activity class loads cleanly on older releases) and onDestroy unregisters"},{"t":"0018 — System back and a repeating timer message","h":"iOS: a C function, not yet called","u":"architecture/0018-back-and-timers.html#ios-a-c-function-not-yet-called","s":"Reference","x":"bool morningstar_ios_back(MorningstarHost *host) calls Engine::back() and returns its answer. On iOS the engine lives on the main thread, so the answer is immediate and no frame is built inside the call; a frame follows a true through the usual needs_frame rule. iOS has no system Back: the intended caller is a leading-edge swipe (UIScreenEdgePanGestureRecognizer). The Swift host does not call it yet; HostViewControll"},{"t":"0018 — System back and a repeating timer message","h":"A repeating timer message","u":"architecture/0018-back-and-timers.html#a-repeating-timer-message","s":"Reference","x":"While it returns Some((interval, message)), message is delivered to update every interval, and frames come only as needed for that. None stops it and the application is fully idle again. It is a function of the state, like view: a countdown returns Some while it runs and None at zero, and there is no start or stop call to forget.It is built entirely on what ADR 0016 added for the caret, with no thread and no clock of"},{"t":"0018 — System back and a repeating timer message","h":"Consequences","u":"architecture/0018-back-and-timers.html#consequences","s":"Reference","x":"The host interfaces grew, both halves together. JNI: nativeBack (16 native methods in all) and a second Rust-to-Java call, onBackResult. C: morningstar_ios_back.App and Application each have one more defaulted method, and Application has tick. No existing application changes: the examples compile untouched.Back on Android is now always answered by the host itself (move to back, or finish) instead of by super.onBackPr"},{"t":"0018 — System back and a repeating timer message","h":"Limitations","u":"architecture/0018-back-and-timers.html#limitations","s":"Reference","x":"One timer per application. Two rhythms are one tick at the faster pace and a counter in the state.A tick is not a clock. It counts foreground intervals; see above for wall-clock deadlines.No animation-frame message. tick with a zero interval delivers once per frame and keeps frames coming, which is continuous rendering; nothing is interpolated for the application.State changed through Ui::app_mut or a background thre"},{"t":"0018 — System back and a repeating timer message","h":"Verified","u":"architecture/0018-back-and-timers.html#verified","s":"Reference","x":"On the development host (macOS) only:Engine: a handled Back produces exactly one frame, a declined one draws nothing, an app without back declines, a Back while hidden is answered and drawn on return: 4 tests in crates/morningstar-runtime/src/engine.rs.UI layer, driving frames with FrameInfo::with_elapsed: 17 tests in crates/morningstar-ui/tests/back_and_tick.rs. Back pops a navigation stack and then declines, reache"},{"t":"0018 — System back and a repeating timer message","h":"Not verified","u":"architecture/0018-back-and-timers.html#not-verified","s":"Reference","x":"Back on any device. No APK was built or installed for this record, and no gesture or key was sent. Unseen on hardware: that the callback is invoked on Android 16 with targetSdk 36; that onBackResult is found and called (its JNI signature (Z)V is checked only by reading); that a handled Back redraws and a declined one moves the task to the background; the keyboard-open and hardware-keyboard cases; three-button navigat"},{"t":"0019 — Effects and platform services: HTTP, recording, playback and stored values","h":"","u":"architecture/0019-effects-and-platform-services.html","s":"Reference","x":"0019 — Effects and platform services: HTTP, recording, playback and stored valuesStatus: Accepted (2026-11-12). Verified on the development host only. Nothing here has run on a device: the Rust type-checks for Android and iOS, the Java compiles against the API 36 SDK, and no request has been made, no sound recorded or played and no value stored on a phone. The Swift has never been compiled. What that leaves open is u"},{"t":"0019 — Effects and platform services: HTTP, recording, playback and stored values","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0019-effects-and-platform-services.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"update changes the state and returns; view describes it. Nothing in that loop can wait, and nothing in it can touch the platform. So an application could not talk to a server, record the user's voice, play a reply, or remember its own identity between launches. The first real client (examples/callready) needs all four.Each of them is something the platform already does well and a pure-Rust implementation would do wor"},{"t":"0019 — Effects and platform services: HTTP, recording, playback and stored values","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0019-effects-and-platform-services.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":""},{"t":"0019 — Effects and platform services: HTTP, recording, playback and stored values","h":"Requests are data; answers are messages","u":"architecture/0019-effects-and-platform-services.html#requests-are-data-answers-are-messages","s":"Reference","x":"An application asks for work with a value and gets the result as a message: The runtime calls effects once after setup and once after every update (and after back). update records in the state that work is wanted; effects moves it into out: The last argument makes the message: an enum variant that holds the result, or a closure. Each request that has an answer gets exactly one, an error included; nothing fails by sta"},{"t":"0019 — Effects and platform services: HTTP, recording, playback and stored values","h":"When an answer is delivered","u":"architecture/0019-effects-and-platform-services.html#when-an-answer-is-delivered","s":"Reference","x":"Engine::service_result(id, result) hands the answer to the application at once, on the render thread, between frames, exactly as Engine::pointer hands over a touch: update runs, then effects, and if the application was waiting for that id a frame is scheduled. An id nobody is waiting for (answered already, given up on with forget_pending, or from before the application was replaced) is dropped and schedules nothing.T"},{"t":"0019 — Effects and platform services: HTTP, recording, playback and stored values","h":"What the application knows before its first frame","u":"architecture/0019-effects-and-platform-services.html#what-the-application-knows-before-its-first-frame","s":"Reference","x":"Waiting for a load to learn one's own device id means a first frame that cannot know it. So the host hands two things over at setup, synchronously:Setup::stored(key): the values stored on earlier runs, as they were when the process started.Setup::install_id(): a 128-bit random identifier as 32 lower-case hexadecimal digits, generated by the host once per installation with the platform's secure random source (SecureRa"},{"t":"0019 — Effects and platform services: HTTP, recording, playback and stored values","h":"Hosts carry requests out","u":"architecture/0019-effects-and-platform-services.html#hosts-carry-requests-out","s":"Reference","x":"After creating the engine, and after every call into it, a host calls Engine::take_service_requests, which is the same rule as for needs_frame and the keyboard: requests only appear inside calls into the engine. The host does the work off the render thread and answers through Engine::service_result. A request it cannot carry out it answers immediately with ServiceRequest::unsupported()."},{"t":"0019 — Effects and platform services: HTTP, recording, playback and stored values","h":"Android","u":"architecture/0019-effects-and-platform-services.html#android","s":"Reference","x":"MorningstarServices.java (new) does all of it; MorningstarActivity only tells it when the activity starts, stops and is destroyed, and passes on the permission result.Out: two more static methods looked up in java.rs next to onTextInputCommand: onHttpRequest(long id, String method, String url, String headers, byte[] body, int timeoutMs) and onServiceCommand(int command, long id, String first, String second, byte[] da"},{"t":"0019 — Effects and platform services: HTTP, recording, playback and stored values","h":"iOS","u":"architecture/0019-effects-and-platform-services.html#ios","s":"Reference","x":"The C interface gained morningstar_ios_create_with_state, a polled queue morningstar_ios_take_service_request (the host drains it after every call, as it drains morningstar_ios_take_text_input), and morningstar_ios_http_result, morningstar_ios_audio_result, morningstar_ios_load_result. A request's strings and bytes are lent until the next take. HostServices.swift (new) implements the services with URLSession (ephemer"},{"t":"0019 — Effects and platform services: HTTP, recording, playback and stored values","h":"No host","u":"architecture/0019-effects-and-platform-services.html#no-host","s":"Reference","x":"Ui::new under nothing (a screenshot tool, a test that does not care) has nobody to carry requests out. They are failed, at the start of the next frame, with the Unsupported error of their kind (load gives None), so an application shows its error state instead of waiting for ever. Ui knows a host is there from the host's first call to service_requests, which is why a host makes that call before the first frame."},{"t":"0019 — Effects and platform services: HTTP, recording, playback and stored values","h":"A test driver instead of a mock platform","u":"architecture/0019-effects-and-platform-services.html#a-test-driver-instead-of-a-mock-platform","s":"Reference","x":"Ui::testing(app) holds every request for the test: Answering delivers the message immediately, like Ui::send. Stored values live in the driver's memory (ui.stored(key), ui.set_stored(key, value)), so store and load need no answering; playback behaves as on a device (stop_playback or a second play ends the pending clip as Interrupted). Ui::with_services(app, |request| ...) adds a closure that answers what it wants to "},{"t":"0019 — Effects and platform services: HTTP, recording, playback and stored values","h":"Alternatives considered","u":"architecture/0019-effects-and-platform-services.html#alternatives-considered","s":"Reference","x":"A Rust HTTP and TLS stack (ureq or reqwest over rustls, on a thread of our own). Rejected:It is the largest dependency the project would have: rustls with a crypto provider (ring or aws-lc-rs, C and assembly that must cross-compile for every Android ABI and iOS), webpki, and a URL and HTTP library, against a policy (ADR 0007) that admits a dependency only where writing or obtaining the equivalent is a significant cos"},{"t":"0019 — Effects and platform services: HTTP, recording, playback and stored values","h":"Threading","u":"architecture/0019-effects-and-platform-services.html#threading","s":"Reference","x":"Render thread (Android) / main thread (iOS): everything the application sees. effects, update with an answer, view. Never blocks on a service.Android HTTP pool: 4 daemon threads, one request each; a fifth request waits for a thread. Plus one timer thread for deadlines.Android service thread: one HandlerThread for recording, playback, storage and their file I/O, in request order, which is what makes a load after a sto"},{"t":"0019 — Effects and platform services: HTTP, recording, playback and stored values","h":"Lifecycle","u":"architecture/0019-effects-and-platform-services.html#lifecycle","s":"Reference","x":"Android's engine lives as long as the process (ADR 0004), not the activity. MorningstarServices holds the application context and never an activity; it looks one up only to ask for the microphone. So activity recreation loses nothing: requests in flight complete and are delivered to the same application. A second nativeInit in a live process is ignored.The activity stops (onStop): the playing clip ends as Interrupted"},{"t":"0019 — Effects and platform services: HTTP, recording, playback and stored values","h":"Security","u":"architecture/0019-effects-and-platform-services.html#security","s":"Reference","x":"Cleartext HTTP is refused, by the platform, not by a check of ours: res/xml/network_security_config.xml sets cleartextTrafficPermitted=\"false\" as the base and allows only 127.0.0.1, localhost and 10.0.2.2, which all mean the developer's own machine (adb reverse, and the emulator's name for its host) and cannot name another machine on a network. The exception is in release builds too; it does not weaken them. A refuse"},{"t":"0019 — Effects and platform services: HTTP, recording, playback and stored values","h":"Consequences","u":"architecture/0019-effects-and-platform-services.html#consequences","s":"Reference","x":"The host interfaces grew, both halves together (above). An application library and a host built from different sides of this change do not work together: nativeInit has the same symbol and different arguments.Application has one more defaulted method, App two. The examples compile untouched.Every Morningstar APK now declares two permissions and a network security config, whether or not the application uses them.Ui::f"},{"t":"0019 — Effects and platform services: HTTP, recording, playback and stored values","h":"Limitations","u":"architecture/0019-effects-and-platform-services.html#limitations","s":"Reference","x":"No streaming, progress, cookies or multipart helpers. A body is bytes in and bytes out, whole. No redirect policy beyond the platform's.A request cannot be cancelled on the wire. forget_pending drops answers; the work still finishes. An application that needs to ignore one late reply puts a sequence number in the closure.The body of a GET or HEAD is not sent.One recording and one clip at a time. No levels while recor"},{"t":"0019 — Effects and platform services: HTTP, recording, playback and stored values","h":"Verified","u":"architecture/0019-effects-and-platform-services.html#verified","s":"Reference","x":"On the development host (macOS) only:Data types: 8 tests in crates/morningstar-core/src/services.rs.Engine: 12 tests in crates/morningstar-runtime/src/engine.rs. Requests reach the host once and in order; an awaited answer produces exactly one frame and the engine is idle before and after; an unawaited or repeated answer changes nothing; answers arrive in the host's order; an answer while hidden is delivered at once "},{"t":"0019 — Effects and platform services: HTTP, recording, playback and stored values","h":"Not verified","u":"architecture/0019-effects-and-platform-services.html#not-verified","s":"Reference","x":"Everything on an Android device. No APK was built or installed for this record. Unseen on hardware: that the upcalls are found and a request leaves the phone; TLS to a real server; the mapping of failures to Offline / Timeout (airplane mode, a dead port, a slow server); the cleartext rule, both that http://127.0.0.1 through adb reverse works and that another http:// is refused; the 10 MB cap; the permission dialog, d"},{"t":"0020 — App identity and launcher icons","h":"","u":"architecture/0020-app-identity-and-launcher-icons.html","s":"Reference","x":"0020 — App identity and launcher iconsStatus: Accepted (2026-11-12). The generator is verified by tests on the development host, and the Android half by building two APKs and reading them back with aapt2. Nothing was installed: how the icon looks on a home screen is not verified. The iOS half has never been through Xcode. Details under \"Verified\" and \"Not verified\".Builds on the thin hosts of ADR 0003 and the entry p"},{"t":"0020 — App identity and launcher icons","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0020-app-identity-and-launcher-icons.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"Every Morningstar app installed with Android's default icon and a name made up by the build script (\"Morningstar callready\"). The application id was a rule in tools/build-android.sh with a special case for one example; the iOS script and project.yml were hard-coded to that example. An app had no way to say what it is called or what it looks like on a home screen, and the two platforms want that said in very different"},{"t":"0020 — App identity and launcher icons","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0020-app-identity-and-launcher-icons.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":""},{"t":"0020 — App identity and launcher icons","h":"An app declares its identity once, in its Cargo.toml","u":"architecture/0020-app-identity-and-launcher-icons.html#an-app-declares-its-identity-once-in-its-cargotoml","s":"Reference","x":"Every key is optional, and so is the table. Paths are relative to the manifest.Why there: the app crate is the application (ADR 0005); its manifest is the one file every app already has, it sits next to the code and the artwork, and package.metadata is the place Cargo reserves for tools. The alternatives were a second file per app (one more thing to find and keep in step), values in the app's Rust code (the hosts nee"},{"t":"0020 — App identity and launcher icons","h":"The manifest is read by Cargo","u":"architecture/0020-app-identity-and-launcher-icons.html#the-manifest-is-read-by-cargo","s":"Reference","x":"morningstar-icon runs cargo metadata --format-version 1 --no-deps and takes five strings, the manifest's directory and the cdylib target's name out of the JSON with a small reader of its own (tools/morningstar-devtools/src/json.rs, about 250 lines: all of JSON, nesting limited, every error with its position).The alternative was to read the [package.metadata.morningstar] table from the manifest's text with a strict re"},{"t":"0020 — App identity and launcher icons","h":"One generator, in the development tool","u":"architecture/0020-app-identity-and-launcher-icons.html#one-generator-in-the-development-tool","s":"Reference","x":"tools/morningstar-devtools gains Identity (the declaration with defaults applied), IconSet (the pictures, checked) and the morningstar-icon binary: It uses what the crate had: its PNG reading and writing and Image::resized, which already averages areas in linear light with premultiplied alpha. An icon must be a square PNG of at least 512 pixels; anything else is refused with the file's name.--android <dir> writes a r"},{"t":"0020 — App identity and launcher icons","h":"The single-icon rule","u":"architecture/0020-app-identity-and-launcher-icons.html#the-single-icon-rule","s":"Reference","x":"An app that gives only icon gets an adaptive icon made from it: the foreground is the icon scaled to the 72 dp a launcher shows, centred on the transparent 108 dp canvas; the background is the colour of the icon's top-left pixel (or icon_background).The consequence: a launcher shows the icon exactly as drawn, cut by its mask. A circular mask removes the corners, as the round icon of API 25 does, so artwork that must "},{"t":"0020 — App identity and launcher icons","h":"The built-in icon","u":"architecture/0020-app-identity-and-launcher-icons.html#the-built-in-icon","s":"Reference","x":"An app that declares no icon and has no assets/icon.png gets a four-pointed star (|x|^0.6 + |y|^0.6 <= 1) in pale gold on a dark field, drawn by the tool with eight-by-eight supersampling. icon_background alone recolours the field."},{"t":"0020 — App identity and launcher icons","h":"Generated, not checked in","u":"architecture/0020-app-identity-and-launcher-icons.html#generated-not-checked-in","s":"Reference","x":"The template hosts contain no icon. tools/build-android.sh generates into templates/android/app/build/generated/morningstar/res and passes -Pmorningstar.resDir=… with the id, name and library name; build.gradle.kts adds that directory to the main source set's resources, and the manifest names @mipmap/ic_launcher and @mipmap/ic_launcher_round. tools/build-ios.sh (which now takes MORNINGSTAR_APP, as the Android script "},{"t":"0020 — App identity and launcher icons","h":"Icons drawn by the framework","u":"architecture/0020-app-identity-and-launcher-icons.html#icons-drawn-by-the-framework","s":"Reference","x":"examples/callready/examples/icon.rs and examples/fundidesk/examples/icon.rs draw each app's icon from its own art (the home screen's vinyl disc; the splash screen's logo) through the offscreen renderer and write assets/icon.png. For the transparent foreground the tool gains screenshot_transparent: a frame rendered over nothing instead of over the app's background, returned with straight alpha."},{"t":"0020 — App identity and launcher icons","h":"Consequences","u":"architecture/0020-app-identity-and-launcher-icons.html#consequences","s":"Reference","x":"An app's name, id and icon are in one reviewable place; the scripts have no per-app knowledge. hello-gpu declares id = \"dev.morningstar.hello\" and keeps the id it always had.The default name changed: an app that declares nothing is now called by its package name (showcase), not Morningstar showcase. All five examples declare a name.Each Android build starts the tool once (a debug build of it, about 2 s for an app wit"},{"t":"0020 — App identity and launcher icons","h":"Limitations","u":"architecture/0020-app-identity-and-launcher-icons.html#limitations","s":"Reference","x":"One icon for all densities and both platforms; no per-density or per-platform artwork, no iOS dark or tinted variants, no monochrome layer for an app's own icon.The legacy square icon is full-bleed with square corners. Launchers of API 24 and 25 show it as it is.The display name is one string: no localisation.id is checked against Android's rule only. An id valid there and unusual for iOS (a segment that is a Swift k"},{"t":"0020 — App identity and launcher icons","h":"Verified","u":"architecture/0020-app-identity-and-launcher-icons.html#verified","s":"Reference","x":"96 tests in tools/morningstar-devtools pass (31 of them new): every Android file at its size; the circular mask (clear corners, untouched inside, an anti-aliased edge, the area of a disc); the single-icon rule pixel for pixel at all five densities, and that under a circle it equals the round icon; a given foreground used as it is; the background from the corner pixel and from the key; the iOS icon's size, flattening "},{"t":"0020 — App identity and launcher icons","h":"Not verified","u":"architecture/0020-app-identity-and-launcher-icons.html#not-verified","s":"Reference","x":"On a device: nothing was installed. How the icon and the name look on a home screen, in the app drawer, in recents and in settings; the adaptive icon under a real launcher's mask and parallax; the themed icon of the built-in star on Android 13 and later; the legacy icons on API 24 and 25.iOS, entirely: no Xcode on this machine. Not verified: that actool accepts the generated catalog and the single-size Contents.json;"},{"t":"0021 — The viewport in view, wrapping rows, scroll control, a bounded text field, animation and a navigator","h":"","u":"architecture/0021-viewport-wrap-scroll-animation-navigation.html","s":"Reference","x":"0021 — The viewport in view, wrapping rows, scroll control, a bounded text field, animation and a navigatorStatus: Accepted (2026-11-12). Implemented in crates/morningstar-layout and crates/morningstar-ui; crates/morningstar-runtime and the hosts are unchanged. Verified on the development host only (macOS, Metal, offscreen): nothing here has run on a device, and nothing has been timed.Lifts, without changing their de"},{"t":"0021 — The viewport in view, wrapping rows, scroll control, a bounded text field, animation and a navigator","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0021-viewport-wrap-scroll-animation-navigation.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"ADR 0013 fixed the model: the application is state, view(&self) returns plain data without a lifetime, widgets carry message values, the tree is rebuilt for every frame, and frames are produced on demand. Everything below had to fit that model rather than add a second one beside it. Three properties decided most of the choices:view is called once per frame anyway. There is no retained tree whose rebuild a cleverer me"},{"t":"0021 — The viewport in view, wrapping rows, scroll control, a bounded text field, animation and a navigator","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0021-viewport-wrap-scroll-animation-navigation.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":""},{"t":"0021 — The viewport in view, wrapping rows, scroll control, a bounded text field, animation and a navigator","h":"1. The viewport is an argument of the view","u":"architecture/0021-viewport-wrap-scroll-animation-navigation.html#1-the-viewport-is-an-argument-of-the-view","s":"Reference","x":"Viewport has the surface's size, the safe-area insets and the keyboard_inset, all in dp; scale; reduce_motion; safe_size(), keyboard_visible(), has_focus() and is_focused(id); and measures text: measure(&Text) -> Size on one line, measure_wrapped(&Text, max_width), line_count(&Text, max_width). Application::view gained a default body (an empty screen) so that an application implements one of the two.Rejected: an elem"},{"t":"0021 — The viewport in view, wrapping rows, scroll control, a bounded text field, animation and a navigator","h":"2. Rows wrap in the layout engine","u":"architecture/0021-viewport-wrap-scroll-animation-navigation.html#2-rows-wrap-in-the-layout-engine","s":"Reference","x":"Style::wrap and Style::line_gap (layout), Container::wrap(), Container::line_gap(dp) and the preset Wrap::new() (UI). Rows only; columns and stacks ignore it.It is done inside the four sweeps, with one new bit of scratch per node (line_break) and no allocation:Sweep 1 (natural widths): a wrapping row's natural width is that of one line, as for any row.Sweep 2 (final widths) is where lines are broken, because it is th"},{"t":"0021 — The viewport in view, wrapping rows, scroll control, a bounded text field, animation and a navigator","h":"3. Scroll control","u":"architecture/0021-viewport-wrap-scroll-animation-navigation.html#3-scroll-control","s":"Reference","x":"Positions stay widget state. A scroll position is changed by the user's finger between frames; if view declared it, every drag would have to be reported back through update to be declared again. So the application does not set a position, it asks for a move, once, through the channel it already uses for everything that is not a change of its own state: ScrollTo::top(id), ::end(id), ::offset(id, dp), ::reveal(widget_i"},{"t":"0021 — The viewport in view, wrapping rows, scroll control, a bounded text field, animation and a navigator","h":"4. A bounded multi-line field is a scroll view of its own text","u":"architecture/0021-viewport-wrap-scroll-animation-navigation.html#4-a-bounded-multi-line-field-is-a-scroll-view-of-its-own-text","s":"Reference","x":"TextInput::max_lines(n), or .max_height(dp). The bound is put on the layout (the measured leaf reports at most that height), so nothing overflows by construction. The text is clipped to the field and translated by an offset that is an ordinary scroll state under the field's own identity: it survives rebuilds, is dragged and flung like a scroll view, and is kept while the field has no focus. When the caret may have mo"},{"t":"0021 — The viewport in view, wrapping rows, scroll control, a bounded text field, animation and a navigator","h":"5. Animation: view says where things are; a widget may ask to be shown getting there","u":"architecture/0021-viewport-wrap-scroll-animation-navigation.html#5-animation-view-says-where-things-are-a-widget-may-ask-to-be-shown-getting-there","s":"Reference","x":"Nothing in the application drives an animation, and there is no animation object to hold. Three entry points: .animate covers the widget's own opacity, transform, layout offset, solid background colour and fixed width and height. Per identity the layer keeps what the view last asked for and, while a change is in progress, where it started and when; the shown value is the eased mix at the frame's elapsed. A change tha"},{"t":"0021 — The viewport in view, wrapping rows, scroll control, a bounded text field, animation and a navigator","h":"6. Navigator<S>","u":"architecture/0021-viewport-wrap-scroll-animation-navigation.html#6-navigatorltsgt","s":"Reference","x":"A Vec<S> that is never empty, with new, current, current_mut, root, stack, depth, can_pop, push, replace, pop, back() -> bool (the answer Application::back wants), pop_to_root, pop_to, reset, set, last_change() and transition(). It is application state and nothing else: it holds no widget state, does not know what a screen looks like and starts nothing. transition() returns the Transition for the current screen's roo"},{"t":"0021 — The viewport in view, wrapping rows, scroll control, a bounded text field, animation and a navigator","h":"Used in the examples","u":"architecture/0021-viewport-wrap-scroll-animation-navigation.html#used-in-the-examples","s":"Reference","x":"CallReady: the tip chips are one wrapping row, in the scripted call and the server's (the character-count estimate that chose a chip's form is gone); the online call's scroll view keeps one id and the application asks for its top when the caller says a new line; the reply field is .max_lines(3) and tests/online.rs allows no overflow; the history stack is a Navigator<Screen>; the end-to-end tests no longer scroll to f"},{"t":"0021 — The viewport in view, wrapping rows, scroll control, a bounded text field, animation and a navigator","h":"Consequences","u":"architecture/0021-viewport-wrap-scroll-animation-navigation.html#consequences","s":"Reference","x":"Application::view is no longer a required method. An application that implements neither shows an empty screen instead of failing to compile.morningstar_layout::Style has two more public fields. Code that builds one with struct-update syntax or the builder methods is unaffected; an exhaustive literal is not (there was none in the repository).Ui::rect_of returns Some where it returned None for a widget scrolled far ou"},{"t":"0021 — The viewport in view, wrapping rows, scroll control, a bounded text field, animation and a navigator","h":"Limitations","u":"architecture/0021-viewport-wrap-scroll-animation-navigation.html#limitations","s":"Reference","x":"No group opacity, as above.A leaving widget is on top and takes no room. A row removed from the middle of a list fades over its neighbours, which have already closed up; animating the gap closing needs the size of the leaving widget to stay in layout, which is not built.Transitions need distinct identities. The same screen pushed twice with the same id neither enters nor leaves.A transform is mixed component-wise: ex"},{"t":"0021 — The viewport in view, wrapping rows, scroll control, a bounded text field, animation and a navigator","h":"Verified","u":"architecture/0021-viewport-wrap-scroll-animation-navigation.html#verified","s":"Reference","x":"On the development host (macOS; GPU tests on Metal through the offscreen renderer):crates/morningstar-layout: 13 new tests in tests/wrap.rs with hand-computed rectangles (one line identical to a plain row; breaking; alignment per line; hugging; height seen by what follows and by a viewport; text offered the whole line, with the exact measure calls; fill within a line; stretch and fill-height within a line; spare heig"},{"t":"0021 — The viewport in view, wrapping rows, scroll control, a bounded text field, animation and a navigator","h":"Not verified","u":"architecture/0021-viewport-wrap-scroll-animation-navigation.html#not-verified","s":"Reference","x":"Anything on a device: how a glide, a slide between screens or a fade looks at 120 Hz on the Galaxy A27; whether 250 to 300 ms and ease-out feel right; frame times while a screen transition lays out and paints two screens.Performance: no timings. The cost statements above are counts of operations read from the code.The bounded field with a real keyboard: composition inside a scrolled field, and the input method's own "},{"t":"0022 — Text is measured and placed as a browser does: fractional widths and pen positions, the browser's baseline rule, kerning across spaces, no hinting","h":"","u":"architecture/0022-text-metrics-and-pixel-snapping.html","s":"Reference","x":"0022 — Text is measured and placed as a browser does: fractional widths and pen positions, the browser's baseline rule, kerning across spaces, no hintingStatus: Accepted (2026-11-12). Verified on the development host only (macOS, Metal, offscreen), against the Chrome references of both example apps. Nothing here has run on a device; the atlas use and frame time on a phone are unmeasured.Extends 0009 (text) and 0015 ("},{"t":"0022 — Text is measured and placed as a browser does: fractional widths and pen positions, the browser's baseline rule, kerning across spaces, no hinting","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0022-text-metrics-and-pixel-snapping.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"Reproducing two designs (examples/fundidesk, examples/callready) against Chrome's rendering left a list of hand corrections in the example code, each of them a place where text landed a fraction of a dp away from the browser's:boxes that hug a text were pinned to widths read from Chrome's layout, because a text measured 48.236 dp wide was laid out as 48.5;lines with a line-height carried nudges of 0.2 to 1 dp, and di"},{"t":"0022 — Text is measured and placed as a browser does: fractional widths and pen positions, the browser's baseline rule, kerning across spaces, no hinting","h":"Decisions","u":"architecture/0022-text-metrics-and-pixel-snapping.html#decisions","s":"Reference","x":""},{"t":"0022 — Text is measured and placed as a browser does: fractional widths and pen positions, the browser's baseline rule, kerning across spaces, no hinting","h":"A text's width is the sum of its advances, unrounded","u":"architecture/0022-text-metrics-and-pixel-snapping.html#a-texts-width-is-the-sum-of-its-advances-unrounded","s":"Reference","x":"morningstar-ui reports measured.width / scale to layout instead of ceil(measured.width) / scale. A hugging box is as wide as a browser makes it, and what follows it in a row starts where it starts in a browser. The existing guard against wrapping at a width a hair under the natural one (half a pixel) stays."},{"t":"0022 — Text is measured and placed as a browser does: fractional widths and pen positions, the browser's baseline rule, kerning across spaces, no hinting","h":"Horizontally, glyphs keep their fraction to a quarter of a pixel","u":"architecture/0022-text-metrics-and-pixel-snapping.html#horizontally-glyphs-keep-their-fraction-to-a-quarter-of-a-pixel","s":"Reference","x":"TextContext::draw no longer rounds origin.x. Each glyph's pen position is the paragraph's origin plus the glyph's fractional offset in the line; its whole part places the quad and its fraction, rounded to the nearest quarter, selects the glyph image rasterised for that offset (the four sub-pixel bins Skia uses too). Quads stay on whole pixels and map texels to pixels one to one, so nothing is resampled.The UI layer h"},{"t":"0022 — Text is measured and placed as a browser does: fractional widths and pen positions, the browser's baseline rule, kerning across spaces, no hinting","h":"Vertically, each line's top goes to the nearest pixel row and the baseline is a whole number of rows below it","u":"architecture/0022-text-metrics-and-pixel-snapping.html#vertically-each-lines-top-goes-to-the-nearest-pixel-row-and-the-baseline-is-a-whole-number-of-rows-below-it","s":"Reference","x":"Derived from the line boxes Chrome reported for CallReady (examples/callready/design/layout/*.txt, at 2 px per dp) and checked against the pixels of the references:The paragraph's font gives an ascent and a descent, each rounded to a whole device pixel: A = round(ascent * size), D = round(descent * size). (The \"strut\": the font of the paragraph's style, not of a span with a family of its own.)Half the leading is roun"},{"t":"0022 — Text is measured and placed as a browser does: fractional widths and pen positions, the browser's baseline rule, kerning across spaces, no hinting","h":"Kerning between a letter, a space and a letter is applied after shaping","u":"architecture/0022-text-metrics-and-pixel-snapping.html#kerning-between-a-letter-a-space-and-a-letter-is-applied-after-shaping","s":"Reference","x":"cosmic-text shapes each word by itself and each run of blanks by itself, so pairs with a space in them are never kerned; a browser shapes the run whole. For every space of a left-to-right line whose neighbours are in the same font, weight and span, the text engine shapes the three characters as one piece (ShapeWord::new, which is public) and as three, and moves the rest of the line by the difference. The result is re"},{"t":"0022 — Text is measured and placed as a browser does: fractional widths and pen positions, the browser's baseline rule, kerning across spaces, no hinting","h":"Optional ligatures are off where letters are spaced","u":"architecture/0022-text-metrics-and-pixel-snapping.html#optional-ligatures-are-off-where-letters-are-spaced","s":"Reference","x":"CSS asks that optional ligatures are not applied when letter-spacing is not zero. The shaper is now given liga and clig off for a style with a letter spacing (Space Grotesk's \"fi\" was one glyph in the middle of a spaced-out word). FundiDesk's verification screen went from 0.105% to 0.087%.Two gaps recorded earlier were checked and are not gaps: a line does break after a hyphen (test a_line_may_break_after_a_hyphen), "},{"t":"0022 — Text is measured and placed as a browser does: fractional widths and pen positions, the browser's baseline rule, kerning across spaces, no hinting","h":"Glyphs are not hinted","u":"architecture/0022-text-metrics-and-pixel-snapping.html#glyphs-are-not-hinted","s":"Reference","x":"The shaper's glyphs are requested with DISABLE_HINTING. Chrome on a dense screen draws outlines as the font has them; hinted horizontal features would be fitted to pixel rows the reference does not put them on. Measured: CallReady's welcome, report and premium screens lose 0.005 to 0.01 points of differing pixels; FundiDesk (Inter, Space Grotesk, which carry no instructions the hinter acts on) does not change. On a l"},{"t":"0022 — Text is measured and placed as a browser does: fractional widths and pen positions, the browser's baseline rule, kerning across spaces, no hinting","h":"A dashed border starts at the top of the left edge and runs down it","u":"architecture/0022-text-metrics-and-pixel-snapping.html#a-dashed-border-starts-at-the-top-of-the-left-edge-and-runs-down-it","s":"Reference","x":"0015 laid the dash pattern clockwise from the bottom of the left edge, from Chromium's source, and noted that it had not been checked against a reference. FundiDesk's new-repair screen has three dashed tiles (border: 1px dashed, radius 12, at 2 px per dp): in Chrome's picture the left edge's straight part begins with a whole dash at its top, the dashes run down it at a period of 10.07 px (65 dashes round the 654.5 px"},{"t":"0022 — Text is measured and placed as a browser does: fractional widths and pen positions, the browser's baseline rule, kerning across spaces, no hinting","h":"What was deleted from the examples","u":"architecture/0022-text-metrics-and-pixel-snapping.html#what-was-deleted-from-the-examples","s":"Reference","x":"CallReady: the pinned sizes of all eight cut-out plates and both score plates (now boxes hugging the letter with the design's padding), four lifts of display letters (-0.5 to -1 dp), the heading written out as two lines with two nudges, two spacers of 5.8 and 14.2 dp, two pinned chip widths, a benefit line split in two parts with a 2.5 dp gap, the report's feedback written out line by line, and two \"a hair more\" cons"},{"t":"0022 — Text is measured and placed as a browser does: fractional widths and pen positions, the browser's baseline rule, kerning across spaces, no hinting","h":"Consequences","u":"architecture/0022-text-metrics-and-pixel-snapping.html#consequences","s":"Reference","x":"Fidelity: with the hand corrections deleted, every one of the seventeen screens measures better than it did with them once 0023 is in as well; the table is there. Measured between the two changes, CallReady's five screens stood at 0.082, 0.165, 0.009, 0.086 and 0.056% against 0.069, 0.206, 0.014, 0.097 and 0.078% before (the welcome screen, almost all turned letters, was the one that was worse).TextContext::draw at a"},{"t":"0022 — Text is measured and placed as a browser does: fractional widths and pen positions, the browser's baseline rule, kerning across spaces, no hinting","h":"Not done","u":"architecture/0022-text-metrics-and-pixel-snapping.html#not-done","s":"Reference","x":"Line breaking with kerned widths; LineHeight::Normal; a strut taken from several fonts when a line mixes families (the tallest wins in CSS).Vertical sub-pixel positions: Chrome does not use them for horizontal text either."},{"t":"0023 — Colours are composited as sRGB-encoded values, like the web platform; light text is weighted","h":"","u":"architecture/0023-compositing-space.html","s":"Reference","x":"0023 — Colours are composited as sRGB-encoded values, like the web platform; light text is weightedStatus: Accepted (2026-11-12). Verified on the development host only (macOS, Metal, offscreen): pixel tests in crates/morningstar-gpu/tests/compositing.rs and the seventeen reference screens of the two example apps. Not run on a device: the choice of a non-sRGB surface format on Vulkan and GLES, and the frame time, are "},{"t":"0023 — Colours are composited as sRGB-encoded values, like the web platform; light text is weighted","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0023-compositing-space.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"The renderer blended in linear light: colours were converted from sRGB to linear, blended, and encoded again by an sRGB render target. That is the physically right way to mix light, and it is not what a design is made with. Browsers and design tools composite the encoded values: rgba(246, 196, 69, 0.25) over black is a quarter of each byte. Every design this framework is meant to reproduce was looked at, approved and"},{"t":"0023 — Colours are composited as sRGB-encoded values, like the web platform; light text is weighted","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0023-compositing-space.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":"Everything is composited in the encoded space. A Color remains what it was, linear RGB with straight alpha, so no application code changes; the renderer encodes it (Color::srgb) when it builds an instance, multiplies by alpha, and from there on blending, gradient interpolation, edge anti-aliasing, image filtering and picture compositing all operate on encoded values. The render target stores what the shader writes, u"},{"t":"0023 — Colours are composited as sRGB-encoded values, like the web platform; light text is weighted","h":"Linear light is not kept as an option","u":"architecture/0023-compositing-space.html#linear-light-is-not-kept-as-an-option","s":"Reference","x":"No consumer needs it: there is no lighting, no HDR, and no content authored for it. A second mode would double the pixel tests that are the renderer's specification and leave every application to discover which one its design assumes. If physically based blending is ever needed (photographic effects), it belongs in an explicit off-screen pass, not in a mode of the one pipeline."},{"t":"0023 — Colours are composited as sRGB-encoded values, like the web platform; light text is weighted","h":"Light text is weighted","u":"architecture/0023-compositing-space.html#light-text-is-weighted","s":"Reference","x":"With plain coverage composited in the encoded space, text is still not Chrome's. Measured on the references (ink summed over text regions): Chrome's text carries 8 to 18% more ink than the plain coverage of the same outlines, in both polarities, and far more at the edges of light text on dark (where plain coverage is 0.1 the reference has about 0.6; 0.3 gives 0.8; for dark text on white, 0.3 and 0.5). That is the bro"},{"t":"0023 — Colours are composited as sRGB-encoded values, like the web platform; light text is weighted","h":"What changed for application authors","u":"architecture/0023-compositing-space.html#what-changed-for-application-authors","s":"Reference","x":"Nothing in the API. Color, hex, gradients, shadows and images are written as before.Pictures change: translucent colours, shadows, gradients and anti-aliased edges now look as they do in the design. Write the design's own values: box-shadow alphas as given, stroke-opacity as an alpha, layers in the design's order. Workarounds for the old blend must be removed (both examples' were).Visual regression references made be"},{"t":"0023 — Colours are composited as sRGB-encoded values, like the web platform; light text is weighted","h":"Measured","u":"architecture/0023-compositing-space.html#measured","s":"Reference","x":"Share of pixels differing from the Chrome reference, default comparison, before this record and 0022 (with every hand correction in place) and after both (with the corrections deleted):ScreenBeforeAfterCallReady 01 welcome0.0685%0.0274%CallReady 02 home0.2064%0.0209%CallReady 03 live call0.0140%0.0064%CallReady 04 call report0.0965%0.0591%CallReady 05 premium0.0775%0.0147%FundiDesk 01 splash0.2868%0.2250%FundiDesk 02"},{"t":"0023 — Colours are composited as sRGB-encoded values, like the web platform; light text is weighted","h":"Not done","u":"architecture/0023-compositing-space.html#not-done","s":"Reference","x":"The launcher-icon generator in morningstar-devtools (icon.rs) still flattens artwork over a background in linear light. It is a separate compositor with its own tests and does not go through the renderer.A device run, and a look at light text on an Android phone next to Chrome on that phone."},{"t":"0025 — Text selection, caret keys, the clipboard and undo","h":"","u":"architecture/0025-text-selection-and-clipboard.html","s":"Reference","x":"0025 — Text selection, caret keys, the clipboard and undoStatus: Accepted (2026-11-12). Verified on the development host only (tests, and the selection looked at as pixels in both themes). Nothing here has run on a device: the Rust type-checks for Android and iOS, the Java compiles against the API 36 SDK, and no finger has long-pressed a field on a phone. The Swift has been parsed and never compiled. What that leaves"},{"t":"0025 — Text selection, caret keys, the clipboard and undo","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0025-text-selection-and-clipboard.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"A TextInput had a caret that a tap placed, and nothing else a person expects of a field: no way to select a word, correct the middle of a number without retyping it, paste a code from a message, or move with the arrow keys of a keyboard. On Android an input method could move its own selection in the host's copy of the text, and Rust ignored the half of it that made it a selection."},{"t":"0025 — Text selection, caret keys, the clipboard and undo","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0025-text-selection-and-clipboard.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":""},{"t":"0025 — Text selection, caret keys, the clipboard and undo","h":"The selection is the framework's; the application still sees only on_input(String)","u":"architecture/0025-text-selection-and-clipboard.html#the-selection-is-the-frameworks-the-application-still-sees-only-on_inputstring","s":"Reference","x":"Selection is transient widget state in Ui, next to the caret, by the identity rules of ADR 0013: EditState gained an anchor (the end of the selection that stays put; the caret is the end that moves). Nothing about it reaches the application, which keeps receiving the whole new string per change. Outside access is for tests and for an application that wants a field's contents selected: Ui::selection() -> Option<(usize"},{"t":"0025 — Text selection, caret keys, the clipboard and undo","h":"Gestures","u":"architecture/0025-text-selection-and-clipboard.html#gestures","s":"Reference","x":"Pointer events carry no time. The clock is the frames' (FrameInfo::elapsed), and a frame is drawn after every touch, so:GestureRecognisedDoesTapfinger up on the fieldcaret at the nearest character boundary; any selection collapses, handles and menu goDouble tapa second tap on the same field within 400 ms of frame time and 24 dpselects the word there (Unicode word boundaries, UAX #29, via unicode-segmentation); handle"},{"t":"0025 — Text selection, caret keys, the clipboard and undo","h":"What is drawn","u":"architecture/0025-text-selection-and-clipboard.html#what-is-drawn","s":"Reference","x":"Highlight: the theme's primary colour at 40% (Material's text-selection colour), behind the text, one rectangle per visual line and one per run on a line that mixes directions. The rectangles come from TextContext::selection_rects (new, the one addition to morningstar-text), built from the same caret map as caret and index_at, so they follow right-to-left runs and partial ligatures. Edges are snapped to whole pixels "},{"t":"0025 — Text selection, caret keys, the clipboard and undo","h":"Scrolling under a dragged handle","u":"architecture/0025-text-selection-and-clipboard.html#scrolling-under-a-dragged-handle","s":"Reference","x":"While a handle is held within 24 dp of an edge of what can scroll, or past it, the text moves under the finger at 60 dp/s at the start of that zone, rising with depth to 900 dp/s: first the field itself (a bounded multi-line field vertically, a single line wider than its box sideways), and when that has nowhere further to go, the scroll view around it (against the visible part of the viewport, the keyboard's top incl"},{"t":"0025 — Text selection, caret keys, the clipboard and undo","h":"Keys","u":"architecture/0025-text-selection-and-clipboard.html#keys","s":"Reference","x":"New TextInputEvents: Move { to: CaretMove, extend }, SelectAll, Copy, Cut, Paste, Undo, Redo. CaretMove is Left, Right, WordLeft, WordRight, Up, Down, LineStart, LineEnd, TextStart, TextEnd.Left and right are the reading direction's backward and forward; in a value that reads right to left the Left key moves on through the text. Without extend a selection collapses to the end the key points at.Moves by lines need the"},{"t":"0025 — Text selection, caret keys, the clipboard and undo","h":"The input method and the selection","u":"architecture/0025-text-selection-and-clipboard.html#the-input-method-and-the-selection","s":"Reference","x":"TextInputRequest and TextEditState each gained anchor: Option<usize>.In: nativeTextEdited already carried selectionStart and selectionEnd; Rust now reads both (Android's selection end is the caret). Engine::text_input records the anchor in what the host is believed to hold, so the field adopting it produces no echo.Out: onTextInputCommand takes selectionStart, selectionEnd in place of caret (descriptor (IIIIIILjava/l"},{"t":"0025 — Text selection, caret keys, the clipboard and undo","h":"The clipboard is a platform service","u":"architecture/0025-text-selection-and-clipboard.html#the-clipboard-is-a-platform-service","s":"Reference","x":"Two more ServiceRequests: SetClipboard(String) (not answered) and ReadClipboard, answered with ServiceResult::Clipboard(Option<String>). A field's copy and paste are requests the UI layer makes for itself through the same queue as the application's (EffectState::request), with no handler of the application's: Ui remembers the id of a paste and applies the answer to the field that asked, if it still has the focus. An "},{"t":"0025 — Text selection, caret keys, the clipboard and undo","h":"Undo and redo","u":"architecture/0025-text-selection-and-clipboard.html#undo-and-redo","s":"Reference","x":"A bounded history per focused field (History in edit.rs, 100 steps, oldest dropped): the value, caret and selection before each step. A run of typing is one step, and so is a run of backspaces; whitespace, a move of the caret, a paste, a cut and a replaced selection start a new one. Undo and Redo deliver the restored value through on_input like any edit. The history lives with the focus: leaving the field forgets it,"},{"t":"0025 — Text selection, caret keys, the clipboard and undo","h":"Alternatives considered","u":"architecture/0025-text-selection-and-clipboard.html#alternatives-considered","s":"Reference","x":"The platform's own selection UI (Android's ActionMode and handle drawables over the SurfaceView; UITextInteraction on iOS). Rejected: both need the platform to know where every character is, which means reporting text geometry to the host continuously (CursorAnchorInfo, the full UITextInput protocol), and the result would look native and move a frame behind the text the framework draws. The framework already has the "},{"t":"0025 — Text selection, caret keys, the clipboard and undo","h":"Consequences","u":"architecture/0025-text-selection-and-clipboard.html#consequences","s":"Reference","x":"The host interfaces grew, both halves together. JNI: 22 native methods (nativeTextMove, nativeSetReduceMotion of ADR 0026, nativeClipboardResult are new) and 5 upcalls; onTextInputCommand changed its signature, so a library and a host from different sides of this change show no keyboard (the lookup fails and says so in the log). C: morningstar_ios_text_command, morningstar_ios_text_move, morningstar_ios_clipboard_res"},{"t":"0025 — Text selection, caret keys, the clipboard and undo","h":"Limitations","u":"architecture/0025-text-selection-and-clipboard.html#limitations","s":"Reference","x":"No magnifier. Skipped: it needs the framebuffer under the finger drawn again, enlarged, which the display list cannot express without a second pass or a snapshot. Not trivial, so not built.No insertion handle: a caret alone cannot be dragged. Tap again, or long-press.A long press does not go on to extend the selection if the finger then moves; lift and use the handles.The menu's labels are English and fixed (MENU_LAB"},{"t":"0025 — Text selection, caret keys, the clipboard and undo","h":"Verified","u":"architecture/0025-text-selection-and-clipboard.html#verified","s":"Reference","x":"On the development host (macOS) only:Editing model: 12 new unit tests in crates/morningstar-ui/src/edit.rs (42 in all): ordering and snapping of a selection, typing over it in both directions, room freed in a full field, deletion (Arabic with marks), adoption of the platform's selection and its loss under a field rule, words (punctuation, apostrophes, decimals, Arabic, whitespace), word jumps, key moves and collapse,"},{"t":"0025 — Text selection, caret keys, the clipboard and undo","h":"Not verified","u":"architecture/0025-text-selection-and-clipboard.html#not-verified","s":"Reference","x":"Everything on an Android device. No APK was built. To try, in a field with a few words in it:double tap and long press select a word; the two handles and the menu appear where expected and are comfortable to hit; the long-press timing feels right against other apps;dragging each handle, across lines, and holding one at the edge of a long single-line field, of a bounded multi-line field (CallReady's reply) and of a sc"},{"t":"0026 — System-bar content colour and \"reduce motion\" from the platform","h":"","u":"architecture/0026-system-bars-and-reduce-motion.html","s":"Reference","x":"0026 — System-bar content colour and \"reduce motion\" from the platformStatus: Accepted (2026-11-12). Verified on the development host only. The Rust type-checks for Android and iOS and the Java compiles against the API 36 SDK; neither the bar icons nor the setting has been looked at on a device. The Swift has been parsed and never compiled.Closes the known defect \"Status-bar icon colour\" of the verification table (wh"},{"t":"0026 — System-bar content colour and \"reduce motion\" from the platform","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0026-system-bars-and-reduce-motion.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"The surface is drawn edge to edge under transparent system bars. The system draws its icons in one colour unless told otherwise, and it cannot see what the application paints behind them: FundiDesk, a light design, had white status-bar icons on a near-white screen.Separately, Ui::set_reduce_motion existed and nothing but tests called it."},{"t":"0026 — System-bar content colour and \"reduce motion\" from the platform","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0026-system-bars-and-reduce-motion.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":""},{"t":"0026 — System-bar content colour and \"reduce motion\" from the platform","h":"System bars: declared per frame, light or dark content","u":"architecture/0026-system-bars-and-reduce-motion.html#system-bars-declared-per-frame-light-or-dark-content","s":"Reference","x":"morningstar-core has SystemBars { status: BarContent, navigation: BarContent } with BarContent::{Light, Dark} (the colour of the icons, not of the screen), SystemBars::LIGHT_CONTENT, SystemBars::DARK_CONTENT, and SystemBars::for_background(color): dark icons when the colour's relative luminance (WCAG's, on the linear components a Color already holds) is above 0.179, the point where black and white text contrast equal"},{"t":"0026 — System-bar content colour and \"reduce motion\" from the platform","h":"Reduce motion: from the platform into FrameInfo","u":"architecture/0026-system-bars-and-reduce-motion.html#reduce-motion-from-the-platform-into-frameinfo","s":"Reference","x":"Engine::set_reduce_motion(bool) (a change produces one frame, a repeat nothing) and FrameInfo::reduce_motion. Ui uses forced || info.reduce_motion every frame, where forced is what Ui::set_reduce_motion(true) sets: the system's setting is followed without the application doing anything, and an application's own \"less motion\" switch can still force it on. Viewport::reduce_motion tells a view, as before.Android: native"},{"t":"0026 — System-bar content colour and \"reduce motion\" from the platform","h":"Alternatives considered","u":"architecture/0026-system-bars-and-reduce-motion.html#alternatives-considered","s":"Reference","x":"The host samples the frame under each bar and decides. Works without the application, costs a read-back per change, and is wrong while a screen slides in.Bars as a property of the theme (Theme::system_bars). The default already comes from the theme; a field would have to be set by every theme literal, and a screen that differs from its theme would still need the override.A scrim: translucent bar backgrounds so that e"},{"t":"0026 — System-bar content colour and \"reduce motion\" from the platform","h":"Consequences","u":"architecture/0026-system-bars-and-reduce-motion.html#consequences","s":"Reference","x":"JNI: nativeSetReduceMotion and the onSystemBars upcall; C: morningstar_ios_set_reduce_motion, morningstar_ios_take_system_bars, MorningstarSystemBars. A host older than the library is told nothing about the bars and says so once in the log; a library older than the host fails to link nativeSetReduceMotion (an UnsatisfiedLinkError at start): both halves ship together, as before.Application has one more defaulted metho"},{"t":"0026 — System-bar content colour and \"reduce motion\" from the platform","h":"Limitations","u":"architecture/0026-system-bars-and-reduce-motion.html#limitations","s":"Reference","x":"One colour per bar, for the whole bar. No per-region contrast.Android 23 to 25 navigation bar and everything below 23 keep light icons; an application with a light bottom edge there can pad it with a darker bar itself.Three-button navigation with a translucent system scrim (the default on some devices when contrast is enforced) is left as the platform draws it.Flings are not reduced (ADR 0021): they follow the finger"},{"t":"0026 — System-bar content colour and \"reduce motion\" from the platform","h":"Verified","u":"architecture/0026-system-bars-and-reduce-motion.html#verified","s":"Reference","x":"On the development host (macOS) only:SystemBars::for_background: white, black, mid grey, saturated blue, yellow, NaN (1 test in morningstar-core).Engine: the host is told once per change and a silent frame leaves the bars alone; reduce motion reaches FrameInfo, a change produces exactly one frame and a repeat none (2 tests in engine.rs).UI: the bars follow the light and the dark theme and the application's override, "},{"t":"0026 — System-bar content colour and \"reduce motion\" from the platform","h":"Not verified","u":"architecture/0026-system-bars-and-reduce-motion.html#not-verified","s":"Reference","x":"On an Android device: that FundiDesk shows dark status-bar and navigation icons and CallReady light ones, at launch and after the activity is recreated (rotate with auto-rotate on, or change the display size); that a theme change at run time changes them; gesture navigation and three-button navigation; that the log line system bars: status icons dark, navigation icons dark appears once and not per frame.Reduce motion"},{"t":"0027 — SVG files loaded at run time: a strict reader for a static subset, drawn through the vector-picture path","h":"","u":"architecture/0027-svg-loading.html","s":"Reference","x":"0027 — SVG files loaded at run time: a strict reader for a static subset, drawn through the vector-picture pathStatus: Accepted (2026-11-12). Verified on the development host only (macOS; the rasteriser is CPU code, the two draw paths are the image path and the glyph atlas of earlier records). Nothing here has run on a device and nothing here has been timed.Extends 0015 (one-colour icons) and 0017 (multi-colour pictu"},{"t":"0027 — SVG files loaded at run time: a strict reader for a static subset, drawn through the vector-picture path","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0027-svg-loading.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"Every illustration and most icons of the two reproduced designs were transcribed by hand from the design's SVG into Layers: repeating a group's attributes on each layer, folding stroke-opacity into a colour, combining transforms. It is slow, it is where mistakes come from, and a designer's file cannot simply be dropped in. VectorIcon::svg reads markup, but only one colour, four element kinds and no groups, transforms"},{"t":"0027 — SVG files loaded at run time: a strict reader for a static subset, drawn through the vector-picture path","h":"Decisions","u":"architecture/0027-svg-loading.html#decisions","s":"Reference","x":""},{"t":"0027 — SVG files loaded at run time: a strict reader for a static subset, drawn through the vector-picture path","h":"An owned picture parsed at run time, by a reader written here","u":"architecture/0027-svg-loading.html#an-owned-picture-parsed-at-run-time-by-a-reader-written-here","s":"Reference","x":"morningstar-text gains SvgPicture::parse(&str) -> Result<SvgPicture, SvgError>. The result is a shared handle (Arc), cheap to clone, to a tree of shapes with their transforms resolved, the gradients and the clips. It is rasterised by the compositor VectorPicture already uses (picture.rs's canvas, which gained gradient inks, clips of several shapes and group compositing), registered as an image and cached by Frame::dr"},{"t":"0027 — SVG files loaded at run time: a strict reader for a static subset, drawn through the vector-picture path","h":"The subset, and that everything else is an error","u":"architecture/0027-svg-loading.html#the-subset-and-that-everything-else-is-an-error","s":"Reference","x":"Read: svg (viewBox, width, height), g, defs, path, rect (rx, ry), circle, ellipse, line, polyline, polygon, clipPath (shapes, user space, each with its transform and clip-rule), linearGradient and radialGradient with stops (gradientUnits both ways, gradientTransform, spreadMethod, a focal point, href to another gradient for stops and attributes, percentages); fill, stroke, fill-opacity, stroke-opacity, opacity, fill-"},{"t":"0027 — SVG files loaded at run time: a strict reader for a static subset, drawn through the vector-picture path","h":"Where the semantics are SVG's and where they are not","u":"architecture/0027-svg-loading.html#where-the-semantics-are-svgs-and-where-they-are-not","s":"Reference","x":"Group opacity and group clips are done as groups. A g with opacity or clip-path and more than one painted child is composited into a canvas of its own and then faded and cut as one, so overlapping children do not show through each other, as in a browser. With one child the opacity and the clip fold into the child (the same result, without the canvas); with neither, the group disappears into its children. Each nested "},{"t":"0027 — SVG files loaded at run time: a strict reader for a static subset, drawn through the vector-picture path","h":"currentColor, and icons that take a colour","u":"architecture/0027-svg-loading.html#currentcolor-and-icons-that-take-a-colour","s":"Reference","x":"currentColor is the colour given with SvgPicture::with_current_color (a cheap clone of the handle; black by default, as on a page that sets no colour), unless a color attribute in the file sets it. The current colour is part of the cache key only for files that use it.A file is single-ink when everything in it is painted in one colour at whatever opacities: only currentColor, or only one literal colour (the usual fil"},{"t":"0027 — SVG files loaded at run time: a strict reader for a static subset, drawn through the vector-picture path","h":"Parse once: where the value lives","u":"architecture/0027-svg-loading.html#parse-once-where-the-value-lives","s":"Reference","x":"Parsing is proportional to the file's length and allocates the tree; it must not happen per frame. Two places to keep the result: the application's own state, for a file that arrives at run time; and SvgAsset, a static-friendly holder (const fn new(&'static str), a OnceLock inside) for a file bundled with include_str!: get() parses on first use, from any thread, and returns the same picture afterwards. A file that do"},{"t":"0027 — SVG files loaded at run time: a strict reader for a static subset, drawn through the vector-picture path","h":"Untrusted input","u":"architecture/0027-svg-loading.html#untrusted-input","s":"Reference","x":"A file may come from a server. The reader and the rasteriser are bounded:BoundValueSource length4 MiBElement nesting64 (the build recursion is therefore at most 64 deep)Elements20,000Attributes on one element128Path commands in the whole file, after arcs become curves200,000Magnitude of any coordinate or length1,000,000; of a composed matrix entry, 10^9Gradient stops; dash array entries256; 64Dashes along one path (c"},{"t":"0027 — SVG files loaded at run time: a strict reader for a static subset, drawn through the vector-picture path","h":"Costs","u":"architecture/0027-svg-loading.html#costs","s":"Reference","x":"Memory. A parsed picture holds its path commands (20 bytes each) and a few words per shape; the 28 inline SVGs of the CallReady design are between 1 and 40 shapes each. While rasterising: 16 bytes per pixel for the canvas (unchanged from 0017), one byte per pixel for each of up to five coverage buffers (three before; the two new ones are allocated only for a clip made of several shapes or for two clips on one shape),"},{"t":"0027 — SVG files loaded at run time: a strict reader for a static subset, drawn through the vector-picture path","h":"Proof","u":"architecture/0027-svg-loading.html#proof","s":"Reference","x":"The design's own SVGs against the hand transcriptions (examples/callready/tests/svg.rs). The inline SVGs of 02-home.html (the disc and both ring sets), 01-welcome.html (waveform, underline) and 03-live-call.html (avatar disc) are extracted from the HTML (the export's sc-camel-view-box put back to viewBox and the root's page-layout style removed, in the test), loaded, and compared byte for byte with the VectorPictures"},{"t":"0027 — SVG files loaded at run time: a strict reader for a static subset, drawn through the vector-picture path","h":"Not done","u":"architecture/0027-svg-loading.html#not-done","s":"Reference","x":"use, symbol, text, mask, filter, pattern, image, CSS in style elements, paint-order, vector-effect, markers, clipPathUnits=\"objectBoundingBox\", a clip-path inside a clipPath. use is the one a designer's file is most likely to need.No per-shape bounding boxes in the compositor: each shape still costs a pass over the whole image. Fine for illustrations of tens of shapes; a file of thousands is slow.No comparison agains"},{"t":"0027 — SVG files loaded at run time: a strict reader for a static subset, drawn through the vector-picture path","h":"Public API added","u":"architecture/0027-svg-loading.html#public-api-added","s":"Reference","x":"morningstar-text: SvgPicture (parse, parse_lenient, width, height, view_box, with_current_color, uses_current_color, is_single_ink, content_hash, rasterise, rasterise_mask), SvgAsset (new, get, check), SvgError, SvgErrorKind, RasterPicture, MAX_SVG_BYTES; TextContext::draw_svg_icon.morningstar-runtime: Frame::draw_picture takes &impl RasterPicture (source-compatible: a &VectorPicture still works); Frame::draw_svg_ico"},{"t":"0028 — Group opacity: display-list layers drawn into an offscreen target, used only where a faded group overlaps itself","h":"","u":"architecture/0028-group-opacity-layers.html","s":"Reference","x":"0028 — Group opacity: display-list layers drawn into an offscreen target, used only where a faded group overlaps itselfStatus: Accepted (2026-11-12). Verified on the development host only (macOS, Metal, offscreen). Nothing here has run on a device, on Vulkan or on GLES, and nothing here has been timed.Supersedes the \"opacity is multiplied into every colour drawn\" decision of 0021 and its \"no group opacity\" limit. Ext"},{"t":"0028 — Group opacity: display-list layers drawn into an offscreen target, used only where a faded group overlaps itself","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0028-group-opacity-layers.html#context","s":"Reference","x":".opacity(x), and the opacity of every transition, were multiplied into each colour of the subtree. For one primitive that is exact. For a card with a label it is not: at 50% the card's background shows through the label, the card through anything laid over it, a shadow through the box that casts it. 0021 recorded this as right for a quick fade and wrong for holding a group at 50%, and left the offscreen layer out."},{"t":"0028 — Group opacity: display-list layers drawn into an offscreen target, used only where a faded group overlaps itself","h":"Decisions","u":"architecture/0028-group-opacity-layers.html#decisions","s":"Reference","x":""},{"t":"0028 — Group opacity: display-list layers drawn into an offscreen target, used only where a faded group overlaps itself","h":"PushLayer { opacity } / PopLayer in the display list","u":"architecture/0028-group-opacity-layers.html#pushlayer--opacity---poplayer-in-the-display-list","s":"Reference","x":"DisplayList::push_layer(opacity) and pop_layer() bracket a group. What is between them is drawn as it would be without them (clips and transforms open around the layer apply to its content; those pushed inside must be popped inside), composed by itself, and then shown at opacity. Layers nest. An opacity of 1 or more is no layer at all; 0, negative or NaN draws nothing until the pop, at no cost; a pop without a push i"},{"t":"0028 — Group opacity: display-list layers drawn into an offscreen target, used only where a faded group overlaps itself","h":"The renderer: one target per nesting depth, passes split at layer boundaries","u":"architecture/0028-group-opacity-layers.html#the-renderer-one-target-per-nesting-depth-passes-split-at-layer-boundaries","s":"Reference","x":"A layer is drawn into an offscreen target of the surface's size and format, cleared to transparent. Because the target has the surface's size, the content's instances are exactly the ones it would have had: same positions, same clips, same transforms, same shader. Nothing is re-laid-out for the layer.When the layer ends, the target is blended into its parent (the frame, or the layer around it) as one instance of the "},{"t":"0028 — Group opacity: display-list layers drawn into an offscreen target, used only where a faded group overlaps itself","h":"A frame without layers is rendered exactly as before","u":"architecture/0028-group-opacity-layers.html#a-frame-without-layers-is-rendered-exactly-as-before","s":"Reference","x":"With no PushLayer in the list the pass structure is one pass with the same commands in the same order: clear, pipeline, globals, and per run the image bind group, the instance slice and the draw. The instance layout, the shader and the pipeline are unchanged; there is no new uniform and no per-instance field. Tested: such a frame reports one pass and one draw call, a layer at opacity 1 gives the same statistics and t"},{"t":"0028 — Group opacity: display-list layers drawn into an offscreen target, used only where a faded group overlaps itself","h":"The UI layer: a layer only where the faded widget overlaps itself","u":"architecture/0028-group-opacity-layers.html#the-ui-layer-a-layer-only-where-the-faded-widget-overlaps-itself","s":"Reference","x":".opacity(x) and transitions now mean group opacity. The rule that decides how it is drawn, evaluated per faded widget per frame (overlaps_itself in ui.rs):A layer, when the widget's opacity is below 1 and it paints things that lie over one another: a box with a background or border and something painted inside it; a box over its own shadow; a stack with more than one painted child; or any such box further down the su"},{"t":"0028 — Group opacity: display-list layers drawn into an offscreen target, used only where a faded group overlaps itself","h":"Costs","u":"architecture/0028-group-opacity-layers.html#costs","s":"Reference","x":"Memory: width * height * 4 bytes of GPU texture per nesting depth in use, only in frames that have layers: 10.4 MB for one level on a 1080 x 2400 surface. None otherwise. No change to instance size (144 bytes), the atlas or image textures.GPU work per layer: a clear of the target, the group's own instances (which would have been drawn anyway), and one blended quad the size of the group's bounding rectangle. On a tile"},{"t":"0028 — Group opacity: display-list layers drawn into an offscreen target, used only where a faded group overlaps itself","h":"Not done","u":"architecture/0028-group-opacity-layers.html#not-done","s":"Reference","x":"Layer targets are the size of the surface, not of the group. A target the size of the group's bounds would save memory and fill for small groups, at the cost of re-basing every instance inside it.No blend modes, no backdrop effects, no masks: a layer is opacity only.The overlap rule does not look at geometry. Two siblings moved over each other by offset or a transform inside a row are not seen as overlapping; a card "},{"t":"0028 — Group opacity: display-list layers drawn into an offscreen target, used only where a faded group overlaps itself","h":"Public API added","u":"architecture/0028-group-opacity-layers.html#public-api-added","s":"Reference","x":"morningstar-core: DrawOp::{PushLayer, PopLayer}; DisplayList::{push_layer, pop_layer}.morningstar-gpu: RenderStats::{passes, layer_textures, layer_texture_bytes}. No wgpu type appears in the public API.morningstar-ui: no new API; .opacity() and transitions changed meaning as above."},{"t":"0028 — Group opacity: display-list layers drawn into an offscreen target, used only where a faded group overlaps itself","h":"Verified","u":"architecture/0028-group-opacity-layers.html#verified","s":"Reference","x":"crates/morningstar-gpu/tests/layers.rs (11 pixel tests, on Metal): two overlapping rectangles in a layer at 50% show the upper one alone in the overlap ([0, 0, 128]), where colour by colour gives [64, 0, 128]; a frame without layers is one pass and one draw call, and a layer at opacity 1 is byte-identical to none; passes, instances and targets with one layer, with two side by side (one target), and none the frame aft"},{"t":"0029 — Turned text: glyphs rasterised for their transform and placed in screen space, unless the transform is changing","h":"","u":"architecture/0029-turned-text.html","s":"Reference","x":"0029 — Turned text: glyphs rasterised for their transform and placed in screen space, unless the transform is changingStatus: Accepted (2026-11-12). Verified on the development host only (macOS, Metal, offscreen). Nothing here has run on a device and nothing here has been timed.Closes the \"rotated text is a resampled bitmap\" approximation of 0017 for text. Extends 0009 (glyphs as display-list operations), 0022 (sub-p"},{"t":"0029 — Turned text: glyphs rasterised for their transform and placed in screen space, unless the transform is changing","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0029-turned-text.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"A transform is carried per instance and applied by the GPU (0017). For a rectangle that is exact: the shape is evaluated in its own space. A glyph is a bitmap, rasterised upright; turned, it is that bitmap resampled bilinearly, softer than a browser's text, which rasterises the outline at its angle. On CallReady, a design full of tilted labels, that softness was most of what still differed from the Chrome references "},{"t":"0029 — Turned text: glyphs rasterised for their transform and placed in screen space, unless the transform is changing","h":"Decisions","u":"architecture/0029-turned-text.html#decisions","s":"Reference","x":""},{"t":"0029 — Turned text: glyphs rasterised for their transform and placed in screen space, unless the transform is changing","h":"The text engine rasterises for the transform it is drawn through","u":"architecture/0029-turned-text.html#the-text-engine-rasterises-for-the-transform-it-is-drawn-through","s":"Reference","x":"DisplayList now knows the transform an operation recorded at this moment will be drawn through (current_transform(): the composition of the open push_transforms, kept as they are pushed and popped). TextContext::draw_spans reads it. If it does more than move (it turns, slants or scales), each glyph is:rasterised from its outline through the transform's linear part (zeno, the rasteriser the upright glyphs come from, o"},{"t":"0029 — Turned text: glyphs rasterised for their transform and placed in screen space, unless the transform is changing","h":"PushScreenSpace in the renderer, without touching the shader","u":"architecture/0029-turned-text.html#pushscreenspace-in-the-renderer-without-touching-the-shader","s":"Reference","x":"Inside it the open transform is set aside (and restored by the pop, with the clip, like a transform's own pop). Clips stay in force. A clip in screen space is exact, as always. A clip that was pushed inside a turning transform is kept in that turned space (0017) and cannot be evaluated for an untransformed instance, so for screen-space operations it acts as its bounding rectangle on screen: never cutting more than th"},{"t":"0029 — Turned text: glyphs rasterised for their transform and placed in screen space, unless the transform is changing","h":"While the transform changes, resample; then one frame to settle","u":"architecture/0029-turned-text.html#while-the-transform-changes-resample-then-one-frame-to-settle","s":"Reference","x":"Rasterising for the angle means a new set of masks per angle. An animated rotation would put a text's worth of glyphs in the atlas every frame, each used once, until the atlas fills and is rebuilt. So:A text is rasterised for its transform unless the same text (the same layout) was drawn in the previous frame through other linear parts and not this one. That is a text whose angle or scale is changing. For that frame "},{"t":"0029 — Turned text: glyphs rasterised for their transform and placed in screen space, unless the transform is changing","h":"Results","u":"architecture/0029-turned-text.html#results-1","s":"Reference","x":"CallReady, share of pixels that differ from the Chrome reference (780 x 1688, default tolerance), before and after:ScreenBeforeAfterLimit in tests/fidelity.rs01 welcome0.0274%0.0095%0.05 (unchanged)02 home0.0209%0.0135%0.05 (unchanged)03 live call0.0064%0.0060%0.05 (unchanged)04 call report0.0591%0.0374%0.05 (was 0.10)05 premium0.0147%0.0076%0.05 (unchanged)Mean error fell on every screen with turned text (0.020 to 0"},{"t":"0029 — Turned text: glyphs rasterised for their transform and placed in screen space, unless the transform is changing","h":"Costs","u":"architecture/0029-turned-text.html#costs","s":"Reference","x":"Atlas: a turned glyph can have sixteen masks (four by four pen fractions) where an upright one has four, per tone and per distinct angle. Measured on the five CallReady screens at 2 and 2.8125 px per dp: the atlas texture is the same size on every screen (1024 x 512 or 1024 x 1024 bytes), with 0 to 38 more rows of it in use (157 to 187 of 512 on the report at 2x, the largest change).CPU: one outline fill per new (gly"},{"t":"0029 — Turned text: glyphs rasterised for their transform and placed in screen space, unless the transform is changing","h":"Not done","u":"architecture/0029-turned-text.html#not-done","s":"Reference","x":"Turned icons and pictures are still resampled: a VectorIcon mask and a picture's image are rasterised upright. The same method applies (their paths are at hand) and was left out.Outlined text (draw_text_outlined) and the caret, selection and composing underline of a turned text field are untouched; the text of a turned field is sharp, its decorations are rectangles and exact already.Exact clipping of screen-space gly"},{"t":"0029 — Turned text: glyphs rasterised for their transform and placed in screen space, unless the transform is changing","h":"Public API added","u":"architecture/0029-turned-text.html#public-api-added","s":"Reference","x":"morningstar-core: DrawOp::{PushScreenSpace, PopScreenSpace}; DisplayList::{push_screen_space, pop_screen_space, current_transform}.morningstar-text: TextContext::take_settle_request. draw and draw_spans keep their signatures.morningstar-runtime: none; Frame::draw_text and draw_text_spans request a redraw when the text engine asks."},{"t":"0029 — Turned text: glyphs rasterised for their transform and placed in screen space, unless the transform is changing","h":"Verified","u":"architecture/0029-turned-text.html#verified","s":"Reference","x":"crates/morningstar-text/tests/turned.rs (8): upright and moved text is recorded exactly as before; an \"l\" turned a quarter lies on its side with the same ink, on whole pixels, where the transform puts it; a stem at 8 degrees has a solid middle between two graded pixels; glyphs step along a 30-degree baseline and take more than one mask for their fractions; a scale rasterises at the scaled size and the size limit and "},{"t":"0030 — Launcher icons are flattened on sRGB-encoded values","h":"","u":"architecture/0030-launcher-icons-flattened-in-the-encoded-space.html","s":"Reference","x":"0030 — Launcher icons are flattened on sRGB-encoded valuesStatus: Accepted (2026-11-12). Verified in tests on the development host; no icon was installed on a device.Amends one detail of 0020 (how the icon generator flattens transparency) to agree with 0023. It changes nothing else of 0020."},{"t":"0030 — Launcher icons are flattened on sRGB-encoded values","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0030-launcher-icons-flattened-in-the-encoded-space.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"Where the icon generator puts a translucent pixel over an opaque colour (the built-in star on its field, an adaptive foreground over its background for the legacy and round icons, a transparent source for the opaque iOS icon, the preview sheet), it blended in linear light, \"as the renderer does\". Since 0023 the renderer does not: it composites sRGB-encoded values, as browsers, design tools and both platforms' launche"},{"t":"0030 — Launcher icons are flattened on sRGB-encoded values","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0030-launcher-icons-flattened-in-the-encoded-space.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":"over in tools/morningstar-devtools/src/icon.rs blends the encoded bytes: (pixel * alpha + under * (255 - alpha) + 127) / 255 per channel. Half-transparent white over black is byte 128 (it was 188).Resizing (Image::resized) still filters in linear light with premultiplied alpha. That is resampling, not compositing: averaging light is what makes a reduced image look like the original from further away, and 0023 leaves "},{"t":"0030 — Launcher icons are flattened on sRGB-encoded values","h":"What changed in generated files","u":"architecture/0030-launcher-icons-flattened-in-the-encoded-space.html#what-changed-in-generated-files","s":"Reference","x":"Compared byte for byte, before and after, for the two icons this repository has:CallReady (examples/callready/assets/icon.png and icon-foreground.png): all 22 generated Android and iOS files are identical. Its icon is opaque and its foreground is handed to Android as it is. Only the --preview sheet differs.The built-in icon (the star, for an app that declares none): the legacy ic_launcher.png and ic_launcher_round.pn"},{"t":"0030 — Launcher icons are flattened on sRGB-encoded values","h":"Verified","u":"architecture/0030-launcher-icons-flattened-in-the-encoded-space.html#verified","s":"Reference","x":"the_ios_icon_is_1024_opaque_and_flattened_onto_the_background expects byte 128; the 81 unit tests and 4 CLI tests of morningstar-devtools that cover icons pass."},{"t":"0031 — The shaping cache is keyed on everything but colour","h":"","u":"architecture/0031-shaping-cache-without-colour.html","s":"Reference","x":"0031 — The shaping cache is keyed on everything but colourStatus: Accepted (2026-11-12). Verified in tests on the development host. Not timed.Removes a cost recorded in 0021 (\"fading rich text with coloured spans reshapes every frame\"). Extends 0009 and 0015 (the layout cache, rich text). It changes no decision of theirs."},{"t":"0031 — The shaping cache is keyed on everything but colour","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0031-shaping-cache-without-colour.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"A shaped paragraph is cached under a hash of what it was shaped from. That hash included each span's colour, and each cached glyph carried its span's colour. Opacity is multiplied into colours (0021; still so for text, 0028), so a paragraph with a coloured span had a different key in every frame of a fade and was shaped again each time, the expensive step the cache exists to avoid. A paragraph without coloured spans "},{"t":"0031 — The shaping cache is keyed on everything but colour","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0031-shaping-cache-without-colour.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":"Span colours are not hashed. A cached glyph records which span it came from, and its colour is looked up in the spans given to the draw call. Shaping depends on the text, the division into spans, weights, families, italics, size, line height, alignment, letter spacing, tabular numbers and the width; exactly those remain in the key.Two paragraphs that differ only in span colours now share one cache entry, which is cor"},{"t":"0031 — The shaping cache is keyed on everything but colour","h":"Cost","u":"architecture/0031-shaping-cache-without-colour.html#cost","s":"Reference","x":"A glyph's colour costs an index into the spans per glyph per draw instead of a field read. The cached glyph is sixteen bytes smaller (a u32 in place of an Option<Color> of 20)."},{"t":"0031 — The shaping cache is keyed on everything but colour","h":"Verified","u":"architecture/0031-shaping-cache-without-colour.html#verified","s":"Reference","x":"crates/morningstar-text/tests/shaping_cache.rs (2): ten frames of a paragraph with a coloured span fading shape it once, with every glyph in that frame's colour and in the same place; a span colour appearing or going does not reshape, and weight, size, text and the division into spans do. The text crate's other tests and every fidelity figure are unchanged."},{"t":"ADR 0032: Accessibility: a semantics tree derived from the widgets, mirrored by the hosts","h":"","u":"architecture/0032-accessibility.html","s":"Reference","x":"ADR 0032: Accessibility: a semantics tree derived from the widgets, mirrored by the hostsStatus: Accepted. Tested on the development host; nothing in it has been tried with TalkBack or VoiceOver, and the iOS host is not compiled."},{"t":"ADR 0032: Accessibility: a semantics tree derived from the widgets, mirrored by the hosts","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0032-accessibility.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"Morningstar draws every pixel itself on one surface (ADR 0003, 0013). To the platform's assistive technology an app is therefore one opaque view: TalkBack says nothing, a blind person cannot use the app at all, and it fails any accessibility review. Native toolkits give each widget a node the screen reader can land on; a framework that owns its pixels has to describe them.What was wanted: that a Morningstar screen re"},{"t":"ADR 0032: Accessibility: a semantics tree derived from the widgets, mirrored by the hosts","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0032-accessibility.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":""},{"t":"ADR 0032: Accessibility: a semantics tree derived from the widgets, mirrored by the hosts","h":"1. A semantics tree, plain data in core","u":"architecture/0032-accessibility.html#1-a-semantics-tree-plain-data-in-core","s":"Reference","x":"morningstar-core::SemanticsTree is the nodes a screen reader can land on, in reading order, which is also depth-first order (a node is followed by everything inside it; the first node is the root and stands for the surface). A SemanticsNode has:id: the widget's identity (ADR 0013: its explicit .id(..) or its place in the tree), so a node is the same node from frame to frame and the reader's focus stays on it;bounds: "},{"t":"ADR 0032: Accessibility: a semantics tree derived from the widgets, mirrored by the hosts","h":"2. Derived from the frame that was painted, in the UI layer","u":"architecture/0032-accessibility.html#2-derived-from-the-frame-that-was-painted-in-the-ui-layer","s":"Reference","x":"The painter already visits every element with its rectangle, its scroll translation, its clip, its transform and its opacity. When a frame is to be described it also notes, per element, where it ended up on the screen and what it is (crates/morningstar-ui/src/semantics.rs); the tree is built from those notes after painting. Nothing is laid out or measured twice, and the bounds are by construction where the pixels are"},{"t":"ADR 0032: Accessibility: a semantics tree derived from the widgets, mirrored by the hosts","h":"3. Built only when asked for","u":"architecture/0032-accessibility.html#3-built-only-when-asked-for","s":"Reference","x":"FrameInfo::accessibility says that assistive technology is on; hosts set it through Engine::set_accessibility from the platform's own setting (below). The UI layer describes a frame when it is set, or when a test called Ui::set_accessibility(true). Otherwise the cost of accessibility to a frame is one branch per element painted, and nothing is collected, built or kept: crates/morningstar-ui/tests/semantics_alloc.rs c"},{"t":"ADR 0032: Accessibility: a semantics tree derived from the widgets, mirrored by the hosts","h":"4. To the host: only what changed, in one buffer","u":"architecture/0032-accessibility.html#4-to-the-host-only-what-changed-in-one-buffer","s":"Reference","x":"App::semantics() returns the tree of the last frame, if it was described; Ui implements it. After each frame the engine (Engine::take_semantics_update) compares that tree with the copy of what the host holds and, if they differ, appends an update to a byte buffer: the ids of nodes that went, and every node that is new or changed, each with a byte saying what changed (bounds, label, value, state, children, scroll, sel"},{"t":"ADR 0032: Accessibility: a semantics tree derived from the widgets, mirrored by the hosts","h":"5. Android: a virtual view hierarchy","u":"architecture/0032-accessibility.html#5-android-a-virtual-view-hierarchy","s":"Reference","x":"MorningstarAccessibility (new, beside MorningstarActivity) is the AccessibilityNodeProvider of the host view.The tree crosses once per change: the render thread calls MorningstarNative.onSemanticsUpdate(byte[]), which posts the array to the main thread; applyUpdate decodes it into a static table of nodes. Every query of the accessibility framework (createAccessibilityNodeInfo, findFocus) is answered from that table o"},{"t":"ADR 0032: Accessibility: a semantics tree derived from the widgets, mirrored by the hosts","h":"6. iOS: accessibility elements (not compiled)","u":"architecture/0032-accessibility.html#6-ios-accessibility-elements-not-compiled","s":"Reference","x":"The same buffer through morningstar_ios_take_semantics, actions through morningstar_ios_accessibility_action, on and off through morningstar_ios_set_accessibility (isVoiceOverRunning or isSwitchControlRunning, and their notifications). HostAccessibility.swift keeps the tree and vends one UIAccessibilityElement per node that says or does something as the Metal view's accessibilityElements, in the tree's order: label, "},{"t":"ADR 0032: Accessibility: a semantics tree derived from the widgets, mirrored by the hosts","h":"7. Testing, a checker and a picture","u":"architecture/0032-accessibility.html#7-testing-a-checker-and-a-picture","s":"Reference","x":"ui.set_accessibility(true), then ui.semantics() is the tree of the last frame, with find_by_label, find(role, label), find_containing, with_role, children, spoken(). ui.perform(id, Action::Activate) does what TalkBack's double tap does, through the same code; ui.activate(\"Save\") is the short form. Asking for the tree of a frame that was not described panics, so that a test asserting \"no issues\" cannot pass without ha"},{"t":"ADR 0032: Accessibility: a semantics tree derived from the widgets, mirrored by the hosts","h":"Consequences","u":"architecture/0032-accessibility.html#consequences","s":"Reference","x":"An app built from the stock widgets is readable without change; what it must add is a label for each control that is only a picture, and the checker says which.The tree is rebuilt whole on every described frame and compared whole. For a few hundred nodes that is small beside painting; during a fling every row's bounds change and every frame sends them. No throttling was added without a measurement to justify it.Eleme"},{"t":"ADR 0032: Accessibility: a semantics tree derived from the widgets, mirrored by the hosts","h":"Known gaps","u":"architecture/0032-accessibility.html#known-gaps","s":"Reference","x":"Font scale. The scale the hosts report is density only; Android's \"font size\" and iOS's Dynamic Type are ignored, so text does not grow for a user who asked for larger text. It would take a second number from the hosts (Configuration.fontScale, UIFontMetrics) in FrameInfo, multiplied into text sizes (not box sizes) in the UI layer, and screens that hold when text is 1.3 to 2 times larger, which the designed examples "},{"t":"ADR 0032: Accessibility: a semantics tree derived from the widgets, mirrored by the hosts","h":"Not verified","u":"architecture/0032-accessibility.html#not-verified","s":"Reference","x":"Everything on an Android device. No APK was built and TalkBack has never read a Morningstar screen. The Rust type-checks for Android, the Java compiles against API 36 with every lint as an error, and the JNI descriptors match; that is all. docs/architecture/README.md has the checklist for the owner.Whether TalkBack speaks a hint given as part of a content description the way intended, announces a live region's change"},{"t":"0033 — Radial gradients: one instance per pair of stops in the quad pipeline; the instance grows to 160 bytes","h":"","u":"architecture/0033-radial-gradients.html","s":"Reference","x":"0033 — Radial gradients: one instance per pair of stops in the quad pipeline; the instance grows to 160 bytesStatus: Accepted (2026-11-13). Verified on the development host only (macOS, Metal, offscreen). Nothing here has run on a device and nothing here has been timed.Extends 0011 (the drawing vocabulary and its one draw call) and 0017 (the instance layout). Colours are interpolated as 0023 says. It changes no decis"},{"t":"0033 — Radial gradients: one instance per pair of stops in the quad pipeline; the instance grows to 160 bytes","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0033-radial-gradients.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"The Atlas design (examples/atlas) is built on CSS radial-gradient: 51 uses in ten pages. A full-screen wash behind the content, a glass orb made of four gradients stacked in one box (three translucent spots over a four-stop body), a glow and a highlight that are ellipses fading to nothing. The renderer had a two-stop linear gradient as a box fill and radial gradients only inside vector pictures, which are rasterised "},{"t":"0033 — Radial gradients: one instance per pair of stops in the quad pipeline; the instance grows to 160 bytes","h":"Decisions","u":"architecture/0033-radial-gradients.html#decisions","s":"Reference","x":""},{"t":"0033 — Radial gradients: one instance per pair of stops in the quad pipeline; the instance grows to 160 bytes","h":"A radial gradient is a quad fill, evaluated per pixel","u":"architecture/0033-radial-gradients.html#a-radial-gradient-is-a-quad-fill-evaluated-per-pixel","s":"Reference","x":"DisplayList::fill_radial_gradient_rect(rect, corner_radius, RadialGradient { center, radius_x, radius_y, stops }, blur) fills a rounded rectangle. The gradient is CSS's: the colour at a point depends on where it lies between the centre and the ending ellipse (radius_x, radius_y; equal for a circle), the first stop's colour inside its offset, the last stop's beyond its offset, premultiplied sRGB-encoded mixing between"},{"t":"0033 — Radial gradients: one instance per pair of stops in the quad pipeline; the instance grows to 160 bytes","h":"Any number of stops, as one instance per pair of neighbouring stops","u":"architecture/0033-radial-gradients.html#any-number-of-stops-as-one-instance-per-pair-of-neighbouring-stops","s":"Reference","x":"DrawOp is Copy and an instance is a fixed number of floats, so a list of stops fits in neither. Instead the display list holds one DrawOp::RadialGradientRect per segment: the two offsets it lies between and their two colours (RadialSegment). fill_radial_gradient_rect emits them: one for the inside of the first stop if its offset is above zero, one per pair of neighbours, one for what lies beyond the last stop (its to"},{"t":"0033 — Radial gradients: one instance per pair of stops in the quad pipeline; the instance grows to 160 bytes","h":"The instance grows from 144 to 160 bytes","u":"architecture/0033-radial-gradients.html#the-instance-grows-from-144-to-160-bytes","s":"Reference","x":"A segment needs its centre (2), its radii (2), its two offsets (2) and, for 0034, a blur (1): seven floats where a gradient instance had the four of extra and one spare in params. A tenth vec4<f32>, extra2, is added to every instance: ten of sixteen vertex attributes, thirteen varying locations (40 components; the limit of Limits::downlevel_defaults() is 60 components, and WebGL2-class GLES allows 15 locations). The "},{"t":"0033 — Radial gradients: one instance per pair of stops in the quad pipeline; the instance grows to 160 bytes","h":"In the UI layer: Radial, with CSS's shape, position and size","u":"architecture/0033-radial-gradients.html#in-the-ui-layer-radial-with-csss-shape-position-and-size","s":"Reference","x":"Container::radial(Radial) paints a gradient over the box's background colour and over the radial gradients added before it; Container::radials([..]) takes a CSS background list as written, the first on top. A Radial is circle() or ellipse(), .at(x, y) in fractions of the box (at 30% 22% is .at(0.3, 0.22)), .extent(RadialExtent::{ClosestSide, FarthestSide, ClosestCorner, FarthestCorner, Radii(x, y)}) with CSS's defaul"},{"t":"0033 — Radial gradients: one instance per pair of stops in the quad pipeline; the instance grows to 160 bytes","h":"Not done","u":"architecture/0033-radial-gradients.html#not-done","s":"Reference","x":"Multi-stop and repeating linear gradients, conic gradients. The same segment scheme would carry a multi-stop linear gradient; no design here has one.A radius per corner on a gradient-filled box (0036): the slot is taken.Colour interpolation in another space (in oklch): everything is sRGB-encoded, as 0023 decided.Dithering. A slow fade over a large area is banded exactly as a browser's is."},{"t":"0033 — Radial gradients: one instance per pair of stops in the quad pipeline; the instance grows to 160 bytes","h":"Verification","u":"architecture/0033-radial-gradients.html#verification","s":"Reference","x":"crates/morningstar-gpu/tests/radial.rs (11 tests on the real GPU): two stops against the definition at every pixel; four stops on an off-centre ellipse with no seam; a fade to transparent keeping its hue; two gradients stacked in a circle; a gradient under a quarter turn. crates/morningstar-ui/tests/decor.rs: the display list of a box with a CSS list of layers, and the radii of every size keyword. On Atlas, the orb, "},{"t":"0034 — Blur of a box's background, evaluated per pixel: a solid box as its shadow, a radial gradient by quadrature; no blur pass","h":"","u":"architecture/0034-blur-of-a-box-background.html","s":"Reference","x":"0034 — Blur of a box's background, evaluated per pixel: a solid box as its shadow, a radial gradient by quadrature; no blur passStatus: Accepted (2026-11-13). Verified on the development host only (macOS, Metal, offscreen). Nothing here has run on a device and nothing here has been timed.Extends 0011 (shadows as an analytic blur) and 0033. It adds no render pass and changes no decision of theirs."},{"t":"0034 — Blur of a box's background, evaluated per pixel: a solid box as its shadow, a radial gradient by quadrature; no blur pass","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0034-blur-of-a-box-background.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"CSS filter: blur(Npx) blurs whatever an element paints with a Gaussian of standard deviation N. The general way to do that is a post-process: draw the element into an offscreen target, blur it in two passes, composite. The Atlas design uses the filter five times, always on the same thing: an ellipse under the orb filled with radial-gradient(ellipse, rgba(214,92,190,0.5), rgba(214,92,190,0) 70%), blurred by a twentiet"},{"t":"0034 — Blur of a box's background, evaluated per pixel: a solid box as its shadow, a radial gradient by quadrature; no blur pass","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0034-blur-of-a-box-background.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":"Container::blur(sigma) blurs the box's background only, in the quad's own fragment shader, with no offscreen pass. What it means depends on the background:A solid colour. A blurred rounded rectangle is exactly what a shadow is. The UI layer draws Shadow { blur: 2 * sigma, offset 0 } in the box's colour in place of the fill (a blur radius is two standard deviations, 0011). Exact along x, a 6-point quadrature along y, "},{"t":"0034 — Blur of a box's background, evaluated per pixel: a solid box as its shadow, a radial gradient by quadrature; no blur pass","h":"Verification","u":"architecture/0034-blur-of-a-box-background.html#verification","s":"Reference","x":"a_blurred_glow_matches_a_gaussian_blur_of_the_gradient in crates/morningstar-gpu/tests/radial.rs compares the glow in miniature with a brute-force Gaussian blur of the gradient: every sampled pixel within 4 of 255. crates/morningstar-ui/tests/decor.rs holds what the UI layer emits for a blurred solid box and a blurred gradient. On Atlas the glow under the orb shows on five of ten screens, each within its fidelity lim"},{"t":"0035 — Inner shadows, and several shadows on one box","h":"","u":"architecture/0035-inner-shadows-and-several-shadows.html","s":"Reference","x":"0035 — Inner shadows, and several shadows on one boxStatus: Accepted (2026-11-13). Verified on the development host only (macOS, Metal, offscreen). Nothing here has run on a device and nothing here has been timed.Extends 0011 (shadows) and uses the instance slot of 0033."},{"t":"0035 — Inner shadows, and several shadows on one box","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0035-inner-shadows-and-several-shadows.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"A box had one drop shadow. The Atlas orb has four in one box-shadow: two blurred inner lights (inset 0 -18px 35px rgba(255,255,255,0.5), inset 0 9px 21px ..), a one-pixel inner ring (inset 0 0 0 1px ..) and a drop shadow."},{"t":"0035 — Inner shadows, and several shadows on one box","h":"Decisions","u":"architecture/0035-inner-shadows-and-several-shadows.html#decisions","s":"Reference","x":"An inner shadow is one more kind of quad. DisplayList::draw_inset_shadow(rect, corner_radius, shadow) (DrawOp::InsetShadow) paints the shadow's colour inside the box wherever the box's own blurred silhouette, moved by the offset and shrunk by the spread, does not reach: CSS's definition, a sheet of the colour with a hole in it, seen through the box. Coverage is inside(p) * (1 - hole(p - offset)), where hole is the sh"},{"t":"0035 — Inner shadows, and several shadows on one box","h":"Verification","u":"architecture/0035-inner-shadows-and-several-shadows.html#verification","s":"Reference","x":"crates/morningstar-gpu/tests/radial.rs: an inner shadow against a brute-force blur of the offset hole (within 4 of 255 inside the box), and a sharp one with a spread as an exact ring. crates/morningstar-ui/tests/decor.rs: the order of the operations of a box with three shadows and a border."},{"t":"0036 — A radius per corner, for solid and bordered rectangles","h":"","u":"architecture/0036-a-radius-per-corner.html","s":"Reference","x":"0036 — A radius per corner, for solid and bordered rectanglesStatus: Accepted (2026-11-13). Verified on the development host only (macOS, Metal, offscreen). Nothing here has run on a device.Extends 0011 and uses the instance slot of 0033. Closes \"per-corner radii\" in the list of what is not available in docs/building-screens.md."},{"t":"0036 — A radius per corner, for solid and bordered rectangles","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0036-a-radius-per-corner.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"A chat bubble points at its speaker with one tighter corner (border-radius: 20px 20px 6px 20px on a translucent fill, which cannot be faked by overlapping two boxes), and a bottom sheet has round corners at the top only."},{"t":"0036 — A radius per corner, for solid and bordered rectangles","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0036-a-radius-per-corner.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":"DisplayList::fill_corners_rect(rect, CornerRadii, fill, border) (DrawOp::CornersRect) is fill_bordered_rect with four radii, clockwise from the top-left as in CSS, carried in extra2. The fragment stage takes the radius of the quadrant the pixel is in and evaluates the same rounded-box distance with it; the fill, the inside border and their anti-aliasing are the rectangle's. With four equal radii the picture is identi"},{"t":"0036 — A radius per corner, for solid and bordered rectangles","h":"Verification","u":"architecture/0036-a-radius-per-corner.html#verification","s":"Reference","x":"each_corner_has_its_own_radius and equal_radii_are_the_plain_rounded_rectangle in crates/morningstar-gpu/tests/radial.rs; the operation the UI layer emits in crates/morningstar-ui/tests/decor.rs. Atlas's bubble and sheet are inside their screens' fidelity limits (0.01% and 0.02%)."},{"t":"0037 — Scroll views that scroll sideways","h":"","u":"architecture/0037-scroll-views-that-scroll-sideways.html","s":"Reference","x":"0037 — Scroll views that scroll sidewaysStatus: Accepted (2026-11-13). Verified in tests on the host only. How it feels under a finger (the slop, the fling, the choice between two directions) has not been tried on a device.Extends 0013 and 0021 (scroll positions kept by identity, moves asked for through effects) and 0010. Closes \"horizontal scrolling\" in the list of what is not available in docs/building-screens.md."},{"t":"0037 — Scroll views that scroll sideways","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0037-scroll-views-that-scroll-sideways.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"A row of filter chips wider than the screen is the commonest horizontal scroller on a phone; Atlas's Explore has one (in the design's picture the fifth chip is cut off by the screen's edge). A scroll view scrolled along y only: its content overflowed downwards and was translated."},{"t":"0037 — Scroll views that scroll sideways","h":"Decisions","u":"architecture/0037-scroll-views-that-scroll-sideways.html#decisions","s":"Reference","x":"Layout: a row may offer its children unlimited width. A hugging child of a row is normally offered what the row has left, so that text wraps instead of overflowing (0010). Style::unbounded (rows only) offers infinity instead: each hugging child takes its natural width, and what does not fit runs past the row's end. Nothing else in the four sweeps changes. That overflow is the scroll view's content and is not reported"},{"t":"0037 — Scroll views that scroll sideways","h":"Verification","u":"architecture/0037-scroll-views-that-scroll-sideways.html#verification","s":"Reference","x":"crates/morningstar-ui/tests/decor.rs: natural-width layout with no overflow reported, a drag, the end stop, a tap after a drag, a request from the application, and the choice between a sideways row and the page around it. examples/atlas/tests/e2e.rs reaches and taps the chip that starts off screen, by dragging, on a 384 dp and a 360 dp wide phone. The other examples' suites are unchanged and pass."},{"t":"0038 — Live rooms: voice through LiveKit as an opt-in platform service","h":"","u":"architecture/0038-live-rooms.html","s":"Reference","x":"0038 — Live rooms: voice through LiveKit as an opt-in platform serviceStatus: Accepted (2026-11-15). Verified on the development host only, and by building two APKs and reading them back. Nothing here has run on a device and no room has been joined: the Rust type-checks for Android and iOS, the Android host (Java and one Kotlin file) compiles and packages against LiveKit 2.30.0, and the server that hands out tickets "},{"t":"0038 — Live rooms: voice through LiveKit as an opt-in platform service","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0038-live-rooms.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"Barua Talk (examples/barua-talk) rings, answers, chats and dials through its server, and for every room and call the server hands the app a LiveKit ticket: a wss:// address and a token. The framework had nothing to join with, so a connected call showed \"Live audio and video need the next version\" and carried no sound.A media client is the opposite of the services of ADR 0019 in three ways:It does not finish. A reques"},{"t":"0038 — Live rooms: voice through LiveKit as an opt-in platform service","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0038-live-rooms.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":""},{"t":"0038 — Live rooms: voice through LiveKit as an opt-in platform service","h":"The surface","u":"architecture/0038-live-rooms.html#the-surface","s":"Reference","x":"One live room at a time, voice only. The data is in morningstar-core (services.rs), the plumbing in morningstar-runtime is unchanged, and the API is on Effects in morningstar-ui: Message::Live receives each LiveEvent, in order:EventMeaningConnectedThe room is joined; the others are heard, and hear this device unless it joined muted.ParticipantJoined(LiveParticipant { identity, name })Someone is in the room: sent for "},{"t":"0038 — Live rooms: voice through LiveKit as an opt-in platform service","h":"A request that is answered more than once","u":"architecture/0038-live-rooms.html#a-request-that-is-answered-more-than-once","s":"Reference","x":"ServiceRequest::LiveJoin is answered with ServiceResult::Live(LiveEvent) under the join's id, as often as there are events. Nothing in morningstar-runtime had to change: Engine::service_result(id, result) already hands whatever it is given to the application and schedules a frame if the application took it. Ui keeps the room's handler (an FnMut, where every other request's is an FnOnce) until the final event, then dr"},{"t":"0038 — Live rooms: voice through LiveKit as an opt-in platform service","h":"Lifecycle: a room is never connected by accident","u":"architecture/0038-live-rooms.html#lifecycle-a-room-is-never-connected-by-accident","s":"Reference","x":"The microphone is the reason for every rule here. Each is tested where it can be (crates/morningstar-ui/tests/live.rs, crates/morningstar-runtime/src/engine.rs, examples/barua-talk/tests/sound.rs); the host's half compiles and is unrun.What happensWhat the room doesWhereThe app leaves the screen (onStop: Home, another app, the screen locked)The host leaves the room and the application is told Disconnected(Hidden), at"},{"t":"0038 — Live rooms: voice through LiveKit as an opt-in platform service","h":"Opt-in per app","u":"architecture/0038-live-rooms.html#opt-in-per-app","s":"Reference","x":"An app that joins live rooms says so where it says its name (ADR 0020): morningstar-icon <package> --print services prints the list, comma-separated, an empty line for none (identity.rs: a list of known names; an unknown name, or anything but a list of strings, is an error that names the key and lists the services).tools/build-android.sh reads it with the id, the name and the library and passes -Pmorningstar.services"},{"t":"0038 — Live rooms: voice through LiveKit as an opt-in platform service","h":"Android: LiveKit's client","u":"architecture/0038-live-rooms.html#android-livekits-client","s":"Reference","x":"io.livekit:livekit-android:2.30.0, the newest stable release on Maven Central when this was written (published 2026-11-03), Apache-2.0. From its repository and its published metadata rather than from memory:LiveKit 2.30.0The hostminSdk2124Kotlinbuilt with 1.9.25; the host compiles its one file with the plugin's own compiler (2.2.10)Java, plus that fileRepositoriesMaven Central, and JitPack for com.github.davidliu:aud"},{"t":"0038 — Live rooms: voice through LiveKit as an opt-in platform service","h":"iOS","u":"architecture/0038-live-rooms.html#ios","s":"Reference","x":"The two halves are in step and the service is not there. morningstar_ios.h has the four request kinds (MORNINGSTAR_SERVICE_LIVE_JOIN 11 to _SPEAKER 14), the event kinds and codes, and morningstar_ios_live_event; morningstar-ios implements them (the same from_wire); HostServices.swift answers every join MORNINGSTAR_LIVE_FAILED with MORNINGSTAR_LIVE_ERROR_UNSUPPORTED, logs \"live rooms are not available on iOS yet\", and"},{"t":"0038 — Live rooms: voice through LiveKit as an opt-in platform service","h":"The test driver","u":"architecture/0038-live-rooms.html#the-test-driver","s":"Reference","x":"Ui::testing plays the room, as it plays the server: The driver behaves as a host does: one room at a time (a second join ends the first with Replaced), leaving ends it with Left, forget_pending leaves it, and those endings are delivered at the start of the next frame or by deliver_effects, not inside the update that caused them. A stand-in host (Ui::with_services) can answer a join with its first event; with no host "},{"t":"0038 — Live rooms: voice through LiveKit as an opt-in platform service","h":"Barua Talk","u":"architecture/0038-live-rooms.html#barua-talk","s":"Reference","x":"The first user (examples/barua-talk/SCREENS.md, \"Sound\"). One function, run after every message, makes the room follow the call in progress: joined once when a call between two people becomes active, when a room's call gets its ticket, and as soon as a phone call is dialled; left when that call is no longer the one in progress, however that came about. Mute and Speaker act on the room. The call screens say how the so"},{"t":"0038 — Live rooms: voice through LiveKit as an opt-in platform service","h":"Video: the design to follow","u":"architecture/0038-live-rooms.html#video-the-design-to-follow","s":"Reference","x":"Carried out by 0039, with the second answer below. Kept as it was written.Not built. Voice had to be whole first, and video cannot be finished properly without a device: nothing about it (a camera, a decoder's output format, the cost of getting 30 pictures a second onto the screen) can be checked on this machine. This is the design that would be followed.The question is how a decoded frame gets onto the screen beside"},{"t":"0038 — Live rooms: voice through LiveKit as an opt-in platform service","h":"Consequences","u":"architecture/0038-live-rooms.html#consequences","s":"Reference","x":"ServiceRequest has four more variants and ServiceResult one; a low-level App that matches on them exhaustively must add arms. Application is unchanged.\"Every request is answered exactly once\" (ADR 0019) now has one stated exception.forget_pending now does something on the device (it leaves a joined room).The host interfaces grew, both halves together. As always, an application library and a host from different sides "},{"t":"0038 — Live rooms: voice through LiveKit as an opt-in platform service","h":"Limitations","u":"architecture/0038-live-rooms.html#limitations","s":"Reference","x":"Voice only in this record. The camera, remote pictures, screen sharing and data messages are 0039, which also changes one thing said here: a room in which this device's screen is shared is not left when the app leaves the screen.One room at a time, and no way to be in a room without the microphone permission (listening only).Not in the background. Leaving the screen, or the screen locking, ends the room. There is no "},{"t":"0038 — Live rooms: voice through LiveKit as an opt-in platform service","h":"Verified","u":"architecture/0038-live-rooms.html#verified","s":"Reference","x":"On the development host (macOS):Data types and the wire decoding: 13 tests in crates/morningstar-core/src/services.rs (5 new: defaults, that nothing prints the ticket, which events are final, the four values of each event, unknown codes).Engine: 2 new tests in crates/morningstar-runtime/src/engine.rs (a join answered once per event with one frame each and idle between; events delivered to a hidden app).UI layer: 22 t"},{"t":"0038 — Live rooms: voice through LiveKit as an opt-in platform service","h":"Not verified","u":"architecture/0038-live-rooms.html#not-verified","s":"Reference","x":"Everything on an Android device. No APK was installed. Unseen: that LiveKit's native libraries load beside the app's; the microphone question from a join, its refusal, and \"don't ask again\"; that a real ticket connects; that the microphone is published and heard, and remote sound plays; MODE_IN_COMMUNICATION set and restored, and audio focus against music or a phone call; earpiece and loudspeaker, and a wired headset"},{"t":"0039 — What a live room carries besides sound: data, pictures, the camera and the screen","h":"","u":"architecture/0039-live-video-screen-and-data.html","s":"Reference","x":"0039 — What a live room carries besides sound: data, pictures, the camera and the screenStatus: Accepted (2026-11-15). Verified on the development host only, and by building three APKs and reading them back. Nothing here has run on a device, no room has been joined, no camera has been opened and no frame has been decoded: the conversion and the drawing are pixel-tested on the host's GPU with frames made in the tests,"},{"t":"0039 — What a live room carries besides sound: data, pictures, the camera and the screen","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0039-live-video-screen-and-data.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"Barua Talk's calls carried sound and said \"Video needs the next version.\" Its owner asked for video calls, screen sharing and chat through the room. The tickets it is given are LiveKit's and already allow all three; the framework could join a room and do nothing in it but talk."},{"t":"0039 — What a live room carries besides sound: data, pictures, the camera and the screen","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0039-live-video-screen-and-data.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":""},{"t":"0039 — What a live room carries besides sound: data, pictures, the camera and the screen","h":"1. Data","u":"architecture/0039-live-video-screen-and-data.html#1-data","s":"Reference","x":"Effects::send_live_data(topic, payload, reliable) sends bytes to everyone else in the room; what arrives is LiveEvent::Data(LiveData { from, topic, payload }). Not answered, not sent without a room, and not sent when the payload is over MAX_LIVE_DATA_BYTES (15,000: what LiveKit carries reliably in one piece). LiveData prints its size, never its contents or its sender.A data message carries bytes, which the four value"},{"t":"0039 — What a live room carries besides sound: data, pictures, the camera and the screen","h":"2. Pictures","u":"architecture/0039-live-video-screen-and-data.html#2-pictures","s":"Reference","x":"The data. VideoTrack(u32) names one video, given by the host and never reused. VideoFrame is one decoded picture as I420: three tightly packed planes, a rotation (VideoRotation, the quarter turns that show it upright) and whether it is mirrored. VideoFrame::from_i420 copies planes that have padding into the buffers of an earlier frame, so a stream allocates at its start and when it grows; from_rgba8 makes one for tes"},{"t":"0039 — What a live room carries besides sound: data, pictures, the camera and the screen","h":"3. This device's screen","u":"architecture/0039-live-video-screen-and-data.html#3-this-devices-screen","s":"Reference","x":"Effects::set_live_screen_share(bool). On Android the system asks the user every time (MediaProjectionManager.createScreenCaptureIntent, through the activity); a refusal is MediaFailed { Screen, PermissionDenied }. LiveKit 2.30.0 provides the capture and its foreground service (ScreenCaptureService, type mediaProjection, started by setScreenShareEnabled with the ScreenCaptureParams the host builds), read from its clas"},{"t":"0039 — What a live room carries besides sound: data, pictures, the camera and the screen","h":"Opt-in","u":"architecture/0039-live-video-screen-and-data.html#opt-in","s":"Reference","x":"Two more names beside live, both meaningless without it (the build says so):servicesWhat the APK gainsliveVoice and data. As 0038: LiveKit, WebRTC, ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE, MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS. Remote video is given up (unsubscribed), so nothing is received or decoded for pictures the app cannot show.live-videoPictures: the others' cameras and shared screens are shown, and this device's camera can be turned on. CAMERA"},{"t":"0039 — What a live room carries besides sound: data, pictures, the camera and the screen","h":"The two halves","u":"architecture/0039-live-video-screen-and-data.html#the-two-halves","s":"Reference","x":"Out (Android): four more SERVICE_LIVE_* commands through onServiceCommand (14 data, 15 camera, 16 switch camera, 17 screen share), and a fourth settings byte in the join (camera).In (Android): nativeLiveEvent kinds 8 (VideoStarted), 9 (VideoStopped), 10 (MediaFailed), and three new natives: nativeLiveData, nativeVideoFrame, nativeVideoEnded. The activity passes on onRequestPermissionsResult (the camera question, whic"},{"t":"0039 — What a live room carries besides sound: data, pictures, the camera and the screen","h":"Barua Talk","u":"architecture/0039-live-video-screen-and-data.html#barua-talk","s":"Reference","x":"examples/barua-talk declares all three services. A call with video joins with the camera as its button stands. On the call screen the other side's camera fills the screen over their face (which shows until the first frame and while their camera is off), a screen someone shares is shown whole in place of it, the user's own camera is in the designed tile (touching it changes camera), the Camera switch acts on the room,"},{"t":"0039 — What a live room carries besides sound: data, pictures, the camera and the screen","h":"Consequences","u":"architecture/0039-live-video-screen-and-data.html#consequences","s":"Reference","x":"An application can show any video of a room anywhere a picture can go, with the framework's own clipping and compositing, and the fidelity tests can render a call with pictures (screenshot_frame_with_videos).A frame of video is copied twice on its way to the screen: out of WebRTC's buffer (which also converts a hardware buffer to I420 first), and into three textures. Then one conversion pass and the application's fra"},{"t":"0039 — What a live room carries besides sound: data, pictures, the camera and the screen","h":"Limitations","u":"architecture/0039-live-video-screen-and-data.html#limitations","s":"Reference","x":"Android only; iOS refuses the room.No reduced copies of a video. A picture shown much smaller than it is (720p in a 100 dp tile) is sampled bilinearly from its full size and is coarser than an image would be.A whole application frame per picture. There is no path that redraws only a video.One conversion: BT.601 limited range. A source that says it is BT.709 or full range is drawn slightly off in colour; WebRTC's fram"},{"t":"0039 — What a live room carries besides sound: data, pictures, the camera and the screen","h":"Tests","u":"architecture/0039-live-video-screen-and-data.html#tests","s":"Reference","x":"crates/morningstar-core/src/video.rs: 8 (the round trip of a colour, the ends of the range, padding, refusals, reuse of buffers, the four turns and the mirror, degrees, no picture in Debug). services.rs: 2 new (the three kinds across the boundary, the four commands).crates/morningstar-gpu/tests/video.rs: 8 pixel tests on the host's GPU against the CPU reference: colours, range, every turn with and without the mirror,"},{"t":"0039 — What a live room carries besides sound: data, pictures, the camera and the screen","h":"Not verified","u":"architecture/0039-live-video-screen-and-data.html#not-verified","s":"Reference","x":"Everything on a device. With the log line that would prove each:A data message arrives: live: id=N data received, K bytes, then service: id=N live room: data K bytes; one sent: live: id=N data K bytes sent.A remote camera is shown: live: id=N video T started (camera), live: video T first frame WxH rotation R, service: id=N live room: someone's Camera started, track T. That the picture is upright, the right colours an"},{"t":"0040 — A development preview: the app in a window on the Mac, reloaded when its source changes","h":"","u":"architecture/0040-development-preview.html","s":"Reference","x":"0040 — A development preview: the app in a window on the Mac, reloaded when its source changesStatus: Accepted (2026-11-17). Verified on the development host, which is the only place it runs: see \"What was verified\". It is a development tool; nothing of it is linked into an app built for a phone.Extends 0003 (thin native hosts that drive one Engine) with a third host, for the development machine, and 0008 (the loop f"},{"t":"0040 — A development preview: the app in a window on the Mac, reloaded when its source changes","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0040-development-preview.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"Until now an app could be seen in two ways: as a PNG of one frame (screenshot examples, in seconds, without interaction), or on a phone (an APK build and an install, a minute or more, with a cable). Everything in between, tapping through screens, typing into a field, watching an animation, scrolling a list, talking to the app's server, needed the phone. The owner asked for a live preview."},{"t":"0040 — A development preview: the app in a window on the Mac, reloaded when its source changes","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0040-development-preview.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":"tools/morningstar-preview runs any Morningstar app in a window on the Mac and reloads it when its source changes: It is two processes."},{"t":"0040 — A development preview: the app in a window on the Mac, reloaded when its source changes","h":"The host: a third platform host","u":"architecture/0040-development-preview.html#the-host-a-third-platform-host","s":"Reference","x":"morningstar_preview::host is to the Mac what MorningstarActivity is to Android: it owns a native window (through winit), translates its events into calls on the same Engine, draws when Engine::needs_frame says so and sleeps otherwise, and carries out the app's service requests. The app cannot tell it from a phone except by what is answered Unsupported.Surface. NativeSurface gains one variant, AppKitView (an NSView*, "},{"t":"0040 — A development preview: the app in a window on the Mac, reloaded when its source changes","h":"The supervisor: build again, start again","u":"architecture/0040-development-preview.html#the-supervisor-build-again-start-again","s":"Reference","x":"morningstar-preview <app> (the binary) reads the app's identity from cargo metadata (as the build scripts do, 0020), writes a runner crate of two files under target/preview/<app>/runner, builds it, starts it, and watches the sources.How the app gets in. An app is a library; morningstar::app! exports __morningstar_create_app and there is no main. The runner is a binary crate that depends on the app and on the preview "},{"t":"0040 — A development preview: the app in a window on the Mac, reloaded when its source changes","h":"Dependencies (adds to 0007's inventory)","u":"architecture/0040-development-preview.html#dependencies-adds-to-0007s-inventory","s":"Reference","x":"CrateVersionUsed byWhywinit0.30.13 (the newest stable; 0.31 is in beta)morningstar-preview, macOS onlyThe window, its events and its handle. default-features = false with rwh_06: no X11 or Wayland. Brings the objc2 family on macOS. Never linked into an app, a framework crate or morningstar-devtools. Its raw-window-handle (0.6.2) is the one wgpu 30 already uses.Deliberately absent: an HTTP client and a TLS stack (the "},{"t":"0040 — A development preview: the app in a window on the Mac, reloaded when its source changes","h":"Costs and limits","u":"architecture/0040-development-preview.html#costs-and-limits","s":"Reference","x":"Disk. In the workspace's target/debug: winit and the crates it brings, second copies of the GPU and framework crates (with winit in the graph their features unify differently from a -p build of an app alone, so Cargo keeps both), and 45 to 50 MB for each previewed app's binary. Roughly 250 to 300 MB in all on this machine, read from the directory after the fact, not measured precisely.Plain http:// is not refused. Th"},{"t":"0040 — A development preview: the app in a window on the Mac, reloaded when its source changes","h":"What was verified","u":"architecture/0040-development-preview.html#what-was-verified","s":"Reference","x":"All on the development machine (an Apple M4, macOS, a Retina display at 2 pixels per point and an external one at 1), on 2026-11-17.Tests: 61 in tools/morningstar-preview (cargo test -p morningstar-preview): 53 unit tests (the command line and its round trip to the host, the fit of the window to a display, the runner's two files and the lock's copy, the workspace read from cargo metadata, the watcher and its debounce"},{"t":"0040 — A development preview: the app in a window on the Mac, reloaded when its source changes","h":"Not verified","u":"architecture/0040-development-preview.html#not-verified","s":"Reference","x":"A person's hand. No real click, drag, wheel or key press was made: only the scripted steps, which share the mapping and the engine calls but not the window system's delivery. The keys (Cmd+S, Cmd+R, F1 to F5, Escape), the clipboard (pbcopy, pbpaste), resizing the window, dragging it to the other display and the trackpad's momentum are untried.The window's own pixels. The saved picture is the presented frame drawn aga"},{"t":"0041 — A new app from one command: morningstar-new","h":"","u":"architecture/0041-new-app-generator.html","s":"Reference","x":"0041 — A new app from one command: morningstar-newStatus: Accepted (2026-11-17). Verified on the development host: the generator's tests, a generated app's own tests and screenshots, its server's tests, and one Android build of a generated app. No generated app has run on a device.Uses 0005 (the app crate is the native library), 0013 (Elm-style state), 0019 (effects, and tests with no device), 0020 (identity declared"},{"t":"0041 — A new app from one command: morningstar-new","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0041-new-app-generator.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"Every app so far (examples/showcase to examples/barua-talk) was started by copying another one and removing what was not wanted. That is slow, it carries the copied app's design decisions into the new one, and it is where things go missing: a test file, the icon, the workspace entry, the crate-type the Android build needs. The owner asked for the framework to be scaffolded: starting an app should be one command, and "},{"t":"0041 — A new app from one command: morningstar-new","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0041-new-app-generator.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":""},{"t":"0041 — A new app from one command: morningstar-new","h":"1. The command","u":"architecture/0041-new-app-generator.html#1-the-command","s":"Reference","x":"A binary in tools/morningstar-devtools, beside morningstar-icon, with the logic in the library (scaffold.rs: NewApp, create_app, add_workspace_member) so that it is tested as a library and usable by another tool: Only the package name is required. --name defaults to the package in words (\"My App\"), --id to dev.morningstar.<package> (the default ADR 0020 already gives), services to none, the directory to apps. --works"},{"t":"0041 — A new app from one command: morningstar-new","h":"2. What it writes","u":"architecture/0041-new-app-generator.html#2-what-it-writes","s":"Reference","x":"A crate that is complete in the sense of AGENTS.md, not a stub:FileWhat it isCargo.tomlThe package, [package.metadata.morningstar] with name, id, icon and services, crate-type = [\"cdylib\", \"lib\"], and a dependency on morningstar alone (morningstar-devtools for tests)src/lib.rsapp!, a Model, a Message, two screens (Home, Settings) on a Navigator with its transition, the system's Back, a theme with the app's colour, a "},{"t":"0041 — A new app from one command: morningstar-new","h":"3. Where: apps/, not examples/","u":"architecture/0041-new-app-generator.html#3-where-apps-not-examples","s":"Reference","x":"examples/ holds the framework's own demonstrations. Each is in the table in AGENTS.md, most are the proof of an ADR (a design reproduced, a service exercised), and their fidelity tests are part of what the framework is held to. An app somebody starts is theirs: it has its own purpose, its own pace, and it may leave. Putting it among the examples would make the examples table a list of unrelated products and make \"the"},{"t":"0041 — A new app from one command: morningstar-new","h":"4. Templates kept in the binary","u":"architecture/0041-new-app-generator.html#4-templates-kept-in-the-binary","s":"Reference","x":"The templates are files under tools/morningstar-devtools/templates/app/, compiled in with include_str!. They are the generated files themselves with two additions: {{name}}-style values, and @@if server / @@else / @@end lines around what only one of the two variants has. Nothing else is interpreted, which keeps a template readable as the file it becomes.Kept in the binary rather than read from disk at run time: the c"},{"t":"0041 — A new app from one command: morningstar-new","h":"5. Checked before anything is written","u":"architecture/0041-new-app-generator.html#5-checked-before-anything-is-written","s":"Reference","x":"The package name: lower-case letters, digits and single hyphens, starting with a letter, at most 40 characters, and not the name of a crate the app is built from (morningstar, morningstar-*, core, std, test, ...).The name and the id: by the functions that read them back from Cargo.toml (identity.rs), so that what the generator accepts is exactly what the icon tool and the build scripts accept.The services: known name"},{"t":"0041 — A new app from one command: morningstar-new","h":"6. The first test run writes the pictures","u":"architecture/0041-new-app-generator.html#6-the-first-test-run-writes-the-pictures","s":"Reference","x":"Existing examples commit their reference pictures, and a missing reference fails until the tests are run once with MORNINGSTAR_UPDATE_REFERENCES=1. The generator cannot commit pictures: it does not link the UI layer or a GPU, and a picture rendered on another machine's GPU would not be the reader's. A new app whose first cargo test fails is a bad first minute.So the generated tests/screens.rs writes a reference that "},{"t":"0041 — A new app from one command: morningstar-new","h":"7. The icon","u":"architecture/0041-new-app-generator.html#7-the-icon","s":"Reference","x":"Drawn by the generator on the CPU and written as assets/icon.png, 1024 x 1024: a four-pointed star in white on the app's colour, inside the middle 61% that every Android mask keeps, so ADR 0020's tool makes every launcher form from it without an adaptive foreground. The colour is one of six, chosen by a hash of the package name, and is the theme's primary in the generated src/lib.rs; each of the six carries white tex"},{"t":"0041 — A new app from one command: morningstar-new","h":"8. The server skeleton","u":"architecture/0041-new-app-generator.html#8-the-server-skeleton","s":"Reference","x":"--server writes a Node server with no dependencies in the shape examples/barua-talk/server settled on: settings from the environment and then .env (config.js), the pipeline in app.js (optional bearer token compared in constant time, X-Device-Id, a rate limit per device, one error shape, a log line that is method, path, status and time and never a header or a body), a health check, and one example resource (POST and G"},{"t":"0041 — A new app from one command: morningstar-new","h":"What this costs","u":"architecture/0041-new-app-generator.html#what-this-costs","s":"Reference","x":"Templates are a second copy of how an app is written. When the UI layer's API changes, templates/app/ must change with it. What catches it: tests/new_app.rs generates an app and runs its tests on every run of the devtools tests, so a template that no longer compiles fails there. That test runs cargo inside a test (about 10 s with a warm target directory, which it shares with the repository).The first screens are not "},{"t":"0041 — A new app from one command: morningstar-new","h":"Not done","u":"architecture/0041-new-app-generator.html#not-done","s":"Reference","x":"iOS: the generated app is the same crate the iOS host would link, and tools/build-ios.sh takes it by name, but nothing of iOS has been built on this machine, for this app or any other.No generator for a screen, a widget or a service inside an existing app.No removal command. Removing an app is deleting its directory and its line in the root Cargo.toml.The server skeleton has no deployment recipe."},{"t":"0041 — A new app from one command: morningstar-new","h":"Verification","u":"architecture/0041-new-app-generator.html#verification","s":"Reference","x":"On the development host (macOS, Metal), 2026-11-17:cargo test -p morningstar-devtools --lib scaffold: 15 tests. Names and ids refused with their reasons; services; directories and ports; the file list of each variant; no directive or value left unfilled; no generated line of Rust over 100 characters with the longest names; a quoted name escaped where it is inside a string; the six colours' contrast with white; the ic"},{"t":"0042 — Live rooms that go on in the background, and where their sound goes","h":"","u":"architecture/0042-live-rooms-in-the-background-and-audio-routes.html","s":"Reference","x":"0042 — Live rooms that go on in the background, and where their sound goesStatus: Accepted (2026-11-17). Verified on the development host only, and by building APKs and reading their manifests back. Nothing here has run on a device: no foreground service has been started, no notification shown, no headset connected. The application side is tested with the test driver, the Android host compiles (Kotlin and the merged "},{"t":"0042 — Live rooms that go on in the background, and where their sound goes","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0042-live-rooms-in-the-background-and-audio-routes.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"A call in Barua Talk ended the moment its user looked at another app or the screen locked, and came back when they returned. That is safe and it is not what a call is: people look things up while they talk. 0038 said what going on needs (a foreground service of type microphone and its notification) and left it for this record. 0039 built that service, but only for a room in which the screen is shared.Separately, 0038"},{"t":"0042 — Live rooms that go on in the background, and where their sound goes","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0042-live-rooms-in-the-background-and-audio-routes.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":""},{"t":"0042 — Live rooms that go on in the background, and where their sound goes","h":"1. A room goes on in the background when three things are true","u":"architecture/0042-live-rooms-in-the-background-and-audio-routes.html#1-a-room-goes-on-in-the-background-when-three-things-are-true","s":"Reference","x":"The application asked, per room: LiveJoin::background(LiveBackground::new(title, text)). The notice is what the user will read in the system's notification, and hang_up names the action that ends the room from there (\"Hang up\" unless changed). There is no way to ask for the background without a notice: the user must be able to see that the microphone is open and to close it without finding the app again.The app was b"},{"t":"0042 — Live rooms that go on in the background, and where their sound goes","h":"2. Exactly when such a room ends","u":"architecture/0042-live-rooms-in-the-background-and-audio-routes.html#2-exactly-when-such-a-room-ends","s":"Reference","x":"What happensThe roomThe application hearsThe application leaves it, or joins anotherEndsDisconnected(Left) / Disconnected(Replaced)The user presses the notification's actionEnds at once, in the host, without waiting for the applicationDisconnected(HungUp), while hiddenThe other side closes the room, or removes this personEndsDisconnected(Ended)The connection cannot be keptEndsDisconnected(Lost)forget_pending, or the "},{"t":"0042 — Live rooms that go on in the background, and where their sound goes","h":"3. Audio routes","u":"architecture/0042-live-rooms-in-the-background-and-audio-routes.html#3-audio-routes","s":"Reference","x":"Told: LiveEvent::AudioRoutes(LiveAudioRoutes { available, selected }) once the room is connected and whenever either changes, a headset connecting or leaving in the middle of a call included. Routes are Earpiece, Speaker, Wired, Bluetooth. That event is how an application asks which routes exist: it always has the latest.Chosen: out.set_live_audio_route(route). Not answered; the next AudioRoutes says where the sound "},{"t":"0042 — Live rooms that go on in the background, and where their sound goes","h":"The two halves","u":"architecture/0042-live-rooms-in-the-background-and-audio-routes.html#the-two-halves","s":"Reference","x":"Join (SERVICE_LIVE_JOIN): a fifth flag byte (goes on in the background) and after the five flags the notice as UTF-8, three lines (LiveBackground::to_wire). iOS: the notice as text3.SERVICE_LIVE_AUDIO_ROUTE (18 on Android, 19 on iOS): one byte, the route's number (1 to 4).LIVE_AUDIO_ROUTES (event kind 11): the code is the route in use (0 for none) plus 16, 32, 64, 128 for each of the four that is available. LIVE_END_"},{"t":"0042 — Live rooms that go on in the background, and where their sound goes","h":"The test driver","u":"architecture/0042-live-rooms-in-the-background-and-audio-routes.html#the-test-driver","s":"Reference","x":"Ui::live_app_hidden() does what such a host does when the app leaves the screen: a room joined with a notice, or one sharing the screen, goes on (returns true); any other ends as Hidden. Ui::live_hang_up_from_notice() is the notification's action. LiveRoom::audio_route is the route last asked for. A test of an app built without live-background delivers Disconnected(Hidden) itself, as before."},{"t":"0042 — Live rooms that go on in the background, and where their sound goes","h":"Barua Talk","u":"architecture/0042-live-rooms-in-the-background-and-audio-routes.html#barua-talk","s":"Reference","x":"Declares live-background. Every real call (in-app, a room's, to a phone) is joined with a notice that names it (\"Call with Baraka Mushi\", \"Call to +255…\"). HungUp hangs the call up as the red button does and says \"You ended the call from its notification.\" The last other participant leaving makes the app ask its server about the call at once. The phone call's Speaker button asks for the loudspeaker route, and off aga"},{"t":"0042 — Live rooms that go on in the background, and where their sound goes","h":"Consequences","u":"architecture/0042-live-rooms-in-the-background-and-audio-routes.html#consequences","s":"Reference","x":"An app that opts in shows a notification for every call and declares three more permissions; a store listing shows them. That is why it is opt-in, and why the notice is per room.A room can now outlive the activity it was joined from. The host keeps the application context for that, and the render thread, which belongs to the process, keeps delivering events.LiveJoin grew (a notice is three strings); the UI's test dri"},{"t":"0042 — Live rooms that go on in the background, and where their sound goes","h":"Limitations","u":"architecture/0042-live-rooms-in-the-background-and-audio-routes.html#limitations","s":"Reference","x":"Not incoming calls. A call can go on in the background; it cannot arrive there.The camera is off while the app is hidden.No choice between two Bluetooth headsets, or of the microphone separately from the output: a route is a kind.The notification's small icon is the platform's telephone, not the app's.A user who refuses notifications has a call that goes on with no notification to end it from (the system's own list o"},{"t":"0042 — Live rooms that go on in the background, and where their sound goes","h":"Tests","u":"architecture/0042-live-rooms-in-the-background-and-audio-routes.html#tests","s":"Reference","x":"Core: the notice's wire form, HungUp as a final event, routes to a code and back, unknown numbers refused.UI (tests/live.rs): a room without a notice ends on leaving the screen; one with a notice goes on and ends by each of its five endings; forget_pending leaves it; the notification can only end a room that has one; a shared screen keeps a room until sharing stops; routes arrive and a choice is recorded.Engine: a ba"},{"t":"0042 — Live rooms that go on in the background, and where their sound goes","h":"Not verified","u":"architecture/0042-live-rooms-in-the-background-and-audio-routes.html#not-verified","s":"Reference","x":"Everything on a device. In the order to try, with the log line (adb logcat -s Morningstar) that proves each:Notifications asked on Android 13+: live: asking the user to allow notifications, then live: notifications allowed.The service at connect: live: the microphone's foreground service is running, and the notification with the call's name.Home during a call: live: id=N goes on behind another app: it has a notice; t"},{"t":"0043 — Continuous integration: what runs where, and what it costs","h":"","u":"architecture/0043-continuous-integration.html","s":"Reference","x":"0043 — Continuous integration: what runs where, and what it costsStatus: Accepted (2026-11-17). First run on 2026-11-17: the three Linux jobs passed (format, lint and the tests without a GPU; the Android lint; the three servers' tests). The macOS gpu job has never run: it was made manual-only before the first push, so whether the pixel tests pass on a hosted Mac's GPU, and what that costs, is still unknown."},{"t":"0043 — Continuous integration: what runs where, and what it costs","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0043-continuous-integration.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"Nothing checked a push. The host tests, the fidelity tests, the three servers' tests and the Android type-check were run by hand, package by package, on one machine whose disk is too small to run them together. The repository is private, so runner minutes are paid for."},{"t":"0043 — Continuous integration: what runs where, and what it costs","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0043-continuous-integration.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":"One workflow, .github/workflows/ci.yml, four jobs.JobRunnerWhenWhatrustLinuxevery push to main, every pull requestcargo fmt --check; cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings; the tests of morningstar-core, -layout and -textandroidLinuxthe samecargo ndk -t arm64-v8a clippy of the seven example apps, with the runner image's own NDK. The Rust half only: no Gradle, no APKserversLinux, one per serverthe samen"},{"t":"0043 — Continuous integration: what runs where, and what it costs","h":"What it costs","u":"architecture/0043-continuous-integration.html#what-it-costs","s":"Reference","x":"Prices as published on 2026-11-17: Linux 2-core $0.006 a minute, macOS $0.062 a minute, each job rounded up to a whole minute. The durations below are guesses from how long the same commands take on the development machine, not measurements; the first runs will say.JobGuess, cold cacheGuess, warmAt the guessrust10 to 15 min3 to 6 minunder $0.10android6 to 10 min2 to 4 minunder $0.06servers (three)1 min each1 min each"},{"t":"0043 — Continuous integration: what runs where, and what it costs","h":"Not verified","u":"architecture/0043-continuous-integration.html#not-verified","s":"Reference","x":"That the workflow is accepted by GitHub and each job starts. It parses as YAML with the structure a workflow has; no workflow linter was available.The action versions (checkout@v7, cache@v6, setup-node@v7) are the ones their READMEs show; none was run.That the Linux image builds the whole workspace for clippy without extra system packages. wgpu and the text stack need none to compile; the preview tool (ADR 0040) open"},{"t":"0044 — The documentation site: static files built from the repository's Markdown by one script","h":"","u":"architecture/0044-documentation-site.html","s":"Reference","x":"0044 — The documentation site: static files built from the repository's Markdown by one scriptStatus: Accepted (2026-11-17). Built and looked at on the development machine in headless Chrome at desktop and phone widths. Not published anywhere."},{"t":"0044 — The documentation site: static files built from the repository's Markdown by one script","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0044-documentation-site.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"The documentation was three Markdown guides, the decision records and AGENTS.md. There was nothing to hand to a person who wants to learn the framework from the beginning, no reference of the widgets, and no pictures of what apps look like. The owner supplied a design for a documentation site (four pages: an overview, an installation page, a widget page and a reference page; dark, with a gold accent) and a logo.The d"},{"t":"0044 — The documentation site: static files built from the repository's Markdown by one script","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0044-documentation-site.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":"A static site under site/, generated from Markdown under docs/ by tools/build-docs.py, which needs only Python 3's standard library. The generated HTML is committed, so the site opens from a file manager and can be served by any static host with no build step.The prose lives once. docs/site.json lists the pages. The three existing guides and all decision records are pages of the site as they are; nothing was copied i"},{"t":"0044 — The documentation site: static files built from the repository's Markdown by one script","h":"Where the site departs from the design","u":"architecture/0044-documentation-site.html#where-the-site-departs-from-the-design","s":"Reference","x":"Sections: Guide, Widgets, Reference and Examples. The design's \"Blog\" has no content to hold and is not there.No \"Is this page useful?\" buttons: there is nowhere to send an answer.The version pill shows the workspace's real version from Cargo.toml.A menu button on narrow screens. The design hides the navigation below 780 px and offers no way to reach it.Search works, over an index built with the pages and loaded on f"},{"t":"0044 — The documentation site: static files built from the repository's Markdown by one script","h":"Consequences","u":"architecture/0044-documentation-site.html#consequences","s":"Reference","x":"A change to a guide, a sample, a doc comment or a tool's help must be followed by python3 tools/build-docs.py, or --check fails. A change to a screen should be followed by tools/docs-screenshots.sh; nothing detects a stale picture.site/ is generated output in the repository: about 100 HTML files and under 4 MB in all. Reviews of a documentation change show the Markdown diff and the HTML diff.The Markdown converter is"},{"t":"0044 — The documentation site: static files built from the repository's Markdown by one script","h":"Verification","u":"architecture/0044-documentation-site.html#verification","s":"Reference","x":"python3 tools/build-docs.py --check: passes; every internal link and heading anchor resolves.cargo clippy -p docs-snippets --all-targets -- -D warnings: clean. cargo test -p docs-snippets: 6 pass.Every page was rendered in headless Chrome at 1440 and 390 px wide and a sample of them looked at; the home page was compared with the design's first page.Not verified: the site in any browser but Chrome; with a screen reade"},{"t":"0045 — Released on crates.io, with one command: morningstar","h":"","u":"architecture/0045-crates-io-release-and-the-morningstar-command.html","s":"Reference","x":"0045 — Released on crates.io, with one command: morningstarStatus: Accepted (2026-11-17). Prepared and checked here: every crate packages, the packages carry no secret, private address or personal path, and a standalone app generated by the command passes its own tests and builds into an APK against the packaged crates (through [patch.crates-io]). The preview runs through the command for an app in this repository. Pu"},{"t":"0045 — Released on crates.io, with one command: morningstar","h":"Context","u":"architecture/0045-crates-io-release-and-the-morningstar-command.html#context","s":"Reference","x":"Until now Morningstar was used only from inside this repository. Every crate said publish = false, an app found the framework by a path in the workspace, and the preview, the Android build and the generator were scripts and binaries of the checkout. The owner chose a public release on crates.io so that an app can be started anywhere with cargo install and cargo add, under MIT OR Apache-2.0.Two facts shaped the decisi"},{"t":"0045 — Released on crates.io, with one command: morningstar","h":"Decision","u":"architecture/0045-crates-io-release-and-the-morningstar-command.html#decision","s":"Reference","x":""},{"t":"0045 — Released on crates.io, with one command: morningstar","h":"1. What is published","u":"architecture/0045-crates-io-release-and-the-morningstar-command.html#1-what-is-published","s":"Reference","x":"Twelve crates, one version for all, released together:CrateIsmorningstar-frameworkThe public API. Its library is named morningstar, so app code is unchanged: use morningstar::ui::prelude::*;morningstar-core, -gpu, -text, -layout, -runtime, -ui, -android, -iosThe framework's crates, as beforemorningstar-devtoolsScreenshots in tests, morningstar-diff, -icon, -svg, -new; a generated app's dev-dependencymorningstar-previ"},{"t":"0045 — Released on crates.io, with one command: morningstar","h":"2. The command","u":"architecture/0045-crates-io-release-and-the-morningstar-command.html#2-the-command","s":"Reference","x":"morningstar-cli installs one binary, morningstar:CommandDoesBuilt frommorningstar new <package>A standalone app in ./<package> (section 3)scaffold.rs (ADR 0041)morningstar previewThe app of the current directory in a windowmorningstar-preview (ADR 0040), with the package found from the current directorymorningstar build android [--abis] [--install]What tools/build-android.sh does, for an app anywhereThe host carried "},{"t":"0045 — Released on crates.io, with one command: morningstar","h":"3. A standalone app","u":"architecture/0045-crates-io-release-and-the-morningstar-command.html#3-a-standalone-app","s":"Reference","x":"NewApp::standalone(true) and create_standalone_app write the same app as morningstar-new from the same templates. Templates now say @@if workspace / @@if standalone, and their commands come from placeholders. The differences:Cargo.toml has its own version, edition and publish. It depends on morningstar = { package = \"morningstar-framework\", version = \"=<release>\" } and has morningstar-devtools = \"=<release>\" as a dev"},{"t":"0045 — Released on crates.io, with one command: morningstar","h":"4. The preview of a released command","u":"architecture/0045-crates-io-release-and-the-morningstar-command.html#4-the-preview-of-a-released-command","s":"Reference","x":"The preview builds a runner crate that depends on morningstar-preview (ADR 0040). From a checkout that dependency is a path. From a release, Cargo's cache holds the sources, and Cargo may clean that cache. So a released preview names itself by version instead (morningstar-preview = \"=<release>\"). It recognises a release by the Cargo.toml.orig that cargo package writes into every published crate."},{"t":"0045 — Released on crates.io, with one command: morningstar","h":"5. Publishing","u":"architecture/0045-crates-io-release-and-the-morningstar-command.html#5-publishing","s":"Reference","x":"The owner publishes. It is public and permanent: a version can be yanked but never deleted. Steps: cargo publish --workspace (Cargo 1.90 and later) publishes in dependency order and waits for each crate to be in the index. A next release changes version in [workspace.package] and every =x.y.z in [workspace.dependencies] together."},{"t":"0045 — Released on crates.io, with one command: morningstar","h":"Consequences","u":"architecture/0045-crates-io-release-and-the-morningstar-command.html#consequences","s":"Reference","x":"An app can live anywhere: cargo install morningstar-cli, morningstar new, then cargo test, morningstar preview, morningstar build android. Inside this repository nothing changed: morningstar-new, tools/preview.sh and tools/build-android.sh work as before.The source is public under MIT OR Apache-2.0 from the first publish.A change to templates/android reaches users only in the next release of morningstar-cli, together"},{"t":"Examples","h":"","u":"examples/index.html","s":"Examples","x":"ExamplesThe repository's own apps. Each one exists to show something, and each is tested on the development machine. The pictures are frames rendered by the app's own screenshot program, with no phone.AppShowsScreensServerStarterWhat morningstar-new writes: the smallest complete app2OptionalShowcaseRealistic screens written with the UI layer alone3NoFundiDeskA whole design reproduced: twelve screens held to their pic"},{"t":"Examples","h":"Running any of them","u":"examples/index.html#running-any-of-them","s":"Examples","x":"The apps with a server also run without it: they fall back to a scripted version of themselves, which is what the tests and the screenshots use."},{"t":"Examples","h":"Which to read first","u":"examples/index.html#which-to-read-first","s":"Examples","x":"Read FundiDesk to see how a design becomes screens: its SCREENS.md is the method. Read Starter for the shape of a new app, and Barua Talk for an app with a server, effects and a live room."},{"t":"Starter","h":"","u":"examples/starter.html","s":"Examples","x":"StarterThe app morningstar-new writes. It is not kept in the repository: it is generated, so it cannot drift from the generator. These pictures are of one generated for this page.HomeSettings"},{"t":"Starter","h":"What it shows","u":"examples/starter.html#what-it-shows","s":"Examples","x":"Two screens on a Navigator, with a transition between them and the system's Back.A text input in a framed box, with a label for screen readers.A value kept on the phone with store and read back at setup.A theme with one brand colour.Tests that pass from the first minute: a journey, an accessibility check, and a picture of each screen.With --server it also has an \"Ask the server\" button, an HTTP request with a timeout"},{"t":"Starter","h":"Make one","u":"examples/starter.html#make-one","s":"Examples","x":"Your first app continues from here."},{"t":"Starter","h":"What has been verified","u":"examples/starter.html#what-has-been-verified","s":"Examples","x":"A generated app builds, passes its 11 tests, and builds into an Android APK of about 10 MB. No generated app has been run on a phone yet."},{"t":"Showcase","h":"","u":"examples/showcase.html","s":"Examples","x":"ShowcaseThree realistic screens written with the UI layer and nothing else: no design file, no server. It is the smallest example of an app with several screens, a form and a settings list, and the one docs/building-screens.md tells you to imitate first.HomeSign inSettings"},{"t":"Showcase","h":"What it shows","u":"examples/showcase.html#what-it-shows","s":"Examples","x":"Cards, a gradient, avatars and a scrolling list on one screen.A form with text inputs, focus and validation.A settings list with toggles drawn from boxes.The same screens in Swahili and English, including a line of Arabic shaped right to left.Visual-regression tests: each screen is compared with a reference picture."},{"t":"Showcase","h":"Run it","u":"examples/showcase.html#run-it","s":"Examples","x":""},{"t":"Showcase","h":"What has been verified","u":"examples/showcase.html#what-has-been-verified","s":"Examples","x":"Its home screen on a Galaxy A27 matched the desktop render by eye: the first check of the full shader on Vulkan."},{"t":"FundiDesk","h":"","u":"examples/fundidesk.html","s":"Examples","x":"FundiDeskA repair shop's app: twelve screens reproduced from a real design, each tested against the design's own picture. It is the model to imitate when building from a design.Log inDashboardRepairsRepair details"},{"t":"FundiDesk","h":"What it shows","u":"examples/fundidesk.html#what-it-shows","s":"Examples","x":"The loop from a picture to code. Render, compare with morningstar-diff, read the report, change one thing, repeat.A design's tokens in one place: src/theme.rs holds every colour, src/icons.rs every icon, src/components.rs every piece that occurs more than once.One file per screen, so screens can be built in parallel.Real forms: its text fields are working inputs.A limit per screen in tests/fidelity.rs: the largest sh"},{"t":"FundiDesk","h":"The screens","u":"examples/fundidesk.html#the-screens","s":"Examples","x":"Splash, onboarding, log in, create account, verification, shop setup, dashboard, repairs list, new repair, repair details, record payment and tracking."},{"t":"FundiDesk","h":"Run it","u":"examples/fundidesk.html#run-it","s":"Examples","x":"Start with examples/fundidesk/SCREENS.md in the repository: it is the method, written for whoever builds the next screen."},{"t":"FundiDesk","h":"What has been verified","u":"examples/fundidesk.html#what-has-been-verified","s":"Examples","x":"It launched on a Galaxy A27 (first frame drawn, then idle, from the log). Typing into its fields was verified there with the system keyboard."},{"t":"CallReady","h":"","u":"examples/callready.html","s":"Examples","x":"CallReadyPractice for call-centre work: the app plays an angry customer and scores how the call was handled. Five screens are reproduced from a heavily illustrated design, and more are the app's own, added so that every control leads somewhere.WelcomeHomeA live callThe call report"},{"t":"CallReady","h":"What it shows","u":"examples/callready.html#what-it-shows","s":"Examples","x":"Turned elements: tiles and text drawn through a transform, with text that stays sharp.Multi-colour vector pictures and illustrations loaded from SVG files.Text effects: hard shadows, outlined digits, italic, numbers that line up.A launcher icon of its own, drawn from the app's art by cargo run -p callready --example icon.Recording and playback through effects, with a server that plays the caller."},{"t":"CallReady","h":"Run it","u":"examples/callready.html#run-it","s":"Examples","x":"Without its server it is a scripted app. With it, the call is real: The server needs a PostgreSQL database and a DeepSeek key, and optionally an ElevenLabs key for voice; without the voice key calls run in text. Node 22 or newer.examples/callready/SCREENS.md lists every screen and control."},{"t":"Atlas","h":"","u":"examples/atlas.html","s":"Examples","x":"AtlasAn AI assistant reproduced from a design of ten pages and made to work end to end.OnboardingHomeA chatLive voice"},{"t":"Atlas","h":"What it shows","u":"examples/atlas.html#what-it-shows","s":"Examples","x":"Soft light: radial gradients, a blurred background, inner shadows.A bottom sheet over a screen, and a row of chips that scrolls sideways.A chat that is real: with its server, answers come from a language model, chats are kept and listed, and the chosen model and plan are saved.An app that works without its server: when the server does not answer, it is the scripted app of the design.A server address chosen at build t"},{"t":"Atlas","h":"The screens","u":"examples/atlas.html#the-screens","s":"Examples","x":"Onboarding, sign in, home, chat, the model picker, live voice, explore, plans, and simplified versions of home and chat."},{"t":"Atlas","h":"Run it","u":"examples/atlas.html#run-it","s":"Examples","x":"With its server: The server needs a PostgreSQL database and a DeepSeek key; an ElevenLabs key is optional and gives live voice. Node 22 or newer.Atlas declares no opt-in service, so its APK carries no LiveKit code: about 10 MB. examples/atlas/SCREENS.md is the full description."},{"t":"Barua Talk","h":"","u":"examples/barua-talk.html","s":"Examples","x":"Barua TalkRooms, chat and calls, reproduced from a design of five pages and made to work with a server. It is the app that uses live rooms: sound, both cameras and a shared screen.HomeA roomAn incoming callA video call"},{"t":"Barua Talk","h":"What it shows","u":"examples/barua-talk.html#what-it-shows","s":"Examples","x":"Photographs beside the drawing, a blurred backdrop behind an incoming call, and an arched bar.A live room: an answered call, a joined room and a dialled phone call join with the ticket the server gives. Mute, the loudspeaker, the camera and screen sharing act on the real connection.Honest status: the call screen says in words how the sound stands, including why there is none.Chat: in rooms, and between two people.All"},{"t":"Barua Talk","h":"Fidelity","u":"examples/barua-talk.html#fidelity","s":"Examples","x":"Each designed screen is held to its picture:ScreenMeasured differenceLimitHome0.0847%0.10%Room0.6900%0.70%Incoming call0.0154%0.05%Video call0.2860%0.30%Phone call0.0499%0.05%"},{"t":"Barua Talk","h":"Run it","u":"examples/barua-talk.html#run-it","s":"Examples","x":"With its server: The server needs a PostgreSQL database and a Barua Talk API key. Phone calls cost money upstream and stay off unless the server is told otherwise. Node 22 or newer. server/API.md is the contract between the app and the server."},{"t":"Barua Talk","h":"What has been verified","u":"examples/barua-talk.html#what-has-been-verified","s":"Examples","x":"The app passes its tests on the development machine, and its server's tests pass against the real Barua Talk API for rooms, tickets, chat and in-app calls. The app has been installed on a phone. No call has been confirmed working on a phone yet: sound and pictures through a real room are unverified, and the live preview on a Mac answers live rooms with Unsupported.examples/barua-talk/SCREENS.md has the step-by-step t"},{"t":"services-check","h":"","u":"examples/services-check.html","s":"Examples","x":"services-checkA smoke test for a phone: one screen with one button per platform service. It is also the smallest app that uses effects, with scripted tests for each.The one screen"},{"t":"services-check","h":"What it shows","u":"examples/services-check.html#what-it-shows","s":"Examples","x":"An HTTP request, a recording, playing it back, a stored value, and joining a live room, each started by a button and each reporting its result as a line of text.How every effect is tested: the test reads the request and answers in the host's place.The live service alone: it is the voice-only build, about 30 MB as an APK."},{"t":"services-check","h":"Run it","u":"examples/services-check.html#run-it","s":"Examples","x":"On the phone, press each button and read the line under it; adb logcat -s Morningstar shows what the host did. Rendered on the development machine, as in the picture, every service answers that it is not available: nothing there can carry one out."},{"t":"hello-gpu","h":"","u":"examples/hello-gpu.html","s":"Examples","x":"hello-gpuThe low-level example. It does not use the UI layer: it implements the runtime's App trait directly, paints its own display list and does its own hit-testing. Read it to see what is underneath, not as a way to write an app.IdleAfter seven taps"},{"t":"hello-gpu","h":"What it shows","u":"examples/hello-gpu.html#what-it-shows","s":"Examples","x":"The engine's contract with an app: lifecycle in, a display list out.Drawing operations by hand: rectangles, text, the safe area.Text in three scripts on one screen, including Arabic.It is the default app of tools/build-android.sh, and was the first thing drawn on a phone."},{"t":"hello-gpu","h":"Run it","u":"examples/hello-gpu.html#run-it","s":"Examples","x":""}];
